window.QUESTION_BANK = {"chemphys":[{"c":"5A","t":"Acids and bases","q":"What is the pH of 0.01 M HCl?","a":"2","w":["1","12","0.01"],"e":"HCl dissociates completely, so [H⁺] = 10⁻² M and pH = −log(10⁻²) = 2.","id":"c3e5aa7c5f","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Acids and bases","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Acids and bases","q":"At 25 °C, a solution has a pOH of 4. What is its pH?","a":"10","w":["4","7","14"],"e":"pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C, because K_w = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴.","id":"2d10b4907f","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Acids and bases","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Acids and bases","q":"What is the conjugate base of H₂PO₄⁻?","a":"HPO₄²⁻","w":["H₃PO₄","PO₄³⁻","H₂PO₄²⁻"],"e":"A conjugate base is the species left after an acid donates one proton: H₂PO₄⁻ → H⁺ + HPO₄²⁻.","id":"6a9661215b","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Acids and bases","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Acids and bases","q":"Which of these species is amphoteric?","a":"HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate)","w":["Cl⁻ (chloride)","Na⁺ (sodium)","CO₃²⁻ (carbonate)"],"e":"Bicarbonate can accept a proton (forming H₂CO₃) or donate one (forming CO₃²⁻). Carbonate can only accept.","id":"58a0b49688","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Acids and bases","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Acids and bases","q":"Which acid has the weakest conjugate base?","a":"HI","w":["HF","CH₃COOH","H₂O"],"e":"The stronger the acid, the weaker its conjugate base. HI is a strong acid, so I⁻ is a very weak base.","id":"8572025c72","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Acids and bases","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Buffers","q":"In a buffer where [A⁻] = [HA], the pH equals:","a":"The pKa of the acid","w":["7","The pKb of the base","The pKa minus 1"],"e":"Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]) = pKa + log 1 = pKa.","id":"ab31203957","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Buffers","q":"A buffer is made with an acid of pKa 4.7. In which pH range does it buffer best?","a":"About 3.7 to 5.7","w":["About 6.7 to 8.7","Exactly 7.0","About 1.0 to 2.0"],"e":"Buffers work best within about ±1 pH unit of the pKa, where both HA and A⁻ are present in useful amounts.","id":"808340366b","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Buffers","q":"A buffer has a pKa of 6 and a [A⁻]/[HA] ratio of 10. What is its pH?","a":"7","w":["5","6","16"],"e":"pH = pKa + log(10) = 6 + 1 = 7.","id":"df7c76627e","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Titrations","q":"When a weak acid is titrated with a strong base, the pH at the equivalence point is:","a":"Above 7","w":["Exactly 7","Below 7","Equal to the pKa"],"e":"At equivalence, the solution contains the conjugate base of the weak acid, which makes the solution basic. pH = pKa happens at the half-equivalence point.","id":"fc1b7353c7","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Titrations","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Titrations","q":"At the half-equivalence point of a weak acid titration with a strong base:","a":"pH = pKa","w":["pH = 7","pH = 14 − pKa","All the acid has been neutralized"],"e":"Half the HA has been converted to A⁻, so [HA] = [A⁻] and Henderson–Hasselbalch gives pH = pKa.","id":"602552714d","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Titrations","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Solubility","q":"Adding NaCl to a saturated AgCl solution causes:","a":"More AgCl to precipitate","w":["More AgCl to dissolve","No change in AgCl solubility","Ksp of AgCl to increase"],"e":"The common-ion effect: extra Cl⁻ pushes AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ to the left. Ksp itself only changes with temperature.","id":"8dfe2957b9","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Solubility","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Solubility","q":"Which compound is insoluble in water?","a":"AgCl","w":["KNO₃","NaCl","NH₄Cl"],"e":"Salts of Group 1 cations, ammonium and nitrate are soluble. Chlorides are soluble except with Ag⁺, Pb²⁺ and Hg₂²⁺.","id":"5220c293e4","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Solubility","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Solubility","q":"A salt MX₂ has Ksp = 4 × 10⁻¹². What is its molar solubility?","a":"1 × 10⁻⁴ M","w":["2 × 10⁻⁶ M","4 × 10⁻¹² M","1 × 10⁻⁶ M"],"e":"Ksp = [M][X]² = s(2s)² = 4s³. So s³ = 10⁻¹² and s = 10⁻⁴ M.","id":"a6f56c7ca5","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Solubility","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Colligative properties","q":"Equal molal amounts of NaCl and glucose are dissolved in separate samples of water. Compared with the glucose solution, the NaCl solution's freezing point is lowered:","a":"About twice as much","w":["About half as much","The same amount","Not at all, because NaCl is ionic"],"e":"ΔT_f = iK_f m. NaCl dissociates into 2 ions (i ≈ 2), while glucose stays as one particle (i = 1).","id":"c9e74368c5","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Colligative properties","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Colligative properties","q":"Red blood cells are placed in a solution that is hypertonic to their cytoplasm. Water will:","a":"Move out of the cells, so they shrink (crenate)","w":["Move into the cells, so they swell and lyse","Not move, because the membrane blocks water","Move in and out equally"],"e":"Water moves toward the higher solute concentration, which here is outside the cells.","id":"ce7d16ee32","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Colligative properties","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5A","t":"Colligative properties","q":"Which property is NOT a colligative property?","a":"Density","w":["Osmotic pressure","Boiling-point elevation","Vapor-pressure lowering"],"e":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles, not their identity. Density depends on what the solute is.","id":"3c4b7ac683","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Colligative properties","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Intermolecular forces","q":"What is the strongest intermolecular force between ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) molecules?","a":"Hydrogen bonding","w":["London dispersion forces","Ion–dipole interactions","Covalent bonding"],"e":"Ethanol has an O–H group, so molecules hydrogen-bond to each other. Covalent bonds are within molecules, not between them.","id":"812bc7fc91","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Intermolecular forces","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Intermolecular forces","q":"Which compound has the highest boiling point?","a":"1-butanol","w":["Butane","Diethyl ether","Butanal"],"e":"These have similar molar masses. Only 1-butanol can hydrogen-bond with itself, which takes the most energy to overcome.","id":"e40a1e80e2","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Intermolecular forces","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Intermolecular forces","q":"Among straight-chain alkanes, boiling point increases with chain length mainly because of stronger:","a":"London dispersion forces","w":["Hydrogen bonds","Dipole–dipole forces","Ionic interactions"],"e":"Larger molecules have more electrons and surface area, so they are more polarizable and attract each other more.","id":"a7a8302766","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Intermolecular forces","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Bonding and shape","q":"What is the hybridization of the carbon atom in CO₂?","a":"sp","w":["sp²","sp³","sp³d"],"e":"The carbon has two electron domains (two double bonds), so it's sp hybridized and linear.","id":"fa46bc4be2","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Bonding and shape","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Bonding and shape","q":"What is the molecular geometry of ammonia (NH₃)?","a":"Trigonal pyramidal","w":["Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Bent"],"e":"Four electron domains (three bonds, one lone pair) make it tetrahedral electronically, but the molecular shape is trigonal pyramidal, with bond angles of about 107°.","id":"4ce3dfbb06","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Bonding and shape","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Bonding and shape","q":"CO₂ has polar C=O bonds but is a nonpolar molecule because:","a":"Its linear shape makes the bond dipoles cancel","w":["Carbon and oxygen have equal electronegativity","Its double bonds share electrons equally","It has lone pairs on the carbon"],"e":"Molecular polarity depends on geometry. The two equal bond dipoles point in opposite directions.","id":"ddd5a9e779","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Bonding and shape","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Bonding and shape","q":"How many sigma (σ) and pi (π) bonds are in a carbon–carbon triple bond?","a":"1 σ and 2 π","w":["3 σ and 0 π","2 σ and 1 π","0 σ and 3 π"],"e":"The first bond between two atoms is always σ. Any additional bonds are π bonds.","id":"3eaa3a6b21","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Bonding and shape","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Stereochemistry","q":"A molecule has 3 chiral centers and no internal symmetry. What is the maximum number of stereoisomers?","a":"8","w":["6","3","9"],"e":"The maximum is 2ⁿ, where n is the number of chiral centers: 2³ = 8.","id":"d73c98b9b6","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Stereochemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Stereochemistry","q":"A meso compound is:","a":"A molecule with chiral centers that is achiral because of an internal mirror plane","w":["A 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers, which is optically inactive overall","A molecule with no chiral centers that still rotates polarized light","A diastereomer that has only one chiral center and no symmetry plane"],"e":"Meso compounds such as meso-tartaric acid have stereocenters but are superimposable on their mirror images, so they're optically inactive.","id":"2a581b3518","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Stereochemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Stereochemistry","q":"A pair of enantiomers differ in:","a":"The direction they rotate plane-polarized light","w":["Boiling point at the same pressure","Solubility in water at room temperature","Melting point when each is pure"],"e":"Enantiomers have identical physical properties in an achiral environment except for the direction of optical rotation. They can also differ in how they interact with chiral molecules like enzymes.","id":"5921bc1490","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Stereochemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Stereochemistry","q":"Two stereoisomers that are NOT mirror images of each other are called:","a":"Diastereomers","w":["Enantiomers","Constitutional isomers","Conformers"],"e":"Diastereomers, including cis/trans isomers, differ at some but not all stereocenters and have different physical properties.","id":"c37530ea69","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Stereochemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5B","t":"Stereochemistry","q":"At a chiral center, priorities 1 → 2 → 3 trace clockwise, but the lowest-priority group points toward you. The configuration is:","a":"(S), because the direction must be reversed","w":["(R), because the trace is clockwise","Impossible to tell without a model","Achiral, because the group points forward"],"e":"Cahn–Ingold–Prelog rules assume the lowest-priority group points away. If it points toward you, reverse the direction you traced.","id":"f8b08eb4e4","L":"5B · Molecules and intermolecular forces","T":"Stereochemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Chromatography","q":"On a silica gel TLC plate run with a nonpolar solvent, which compound will have the highest Rf?","a":"The least polar compound","w":["The most polar compound","The compound with the most H-bond donors","The heaviest compound"],"e":"Silica is polar and holds onto polar compounds. Nonpolar compounds spend more time in the mobile phase and travel farther.","id":"c977556647","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Chromatography","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Chromatography","q":"In size-exclusion chromatography, which molecules elute first?","a":"The largest molecules","w":["The smallest molecules","The most negatively charged molecules","The most hydrophobic molecules"],"e":"Small molecules enter the beads' pores and take a longer path. Large molecules are excluded and pass through quickly.","id":"23ab2d408b","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Chromatography","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Chromatography","q":"A cation-exchange column will bind proteins that are:","a":"Positively charged at the buffer's pH","w":["Negatively charged at the buffer's pH","Uncharged at the buffer's pH","Larger than the column's pore size"],"e":"A cation exchanger carries negative groups that hold cations. A protein is positively charged when the pH is below its pI.","id":"c553779f9c","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Chromatography","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Electrophoresis","q":"In SDS-PAGE, proteins are separated mainly by:","a":"Mass, with smaller proteins moving faster","w":["Charge, with more negative proteins moving slower","Isoelectric point","Mass, with larger proteins moving faster"],"e":"SDS denatures proteins and coats them with a uniform negative charge per unit length, so the gel sieves them by size.","id":"29ab776c50","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Electrophoresis","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Electrophoresis","q":"In isoelectric focusing, a protein stops migrating when:","a":"It reaches the region where the pH equals its pI","w":["It reaches the anode at the end of the gel","Its mass matches the pore size of the gel","The surrounding pH becomes exactly neutral"],"e":"At its pI, a protein has no net charge and stops moving in the electric field.","id":"e68a403076","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Electrophoresis","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","q":"A mixture of benzoic acid and naphthalene is dissolved in ether. Which aqueous wash pulls the benzoic acid into the water layer?","a":"Aqueous sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃)","w":["Aqueous hydrochloric acid","Pure water","Brine (saturated NaCl)"],"e":"Base deprotonates the carboxylic acid into a water-soluble carboxylate salt. Naphthalene stays in the ether.","id":"caf98af3b6","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","q":"Fractional distillation, rather than simple distillation, is needed when two liquids have:","a":"Boiling points that are close together","w":["Boiling points more than 100 °C apart","Very different densities","Different solubilities in water"],"e":"Simple distillation works for widely separated boiling points. A fractionating column gives many vaporization–condensation cycles to separate close ones.","id":"0df57f3d6a","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","q":"Recrystallization purifies a solid because the desired compound should be:","a":"Very soluble in the hot solvent and poorly soluble in the cold solvent","w":["Insoluble in the solvent at every temperature, so impurities wash away","Equally soluble hot or cold, so impurities settle out on standing","Poorly soluble in the hot solvent and very soluble in the cold solvent"],"e":"Dissolve the solid hot, then cool it. The product crystallizes while the impurities stay in solution.","id":"fa1e7826a7","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Carbonyl chemistry","q":"Why are aldehydes generally more reactive toward nucleophiles than ketones?","a":"Less steric hindrance and less electron donation from alkyl groups at the carbonyl","w":["Aldehydes have a stronger, shorter C=O bond that is more polarized","Ketones carry a hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon that blocks attack","Aldehydes are stabilized by resonance with the adjacent hydrogen atom"],"e":"Ketones' two alkyl groups block nucleophiles and donate electron density, making the carbonyl carbon less electrophilic.","id":"211a8e50fc","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Carbonyl chemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Carbonyl chemistry","q":"Which carboxylic acid derivative is most reactive toward nucleophilic acyl substitution?","a":"Acyl chloride","w":["Amide","Ester","Carboxylate anion"],"e":"Reactivity order: acyl chloride > anhydride > ester ≈ acid > amide > carboxylate. Cl⁻ is the best leaving group.","id":"97cfeb7f92","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Carbonyl chemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Carbonyl chemistry","q":"An aldehyde reacts with a primary amine under mildly acidic conditions. The main product is:","a":"An imine","w":["An amide","An enamine","A hemiacetal"],"e":"Primary amines add to carbonyls and lose water to form C=N imines (Schiff bases). Secondary amines give enamines.","id":"13411f668f","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Carbonyl chemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Carbonyl chemistry","q":"In the aldol reaction, the nucleophile is:","a":"An enolate formed by removing an alpha hydrogen","w":["A hydroxide ion that attacks the carbonyl oxygen","The carbonyl oxygen of a second molecule","A carbocation formed from the aldehyde"],"e":"The enolate's alpha carbon attacks the carbonyl carbon of another molecule, forming a β-hydroxy carbonyl compound.","id":"7da82c3c22","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Carbonyl chemistry","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Substitution reactions","q":"Which conditions favor an SN2 reaction?","a":"A primary substrate, a strong nucleophile and a polar aprotic solvent","w":["A tertiary substrate, a weak nucleophile and a polar protic solvent","A tertiary substrate, a strong nucleophile and heat","Any substrate in a nonpolar solvent"],"e":"SN2 is a one-step backside attack, so it needs an unhindered carbon and a strong nucleophile that isn't tied up by solvent hydrogen bonding.","id":"ececd5d92d","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Substitution reactions","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Substitution reactions","q":"An SN2 reaction at a chiral carbon gives a product with:","a":"Inverted stereochemistry","w":["Retained stereochemistry","A racemic mixture","No chiral center"],"e":"Backside attack flips the carbon like an umbrella in the wind (Walden inversion).","id":"4ef6731d7f","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Substitution reactions","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Substitution reactions","q":"The rate-determining step of an SN1 reaction is:","a":"Loss of the leaving group to form a carbocation","w":["Attack of the nucleophile","Deprotonation of the product","Formation of a five-coordinate transition state"],"e":"SN1 rate depends only on [substrate]. Tertiary carbons react fastest because they form the most stable carbocations.","id":"7c42bd8b53","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Substitution reactions","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Oxidation and reduction","q":"A primary alcohol is treated with PCC. The product is:","a":"An aldehyde","w":["A carboxylic acid","A ketone","An alkene"],"e":"PCC is a mild oxidant that stops at the aldehyde. Strong oxidants like chromic acid or KMnO₄ continue on to the carboxylic acid.","id":"c6ad89a87c","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Oxidation and reduction","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Oxidation and reduction","q":"Which reagent reduces a ketone to a secondary alcohol?","a":"NaBH₄","w":["KMnO₄","PCC","CrO₃ in acid"],"e":"Sodium borohydride delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon. The other three are oxidizing agents.","id":"dfb798c1dd","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Oxidation and reduction","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Acidity","q":"Phenol (pKa ≈ 10) is much more acidic than ethanol (pKa ≈ 16) because:","a":"Resonance spreads the phenoxide ion's negative charge into the ring","w":["Phenol forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water than ethanol does","The O–H bond in ethanol is weaker and breaks more easily in water","The ethoxide ion is aromatic, which destabilizes its negative charge"],"e":"A more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid. Ethoxide's charge stays on one oxygen.","id":"3eb7d30949","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Acidity","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Lipids and carbohydrates","q":"Saponification of a triglyceride with NaOH produces:","a":"Glycerol and fatty acid salts (soap)","w":["Glycerol and free fatty acids","Three alcohols and a carboxylic acid","Cholesterol and phosphate"],"e":"Base hydrolyzes the three ester bonds, giving glycerol and three carboxylate salts.","id":"c770fcd717","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Lipids and carbohydrates","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Lipids and carbohydrates","q":"α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose are:","a":"Anomers that differ in configuration at C-1","w":["Enantiomers with opposite configurations","Epimers that differ in configuration at C-4","Constitutional isomers with different bonds"],"e":"Anomers differ at the anomeric carbon formed during ring closure. They interconvert in solution through mutarotation.","id":"26773345e2","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Lipids and carbohydrates","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Lipids and carbohydrates","q":"Why is sucrose a non-reducing sugar?","a":"Both anomeric carbons are tied up in the glycosidic bond","w":["It is a pentose sugar that can't form an open chain","It contains no free hydroxyl groups anywhere in it","It is an L-sugar, which can't be oxidized by copper ions"],"e":"Reducing sugars need a free anomeric carbon that can open into an aldehyde. Sucrose links C-1 of glucose to C-2 of fructose.","id":"2731b1ff55","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Lipids and carbohydrates","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Spectroscopy","q":"An IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp peak near 1700 cm⁻¹ and no broad peak at 3200–3600 cm⁻¹. The compound is most likely:","a":"A ketone","w":["An alcohol","A carboxylic acid","An alkane"],"e":"1700 cm⁻¹ is a C=O stretch. A carboxylic acid or alcohol would also show a broad O–H stretch.","id":"24df30ad1f","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Spectroscopy","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5D","t":"Spectroscopy","q":"In ¹H NMR, a singlet near 9.5–10 ppm most likely belongs to:","a":"An aldehyde hydrogen","w":["A methyl group on an alkane","An aromatic hydrogen","An alcohol O–H"],"e":"The aldehyde proton sits on a carbonyl carbon and is strongly deshielded. Aromatic protons appear around 6.5–8 ppm.","id":"d3218c0b17","L":"5D · Biological molecules and reactivity","T":"Spectroscopy","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Thermodynamics","q":"A reaction has ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0. It will be spontaneous:","a":"Only at high temperatures","w":["Only at low temperatures","At all temperatures","At no temperature"],"e":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. The −TΔS term must outweigh the positive ΔH, which happens when T is large.","id":"0417138f60","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Thermodynamics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Thermodynamics","q":"A reaction has an equilibrium constant K > 1. Its standard free energy change ΔG° is:","a":"Negative","w":["Positive","Zero","Equal to K"],"e":"ΔG° = −RT ln K. ln K > 0 when K > 1, so ΔG° < 0.","id":"980e1ca527","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Thermodynamics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Thermodynamics","q":"How much heat raises 100 g of water by 10 °C? (c = 4.18 J/g·°C)","a":"4180 J","w":["418 J","41.8 J","41,800 J"],"e":"q = mcΔT = 100 × 4.18 × 10 = 4180 J.","id":"80e63e61b2","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Thermodynamics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Thermodynamics","q":"For an ideal gas expanding isothermally, which is true?","a":"ΔU = 0, so the heat absorbed equals the work done by the gas","w":["Q = 0, so the change in internal energy equals the work","Work is zero, because the volume does not change at all","ΔU equals the heat absorbed, because no work is done"],"e":"An ideal gas's internal energy depends only on temperature. From ΔU = Q − W, Q = W when ΔU = 0.","id":"e7d0f93e5d","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Thermodynamics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Thermodynamics","q":"Hess's law allows you to calculate a reaction's ΔH because enthalpy is:","a":"A state function, so it depends only on the start and end states","w":["Always negative for spontaneous reactions at any temperature","Equal to the activation energy of the slowest reaction step","Path-dependent, like heat and work, so each step must be summed"],"e":"Adding up the ΔH values of steps that lead from the same reactants to the same products gives the overall ΔH.","id":"f45d922188","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Thermodynamics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Phase changes","q":"On water's phase diagram, the solid–liquid boundary has a negative slope. This means:","a":"Increasing pressure can melt ice at a constant temperature","w":["Increasing pressure freezes liquid water at constant temperature","Ice is denser than liquid water at the melting point","Water cannot exist as a solid at any pressure above 1 atm"],"e":"Ice is less dense than liquid water. Squeezing favors the denser (liquid) phase.","id":"3d9f78d932","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Phase changes","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Phase changes","q":"While a pure substance is boiling at constant pressure, adding heat:","a":"Keeps the temperature constant until all the liquid has vaporized","w":["Raises the temperature steadily as the liquid boils off","Lowers the temperature because evaporation cools the liquid","Raises the boiling point until the vapor pressure doubles"],"e":"The energy goes into breaking intermolecular attractions (heat of vaporization), not into kinetic energy.","id":"17aaf3a742","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Phase changes","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","q":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction by:","a":"Lowering the activation energy without changing ΔG or K","w":["Making ΔG more negative so the reaction is more favorable","Shifting the equilibrium position toward the products","Raising the energy of the reactants above the barrier"],"e":"Catalysts lower the barrier equally for forward and reverse reactions, so equilibrium is reached faster but its position doesn't change.","id":"7062fa9d7c","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","q":"Doubling [A] quadruples the reaction rate. The reaction is:","a":"Second order in A","w":["First order in A","Zero order in A","Fourth order in A"],"e":"Rate ∝ [A]ⁿ, and 2ⁿ = 4 gives n = 2.","id":"aec46d43ee","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","q":"Which type of reaction has a half-life that does not depend on starting concentration?","a":"First order","w":["Zero order","Second order","Third order"],"e":"For a first-order reaction, t½ = 0.693/k, a constant. Radioactive decay is a classic example.","id":"3f2d39aa27","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","q":"What are the units of the rate constant k for a first-order reaction?","a":"s⁻¹","w":["M·s⁻¹","M⁻¹·s⁻¹","M⁻²·s⁻¹"],"e":"Rate (M/s) = k[A] (M), so k has units of 1/s.","id":"f6262d86fc","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Equilibrium","q":"For an exothermic reaction at equilibrium, raising the temperature will:","a":"Shift equilibrium toward reactants and decrease K","w":["Shift equilibrium toward products and increase K","Leave K unchanged","Shift equilibrium toward products without changing K"],"e":"Treat heat as a product of an exothermic reaction. Adding it pushes the reaction left, and temperature is the only factor that changes K.","id":"4aeccf44f3","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Equilibrium","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Equilibrium","q":"If the reaction quotient Q is less than K, the reaction will:","a":"Proceed forward, making more products","w":["Proceed in reverse, making more reactants","Stay where it is","Stop completely"],"e":"Q < K means there are too few products relative to equilibrium, so the reaction moves forward until Q = K.","id":"0208d3d95a","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Equilibrium","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"5E","t":"Equilibrium","q":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), decreasing the container's volume will:","a":"Shift equilibrium toward NH₃","w":["Shift equilibrium toward N₂ and H₂","Have no effect, because K is constant","Increase K"],"e":"Higher pressure favors the side with fewer gas moles: 2 moles of NH₃ versus 4 moles of reactants.","id":"9ecc3ce8a6","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Equilibrium","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"Compared with the upstream segment, the blood pressure at the 1.0 mm narrowing is most likely:","a":"Lower, because the blood moves faster there","w":["Higher, because the vessel is narrower","The same, because the fluid is incompressible","Higher, because the flow speed is higher"],"e":"For horizontal flow, Bernoulli's equation says P + ½ρv² stays constant, so the faster flow at the narrowing has lower pressure.","c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","s":null,"p":"p90212bcf","pi":0,"id":"a0404192b6","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"What is the volume flow rate in the upstream segment?","a":"About 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m³/s","w":["About 8.0 × 10⁻⁷ m³/s","About 1.3 × 10⁻³ m³/s","About 2.5 × 10⁻³ m³/s"],"e":"Q = Av = π(2.0 × 10⁻³ m)²(0.20 m/s) = π × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.20 ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m³/s. Don't forget to convert mm to m.","c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","s":null,"p":"p90212bcf","pi":1,"id":"bcad2cd63f","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"At which radius in Table 1 is the flow speed 16 times its upstream value?","a":"0.5 mm","w":["1.0 mm","1.4 mm","0.25 mm"],"e":"3.20 / 0.20 = 16. By continuity, speed ∝ 1/r², and quartering the radius (2.0 → 0.5 mm) raises speed 16-fold.","c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","s":null,"p":"p90212bcf","pi":2,"id":"a8cbdd1dfc","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"If a downstream arteriole doubles its radius with no other change, its resistance to flow becomes:","a":"1/16 of its original value","w":["1/2 of its original value","1/4 of its original value","16 times its original value"],"e":"From Poiseuille's law, resistance = 8ηL / (πr⁴), so it scales with 1/r⁴. Doubling r divides resistance by 2⁴ = 16.","c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","s":null,"p":"p90212bcf","pi":3,"id":"ba0947326e","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"A patient hyperventilates, lowering arterial P_CO₂ to 20 mmHg while [HCO₃⁻] stays at 24 mM. What is the blood pH? (log 2 ≈ 0.3)","a":"About 7.7","w":["About 7.1","About 7.4","About 8.4"],"e":"Dissolved CO₂ = 0.03 × 20 = 0.6 mM. pH = 6.1 + log(24/0.6) = 6.1 + log 40 = 6.1 + 1.6 = 7.7. This is respiratory alkalosis.","c":"5A","t":"Buffers","s":null,"p":"p4ce3a823","pi":0,"id":"eb9aec7eb4","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"What effect does carbonic anhydrase have on the buffer system?","a":"It helps the system reach equilibrium faster without changing the equilibrium ratio","w":["It raises the apparent pKa of the buffer, so blood pH rises at equilibrium","It shifts the equilibrium toward bicarbonate, so more H⁺ is released","It removes dissolved CO₂ from the blood, so the [HCO₃⁻]/[CO₂] ratio rises"],"e":"Enzymes are catalysts: they lower activation energy for both directions equally, so K and the equilibrium position are unchanged.","c":"5A","t":"Buffers","s":null,"p":"p4ce3a823","pi":1,"id":"ee49413e99","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"A patient with chronic lung disease retains CO₂ for weeks. How would the kidneys most likely compensate?","a":"By retaining more HCO₃⁻, moving the [HCO₃⁻]/[CO₂] ratio back toward 20","w":["By excreting more HCO₃⁻, lowering the ratio and the pH further","By lowering P_CO₂ directly through faster filtration in the kidney","By raising the buffer's pKa so the same ratio gives a higher pH"],"e":"High P_CO₂ lowers the ratio and the pH (respiratory acidosis). Raising [HCO₃⁻] restores the ratio, bringing pH back toward 7.4.","c":"5A","t":"Buffers","s":null,"p":"p4ce3a823","pi":2,"id":"1d31c2810a","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"Buffers usually work best within 1 pH unit of their pKa, yet this buffer works well at 7.4 even though its pKa is 6.1. What best explains this?","a":"It is an open system, because the lungs can quickly remove or retain CO₂","w":["Blood pH actually sits much closer to 6.1 than it does to 7.4","Carbonic anhydrase raises the buffer's pKa inside red blood cells","Bicarbonate is a strong base that fully neutralizes any added acid"],"e":"Because breathing continuously sets the CO₂ concentration, added acid can be blown off as CO₂ instead of piling up as H₂CO₃.","c":"5A","t":"Buffers","s":null,"p":"p4ce3a823","pi":3,"id":"da649af11b","L":"5A · Water and solutions","T":"Buffers","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"Based on Table 1, what kind of lens is this, and what is its focal length?","a":"Converging, with f = +10 cm","w":["Converging, with f = +20 cm","Diverging, with f = −10 cm","Diverging, with f = −20 cm"],"e":"Any row works: 1/f = 1/20 + 1/20 = 1/10, so f = 10 cm. A positive f and real images mean a converging lens.","c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","s":null,"p":"p8b8d34fc","pi":0,"id":"44f7d430ee","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"When the object is 15 cm from the lens, the image is:","a":"4.0 cm tall and inverted","w":["1.0 cm tall and inverted","4.0 cm tall and upright","2.0 cm tall and upright"],"e":"m = −dᵢ/dₒ = −30/15 = −2. The image is twice the 2.0 cm height (4.0 cm), and the negative sign means it is inverted.","c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","s":null,"p":"p8b8d34fc","pi":1,"id":"00e90892eb","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"When the object is 5 cm from the lens, the image is:","a":"Virtual, upright and twice the object's size","w":["Real, inverted and twice the object's size","Virtual, inverted and half the object's size","Real, upright and the same size"],"e":"dᵢ = −10 cm, so m = −(−10)/5 = +2. This is how a magnifying glass works: an object inside the focal length gives an enlarged virtual image.","c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","s":null,"p":"p8b8d34fc","pi":2,"id":"bfb2f9cbba","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"If the student places the object exactly 10 cm from the lens, what will she observe?","a":"No image forms, because the refracted rays leave parallel","w":["A real, inverted image forms 10 cm behind the lens","A virtual, upright image forms 10 cm in front of the lens","A real image forms that is the same size as the object"],"e":"With dₒ = f, 1/dᵢ = 1/f − 1/dₒ = 0, so the image is at infinity.","c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","s":null,"p":"p8b8d34fc","pi":3,"id":"9b5fe787a7","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"Which compound is in Layer A, and in what form?","a":"Aniline, as the water-soluble anilinium ion","w":["Benzoic acid, as the water-soluble benzoate ion","Naphthalene, unchanged and dissolved in water","Aniline, as the neutral free base in the water"],"e":"HCl protonates the basic amine, giving an ionic anilinium salt that dissolves in water. The acid and naphthalene are not protonated.","c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","s":null,"p":"paf9f5059","pi":0,"id":"ca8aa3a465","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"During Step 1, where is the ether layer in the separatory funnel?","a":"On top, because ether is less dense than water","w":["On the bottom, because ether is less dense than water","On the bottom, because it contains more solute","Mixed into the water, because both are polar"],"e":"Ether (0.71 g/mL) floats on the aqueous layer (about 1.0 g/mL). Halogenated solvents like dichloromethane sink.","c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","s":null,"p":"paf9f5059","pi":1,"id":"03cf3881e7","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"If the student used aqueous NaHCO₃ instead of NaOH in Step 2, would benzoic acid still move into the aqueous layer?","a":"Yes, because benzoic acid (pKa 4.2) is a stronger acid than carbonic acid (pKa 6.4)","w":["No, because bicarbonate is too weak a base to deprotonate any carboxylic acid","No, because sodium bicarbonate is not soluble enough in water to work","Yes, but only if the mixture is heated so that the bicarbonate decomposes"],"e":"An acid–base reaction favors the weaker acid. Bicarbonate can deprotonate any acid stronger than carbonic acid, which includes most carboxylic acids but not phenols.","c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","s":null,"p":"paf9f5059","pi":2,"id":"370b07e5ec","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"How should the student recover solid benzoic acid from Layer B?","a":"Add HCl until the solution is acidic, then filter off the precipitate","w":["Add more NaOH until the solution is basic, then evaporate the water","Add anhydrous MgSO₄ to the solution, then filter off the solid","Boil the solution until the sodium benzoate crystallizes as the acid"],"e":"Acidifying reprotonates benzoate back to neutral benzoic acid, which is poorly soluble in cold water and precipitates.","c":"5C","t":"Extraction and distillation","s":null,"p":"paf9f5059","pi":3,"id":"fb51201c38","L":"5C · Separation and purification","T":"Extraction and distillation","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"A dose of technetium-99m is prepared at 6 a.m. What fraction of its original activity remains at 6 a.m. the next day?","a":"1/16","w":["1/4","1/8","1/24"],"e":"24 hours is 4 half-lives of 6 hours: (1/2)⁴ = 1/16.","c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","s":null,"p":"p7e77c390","pi":0,"id":"9f6f35564e","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"Fluorine-18 decays to which nucleus?","a":"Oxygen-18","w":["Neon-18","Fluorine-17","Nitrogen-14"],"e":"Positron emission turns a proton into a neutron: Z drops from 9 to 8 (oxygen), and the mass number stays 18.","c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","s":null,"p":"p7e77c390","pi":1,"id":"d8a9dc0a85","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"Why do the two annihilation photons travel in nearly opposite directions?","a":"The total momentum was nearly zero before annihilation, and it is conserved","w":["The photons carry opposite charges, so they repel each other","Total kinetic energy must be conserved, so they split it evenly","The magnetic field of the detector ring bends their paths apart"],"e":"The positron and electron are nearly at rest when they annihilate. Two photons with equal and opposite momenta keep the total near zero. Photons carry no charge.","c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","s":null,"p":"p7e77c390","pi":2,"id":"7219399a77","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"The energy of each annihilation photon, 511 keV, is equal to:","a":"The rest-mass energy of one electron","w":["The binding energy of the fluorine-18 nucleus","The kinetic energy of the positron","The energy of the technetium-99m gamma ray"],"e":"E = mc² for an electron (or positron) is 511 keV. Two particles' worth of rest mass becomes two 511 keV photons.","c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","s":null,"p":"p7e77c390","pi":3,"id":"52083d2aed","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"q":"What is the rate law for this reaction?","a":"Rate = k[A]²[B]","w":["Rate = k[A][B]","Rate = k[A][B]²","Rate = k[A]²[B]²"],"e":"Trials 1 → 2: doubling [A] quadruples the rate, so it's second order in A. Trials 1 → 3: doubling [B] doubles the rate, so it's first order in B.","c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","s":null,"p":"p53c1c514","pi":0,"id":"912a8b17c7","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"What is the value of the rate constant k at 25 °C?","a":"2.0 M⁻²·s⁻¹","w":["0.20 M⁻¹·s⁻¹","2.0 × 10⁻³ M·s⁻¹","20 M⁻²·s⁻¹"],"e":"From trial 1: k = rate / ([A]²[B]) = 2.0 × 10⁻³ / (0.01 × 0.10) = 2.0 M⁻²·s⁻¹. The overall order is 3, which gives the units.","c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","s":null,"p":"p53c1c514","pi":1,"id":"4399543874","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"What initial rate should the student expect for trial 4?","a":"1.6 × 10⁻² M/s","w":["8.0 × 10⁻³ M/s","3.2 × 10⁻² M/s","4.0 × 10⁻³ M/s"],"e":"Compared with trial 2, only [B] doubles, and the reaction is first order in B: 2 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.6 × 10⁻² M/s.","c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","s":null,"p":"p53c1c514","pi":2,"id":"d441a2eb4f","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"q":"What best explains why the rates double at 35 °C?","a":"More collisions have enough energy to get over the activation barrier","w":["The activation energy of the reaction drops as the temperature rises","The reaction becomes more exothermic, which speeds up product formation","The concentrations of A and B rise because the solution expands"],"e":"By the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae^(−Ea/RT). Raising T increases k even though Ea stays the same, because more molecules have energy above Ea.","c":"5E","t":"Kinetics","s":null,"p":"p53c1c514","pi":3,"id":"c5e26e2b5b","L":"5E · Thermodynamics and kinetics","T":"Kinetics","g":"Foundation 5 · Chemistry of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Kinematics","q":"A ball is thrown horizontally at 10 m/s from the top of a 20 m cliff. Ignoring air resistance (g = 10 m/s²), how long does it take to reach the ground?","a":"2 s","w":["1 s","4 s","1.4 s"],"e":"Vertical motion is independent of horizontal speed: h = ½gt² → 20 = 5t² → t = 2 s.","id":"5b8bd0f175","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Kinematics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Kinematics","q":"A rock is thrown straight up at 20 m/s (g = 10 m/s²). What maximum height does it reach above the launch point?","a":"20 m","w":["10 m","40 m","2 m"],"e":"At the top v = 0, so v₀² = 2gh → h = 400 / 20 = 20 m.","id":"145a3001c9","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Kinematics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Kinematics","q":"A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 3 m/s² for 4 s. How far does it travel?","a":"24 m","w":["12 m","48 m","36 m"],"e":"d = ½at² = ½(3)(16) = 24 m.","id":"b2ab479761","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Kinematics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Kinematics","q":"A projectile is launched at 45° above the horizontal. At the top of its path, which statement is true (no air resistance)?","a":"Its vertical velocity is zero but its horizontal velocity is unchanged","w":["Both its velocity and its acceleration are zero","Its acceleration is zero but its velocity is not","Its horizontal velocity is zero but its vertical velocity is not"],"e":"Gravity acts only vertically, so the horizontal component stays constant. At the apex the vertical component is momentarily zero, while acceleration is still g downward.","id":"a4aa3b7272","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Kinematics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Newton's laws","q":"An object moves in a straight line at constant velocity. What is the net force acting on it?","a":"Zero","w":["Equal to its weight","In the direction of motion","Opposite the direction of motion"],"e":"Newton's first law: constant velocity means zero acceleration, so the net force is zero (forces may act, but they balance).","id":"dbeb6acb86","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Newton's laws","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Newton's laws","q":"A horizontal 10 N force pulls a 2 kg block across a frictionless floor. What is the block's acceleration?","a":"5 m/s²","w":["20 m/s²","0.2 m/s²","10 m/s²"],"e":"a = F/m = 10 N / 2 kg = 5 m/s².","id":"668ddb7825","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Newton's laws","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Newton's laws","q":"A person stands on a scale in an elevator that is accelerating upward. Compared with when the elevator is at rest, the scale reading is:","a":"Greater, because the normal force must exceed the person's weight","w":["Smaller, because the floor is moving away from the person's feet","The same, because the person's mass does not change in the elevator","Zero, because the person and the scale accelerate upward together"],"e":"N − mg = ma, so N = m(g + a) > mg. The scale reads the normal force.","id":"099c3eb323","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Newton's laws","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Newton's laws","q":"A horse pulls a cart forward. By Newton's third law, the cart pulls back on the horse with a force that is:","a":"Equal in magnitude and opposite in direction","w":["Smaller, which is why the cart moves forward","Larger only while the cart is speeding up","Zero once the cart is moving"],"e":"Action-reaction pairs are always equal and opposite and act on different objects. Whether each object accelerates depends on the other forces acting on it, like friction with the ground.","id":"0537b0d65c","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Newton's laws","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Friction and inclines","q":"A block slides down a frictionless incline at angle θ. Its acceleration along the incline is:","a":"g sin θ","w":["g cos θ","g tan θ","g"],"e":"The component of gravity parallel to the incline is mg sin θ, so a = g sin θ.","id":"aa2deb43d6","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Friction and inclines","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Friction and inclines","q":"A 10 kg box rests on a floor with a coefficient of static friction of 0.5 (g = 10 m/s²). What minimum horizontal force will start it moving?","a":"50 N","w":["5 N","100 N","20 N"],"e":"Maximum static friction = μₛN = 0.5 × 100 N = 50 N. You must just exceed this to start motion.","id":"e03db37ea2","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Friction and inclines","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Friction and inclines","q":"Once a sliding box is moving, the friction acting on it is usually:","a":"Smaller than the maximum static friction, because μₖ < μₛ","w":["Larger than static friction, because the box has momentum","Zero, because friction only resists the start of motion","Equal to the applied force at all times"],"e":"Kinetic friction coefficients are typically lower than static ones, which is why it's harder to start a box moving than to keep it moving.","id":"b0088f5daf","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Friction and inclines","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Work and energy","q":"An object moves in a circle at constant speed. How much work does the centripetal force do over one revolution?","a":"Zero","w":["mv²","2πr × mv²/r","It depends on the radius"],"e":"The centripetal force is always perpendicular to the displacement, so W = Fd cos 90° = 0. Kinetic energy stays the same.","id":"989dbf476d","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Work and energy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Work and energy","q":"If an object's speed doubles, its kinetic energy:","a":"Quadruples","w":["Doubles","Stays the same","Halves"],"e":"KE = ½mv², so KE scales with v². Doubling v multiplies KE by 4.","id":"cb200f54bf","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Work and energy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Work and energy","q":"A person lifts a 50 kg barbell 2 m in 4 s (g = 10 m/s²). What is the average power output?","a":"250 W","w":["1000 W","400 W","100 W"],"e":"Work = mgh = 50 × 10 × 2 = 1000 J. Power = 1000 J / 4 s = 250 W.","id":"03215b74ab","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Work and energy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Work and energy","q":"A spring with k = 200 N/m is stretched 0.1 m from equilibrium. How much elastic potential energy does it store?","a":"1 J","w":["2 J","10 J","20 J"],"e":"U = ½kx² = ½ × 200 × 0.01 = 1 J.","id":"fdc3966494","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Work and energy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Work and energy","q":"An ideal lever lets you lift a crate using half the force needed to lift it directly. Compared with lifting it directly, the work you do is:","a":"The same, because you push through twice the distance","w":["Half as much, because the force is halved","Twice as much, because of the lever's motion","One-quarter as much, because work scales with force squared"],"e":"Ideal machines trade force for distance. Mechanical advantage reduces the force but not the work, since W = Fd.","id":"07663be0e0","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Work and energy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Momentum","q":"Two carts collide and stick together on a frictionless track. Which quantity is conserved?","a":"Total momentum, but not total kinetic energy","w":["Total kinetic energy, but not momentum","Both momentum and kinetic energy","Neither momentum nor kinetic energy"],"e":"In any collision with no external net force, momentum is conserved. A perfectly inelastic collision (objects stick) loses kinetic energy to heat and deformation.","id":"770a517f7b","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Momentum","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Momentum","q":"A 1 kg cart moving at 4 m/s hits and sticks to a stationary 3 kg cart. What is their shared speed afterward?","a":"1 m/s","w":["4 m/s","2 m/s","1.33 m/s"],"e":"Momentum: (1)(4) = (1 + 3)v → v = 1 m/s.","id":"c0a0270dd0","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Momentum","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Torque and equilibrium","q":"A 30 kg child sits 2 m from the pivot of a seesaw. Where must a 20 kg child sit on the other side to balance it?","a":"3 m from the pivot","w":["1.33 m from the pivot","2 m from the pivot","4 m from the pivot"],"e":"Balance torques: 30 × 2 = 20 × d → d = 3 m.","id":"d8e27786ed","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Torque and equilibrium","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Torque and equilibrium","q":"For an object to be in static equilibrium, which conditions must hold?","a":"Net force and net torque are both zero","w":["Only the net force is zero; torque can be anything","Only the net torque is zero; force can be anything","The net force must equal the net torque in size"],"e":"Translational equilibrium requires ΣF = 0, and rotational equilibrium requires Στ = 0.","id":"329874bbda","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Torque and equilibrium","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4A","t":"Torque and equilibrium","q":"A 4 N force is applied at the end of a 0.5 m wrench at 30° to the wrench handle. What torque does it produce?","a":"1 N·m","w":["2 N·m","1.7 N·m","0.5 N·m"],"e":"τ = rF sin θ = 0.5 × 4 × sin 30° = 0.5 × 4 × 0.5 = 1 N·m.","id":"8875409c0d","L":"4A · Motion, forces, work and energy","T":"Torque and equilibrium","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Buoyancy","q":"A block floats in fresh water (1000 kg/m³) with 3/4 of its volume submerged. What is the block's density?","a":"750 kg/m³","w":["250 kg/m³","1333 kg/m³","1000 kg/m³"],"e":"For a floating object, fraction submerged = ρ_object / ρ_fluid. So ρ = 0.75 × 1000 = 750 kg/m³.","id":"14016cec33","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Buoyancy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Buoyancy","q":"A fully submerged object is moved deeper in a lake. The buoyant force on it:","a":"Stays about the same, because it depends on the displaced volume","w":["Increases, because water pressure increases with depth","Decreases, because less of the lake is beneath the object","Becomes exactly equal to the object's weight at any depth"],"e":"Buoyant force = ρ_fluid × V_displaced × g. Water is nearly incompressible, so the force barely changes with depth.","id":"60fe71fb76","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Buoyancy","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Fluid pressure","q":"What is the gauge pressure 10 m below the surface of fresh water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 10 m/s²)?","a":"1 × 10⁵ Pa (about 1 atm)","w":["1 × 10⁴ Pa","2 × 10⁵ Pa","1 × 10³ Pa"],"e":"Gauge pressure = ρgh = 1000 × 10 × 10 = 10⁵ Pa. Absolute pressure would be about 2 atm once you add the atmosphere.","id":"38c8ec541c","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid pressure","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Fluid pressure","q":"In a hydraulic lift, the output piston has 10 times the area of the input piston. Compared with the input piston, the output piston:","a":"Exerts 10 times the force but moves 1/10 the distance","w":["Exerts 10 times the force and moves 10 times the distance","Exerts the same force over a larger area","Exerts 1/10 the force but moves 10 times the distance"],"e":"Pascal's principle: pressure is transmitted equally, so F₂ = F₁(A₂/A₁). Volume is conserved, so the larger piston moves a smaller distance and work is unchanged.","id":"db29bcb8a1","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid pressure","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","q":"Blood flows through a vessel that narrows to half its original radius. Assuming continuity holds, the flow speed in the narrow section is:","a":"4 times faster","w":["2 times faster","Half as fast","16 times faster"],"e":"A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. Area ∝ r², so halving r cuts area to 1/4, and speed must rise 4-fold.","id":"8aca1c348c","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","q":"According to Bernoulli's principle, where a horizontal pipe narrows and fluid speeds up, the fluid's pressure:","a":"Decreases","w":["Increases","Stays the same","Drops to zero"],"e":"For horizontal flow, P + ½ρv² is constant along a streamline, so higher speed means lower pressure.","id":"e71902e466","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Fluid flow","q":"By Poiseuille's law, if a blood vessel's radius is halved while the pressure difference stays the same, volume flow rate becomes:","a":"1/16 of the original","w":["1/4 of the original","1/2 of the original","1/8 of the original"],"e":"Q ∝ r⁴ΔP / (ηL). (1/2)⁴ = 1/16, which is why small changes in arteriole radius strongly control blood flow.","id":"a878204289","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Fluid flow","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Gases","q":"Dry air is about 21% oxygen. At sea level (760 mmHg), what is the partial pressure of oxygen in dry air?","a":"About 160 mmHg","w":["About 21 mmHg","About 100 mmHg","About 600 mmHg"],"e":"Dalton's law: P_O₂ = 0.21 × 760 ≈ 160 mmHg.","id":"0e7304f3ef","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Gases","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Gases","q":"By Graham's law, how much faster does H₂ (2 g/mol) effuse than O₂ (32 g/mol) at the same temperature?","a":"4 times faster","w":["16 times faster","2 times faster","8 times faster"],"e":"Rate ∝ 1/√M. √(32/2) = √16 = 4.","id":"36548c83a9","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Gases","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4B","t":"Gases","q":"At constant temperature, a sealed gas sample's pressure is doubled. Its volume:","a":"Halves","w":["Doubles","Stays the same","Quarters"],"e":"Boyle's law: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ at constant T and n.","id":"334ce2000a","L":"4B · Fluids and gas exchange","T":"Gases","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Circuits","q":"Resistors of 2 Ω, 3 Ω and 5 Ω are connected in series. What is the equivalent resistance?","a":"10 Ω","w":["0.97 Ω","30 Ω","3.3 Ω"],"e":"Series resistances add: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 Ω.","id":"c6c741dcaa","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Circuits","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Circuits","q":"Two 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?","a":"3 Ω","w":["12 Ω","6 Ω","36 Ω"],"e":"1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3, so R = 3 Ω. Adding parallel paths always lowers total resistance.","id":"2bf687f2a4","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Circuits","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Circuits","q":"A 12 V battery is connected across a 4 Ω resistor. What power does the resistor dissipate?","a":"36 W","w":["48 W","3 W","12 W"],"e":"I = V/R = 3 A. P = IV = 3 × 12 = 36 W (or V²/R = 144/4).","id":"1f2ae5e533","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Circuits","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Circuits","q":"In a circuit, an ammeter should be connected ___ and should have ___ resistance.","a":"In series; very low","w":["In parallel; very high","In series; very high","In parallel; very low"],"e":"An ammeter must carry the same current as the branch, so it goes in series and should barely change that current. Voltmeters go in parallel with very high resistance.","id":"ce2ab3d44f","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Circuits","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Capacitors","q":"A parallel-plate capacitor stays connected to a battery while its plate separation is doubled. Its capacitance:","a":"Halves, and the stored charge also halves","w":["Doubles, and the charge doubles","Halves, but the charge stays the same","Stays the same, but the voltage doubles"],"e":"C = ε₀A/d, so doubling d halves C. With the battery connected, V is fixed, so Q = CV also halves.","id":"24615e0c08","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Capacitors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Capacitors","q":"A dielectric is inserted between the plates of a charged capacitor that has been disconnected from its battery. What happens?","a":"Capacitance increases and the voltage across it decreases","w":["Capacitance decreases and the voltage increases","Both capacitance and charge increase","Capacitance stays the same and the charge decreases"],"e":"C = κC₀ rises. Isolated, Q cannot change, so V = Q/C drops.","id":"49831431c9","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Capacitors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrostatics","q":"The distance between two point charges is doubled. The electrostatic force between them becomes:","a":"1/4 as large","w":["1/2 as large","Twice as large","4 times as large"],"e":"Coulomb's law: F = kq₁q₂/r². Doubling r divides F by 4.","id":"954324e628","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrostatics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrostatics","q":"A proton is released from rest in a uniform electric field. It will:","a":"Accelerate in the direction of the field, losing electric potential energy","w":["Accelerate opposite the field, gaining electric potential energy","Move at constant velocity along the field, with no change in energy","Stay at rest, because a uniform field exerts equal force on all sides"],"e":"Positive charges feel force along E and move toward lower potential, converting electric potential energy into kinetic energy.","id":"2d8dce8a4c","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrostatics","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrochemistry","q":"In a galvanic cell, oxidation occurs at the ___ and electrons flow through the wire toward the ___.","a":"Anode; cathode","w":["Cathode; anode","Anode; salt bridge","Cathode; salt bridge"],"e":"An Ox, Red Cat: oxidation at the anode, reduction at the cathode. Electrons travel from anode to cathode through the external circuit.","id":"3db3ee94b9","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrochemistry","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrochemistry","q":"For an electrolytic cell, which is true?","a":"ΔG > 0 and E_cell < 0, so an external power source is needed","w":["ΔG < 0 and E_cell > 0, so the reaction runs on its own","ΔG = 0 at all times, because current flows in both directions","The anode is the negative electrode and the cathode is positive"],"e":"Electrolytic cells drive non-spontaneous reactions. The anode is still where oxidation happens, but it is the positive electrode here.","id":"e26545b39b","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrochemistry","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrochemistry","q":"Using ΔG° = −nFE°, a redox reaction with a positive standard cell potential has a ΔG° that is:","a":"Negative, so the reaction is spontaneous under standard conditions","w":["Positive, so the reaction is non-spontaneous under standard conditions","Zero, because n and F cancel out under standard conditions","Positive only if more than one electron is transferred per reaction"],"e":"n and F are positive, so a positive E° gives a negative ΔG°.","id":"669c9413cd","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrochemistry","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4C","t":"Electrochemistry","q":"What is the standard cell potential (E°) of a concentration cell?","a":"0 V","w":["1.10 V","It equals the reduction potential of the metal","It depends on the salt bridge"],"e":"Both half-cells use the same reaction, so E° = 0. Any voltage comes from the concentration difference (the Nernst equation).","id":"83a4d165cd","L":"4C · Electrochemistry and circuits","T":"Electrochemistry","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","q":"An object sits 20 cm in front of a converging lens with focal length 10 cm. The image is:","a":"Real, inverted and the same size, 20 cm from the lens","w":["Virtual, upright and twice the size, 20 cm from the lens","Real, upright and half the size, 10 cm from the lens","Virtual, inverted and the same size, 40 cm from the lens"],"e":"1/f = 1/dₒ + 1/dᵢ → 1/10 = 1/20 + 1/dᵢ → dᵢ = 20 cm. m = −dᵢ/dₒ = −1, so the image is inverted and the same size.","id":"f3068cba84","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","q":"A diverging lens forms an image of a real object. The image is always:","a":"Virtual, upright and smaller than the object","w":["Real, inverted and larger than the object","Real, upright and smaller than the object","Virtual, inverted and larger than the object"],"e":"Diverging lenses spread light out, so rays only appear to meet on the object's side: virtual, upright, reduced.","id":"ec03afa809","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","q":"A person with myopia (nearsightedness) needs corrective lenses that are:","a":"Diverging, because the eye focuses images in front of the retina","w":["Converging, because the eye focuses images behind the retina","Converging, because the eye focuses images in front of the retina","Diverging, because the eye focuses images behind the retina"],"e":"A myopic eye has too much converging power. A diverging lens pushes the focal point back onto the retina. Hyperopia uses converging lenses.","id":"1bb588dc38","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Lenses and mirrors","q":"A lens has a focal length of +25 cm. What is its power?","a":"+4 diopters","w":["+0.04 diopters","+25 diopters","−4 diopters"],"e":"Power = 1/f in meters = 1/0.25 m = +4 D. Converging lenses have positive power.","id":"e1efc4c342","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Lenses and mirrors","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Refraction","q":"Light passes from water (n = 1.33) into glass (n = 1.5). As it enters the glass, it:","a":"Slows down and bends toward the normal","w":["Speeds up and bends away from the normal","Slows down and bends away from the normal","Keeps the same speed but changes frequency"],"e":"Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Moving into a higher index means lower speed and a smaller angle from the normal.","id":"b382febcd4","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Refraction","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Refraction","q":"Total internal reflection can occur when light travels:","a":"From a higher-index medium to a lower-index medium beyond the critical angle","w":["From a lower-index medium into a higher-index medium at any angle","Along the normal, perpendicular to the boundary between two media","From a vacuum into any transparent material beyond a set angle"],"e":"Only going into a lower index can the refracted angle reach 90°. Past the critical angle, all light reflects, which is how fiber-optic endoscopes work.","id":"e89e68a6e0","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Refraction","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Waves and sound","q":"A sound wave passes from air into water. Which property stays the same?","a":"Frequency","w":["Wavelength","Speed","Both speed and wavelength"],"e":"Frequency is set by the source. Sound is faster in water, so with v = fλ the wavelength increases.","id":"37477c11bb","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Waves and sound","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Waves and sound","q":"An ambulance siren moves toward a stationary listener. Compared with the emitted frequency, the listener hears:","a":"A higher frequency","w":["A lower frequency","The same frequency but louder","The same frequency at a higher speed"],"e":"Doppler effect: a source approaching the observer compresses the wavefronts, raising the observed frequency.","id":"a8afa9ab52","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Waves and sound","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Waves and sound","q":"Increasing a sound's intensity by a factor of 100 raises its sound level by:","a":"20 dB","w":["100 dB","10 dB","2 dB"],"e":"β = 10 log(I/I₀). A 100× increase adds 10 log 100 = 20 dB.","id":"b059ecb734","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Waves and sound","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Waves and sound","q":"A pipe closed at one end and open at the other has length L. The wavelength of its fundamental is:","a":"4L","w":["2L","L","L/2"],"e":"The fundamental has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end, a quarter wavelength: L = λ/4.","id":"2a4dab8ba3","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Waves and sound","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Light","q":"Compared with a photon of red light, a photon of blue light has:","a":"Higher frequency and higher energy","w":["Higher frequency but lower energy","Longer wavelength and higher energy","Lower frequency and lower energy"],"e":"E = hf = hc/λ. Blue light has a shorter wavelength, so a higher frequency and more energy per photon.","id":"11bf9a6677","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Light","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4D","t":"Light","q":"Unpolarized light passes through two polarizing filters whose axes are at 90° to each other. How much light gets through?","a":"None","w":["Half","One-quarter","All of it"],"e":"The first filter passes half and polarizes it. Malus's law at the second: I = I₁cos²90° = 0.","id":"cdadb9c5b6","L":"4D · Light and sound","T":"Light","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","q":"When a nucleus undergoes alpha decay, its mass number and atomic number change by:","a":"−4 and −2","w":["−2 and −4","0 and +1","−4 and +2"],"e":"An alpha particle is a helium-4 nucleus (2 protons, 2 neutrons).","id":"5ba3c51901","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","q":"In beta-minus decay, what happens inside the nucleus?","a":"A neutron becomes a proton, and an electron and antineutrino are emitted","w":["A proton becomes a neutron, and a positron and neutrino are emitted","Two protons and two neutrons are ejected together as one particle","An inner-shell electron is captured and combines with a proton"],"e":"β⁻ decay raises the atomic number by 1 and leaves the mass number unchanged.","id":"18fe3018d1","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","q":"In positron emission, the daughter nucleus has:","a":"The same mass number and an atomic number one lower","w":["The same mass number and an atomic number one higher","A mass number one lower and the same atomic number","A mass number four lower and an atomic number two lower"],"e":"β⁺ decay turns a proton into a neutron: Z decreases by 1 and A is unchanged. Example: fluorine-18 → oxygen-18, used in PET scans.","id":"d58bdde4aa","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","q":"A radioisotope has a half-life of 8 days. What fraction of a sample remains after 24 days?","a":"1/8","w":["1/3","1/4","1/16"],"e":"24 days is 3 half-lives: (1/2)³ = 1/8.","id":"aa8702d9c7","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Nuclear decay","q":"Gamma emission changes a nucleus's:","a":"Energy only, not its mass number or atomic number","w":["Atomic number by +1, with no change in mass","Mass number by −1, with no change in charge","Mass and atomic numbers by −4 and −2"],"e":"Gamma rays are high-energy photons released as an excited nucleus relaxes. No particles with mass or charge leave.","id":"8a21e33e53","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Nuclear decay","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Atomic structure","q":"Isotopes of the same element differ in their number of:","a":"Neutrons","w":["Protons","Electrons in a neutral atom","Valence electrons"],"e":"The proton count defines the element. Isotopes share it but have different neutron counts, so different mass numbers.","id":"355d15aa36","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Atomic structure","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Electron configuration","q":"What is the electron configuration of Fe²⁺ (iron is atomic number 26)?","a":"[Ar] 3d⁶","w":["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵"],"e":"Neutral Fe is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. Transition metals lose their 4s electrons first when they form cations.","id":"3e455f78b7","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Electron configuration","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Electron configuration","q":"Hund's rule says that electrons filling a set of equal-energy orbitals will:","a":"Occupy separate orbitals with parallel spins before pairing up","w":["Pair up in one orbital before moving to the next one","Fill the highest-energy orbital in the set first","Take opposite spins in separate orbitals of the set"],"e":"Spreading out with parallel spins minimizes electron-electron repulsion. That's why nitrogen's three 2p electrons are all unpaired.","id":"e68aed1951","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Electron configuration","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Electron configuration","q":"The Pauli exclusion principle states that:","a":"No two electrons in an atom can have the same four quantum numbers","w":["Electrons fill orbitals from lowest to highest energy","You cannot know an electron's position and momentum exactly","Electrons pair only after each orbital has one electron"],"e":"So any one orbital holds at most two electrons, with opposite spins. The other options describe the Aufbau principle, Heisenberg's uncertainty principle and Hund's rule.","id":"c0e186b3f4","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Electron configuration","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Quantum effects","q":"In the photoelectric effect, light below a metal's threshold frequency is made much brighter. What happens?","a":"No electrons are emitted, no matter the intensity","w":["More electrons are emitted with the same energy","Electrons are emitted with more kinetic energy","Electrons are emitted after a time delay"],"e":"Each electron absorbs one photon, and a photon's energy depends only on its frequency. Below threshold, no single photon has enough energy.","id":"aff16b7ea2","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Quantum effects","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Quantum effects","q":"An electron in hydrogen drops from n = 3 to n = 2. The atom:","a":"Emits a photon whose energy equals the difference between the two levels","w":["Absorbs a photon whose energy equals that of the n = 3 level","Emits a photon whose energy equals that of the n = 2 level","Neither absorbs nor emits energy, since the electron stays bound"],"e":"Moving to a lower energy level releases the energy difference as a photon. This transition gives the red Balmer line at about 656 nm.","id":"597cf13a2d","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Quantum effects","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Periodic trends","q":"Moving left to right across a period, first ionization energy generally:","a":"Increases, because effective nuclear charge increases","w":["Decreases, because atoms get larger","Stays the same, because the shell number is constant","Increases, because atomic radius increases"],"e":"More protons in the same shell pull valence electrons more tightly, which shrinks the atoms and makes electrons harder to remove.","id":"1aee91ae50","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Periodic trends","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"},{"c":"4E","t":"Periodic trends","q":"Which atom has the largest atomic radius?","a":"Potassium (K)","w":["Sodium (Na)","Chlorine (Cl)","Argon (Ar)"],"e":"Radius increases down a group (new shells) and decreases across a period. K is lowest and furthest left of these.","id":"4777fca9cb","L":"4E · Atoms, nuclear decay and electrons","T":"Periodic trends","g":"Foundation 4 · Physics of living systems"}],"cars":[{"c":"C1","t":"Humanities","x":"When a seventeenth-century physician coined the word 'nostalgia,' he meant a disease. Soldiers far from home, he observed, grew listless, stopped eating and sometimes died, and he traced their decline to an aching desire to return. Only later did the word soften into the gentle, almost pleasant longing we now mean by it. The change tells us less about soldiers than about ourselves: we have learned to enjoy a feeling our ancestors feared.","q":"The author's main point about the word 'nostalgia' is that:","a":"Its change in meaning reflects a change in how people relate to longing","w":["It was first used by physicians to describe a pleasant feeling","Soldiers today no longer feel the homesickness once described","Physicians were wrong to treat homesickness as a real disease"],"e":"The final sentence says the shift tells us 'about ourselves': we now enjoy a feeling that was once feared.","id":"8c3e126e87","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","t":"Humanities","x":"Museums train us in a particular way of looking. Velvet ropes, hushed rooms and signs forbidding touch all teach that art is something to be contemplated from a respectful distance. Yet many of the objects in these rooms, such as ritual masks, drinking cups and carved chairs, were made to be handled, worn or used. Displayed behind glass, they become paintings without frames.","q":"The author's argument assumes that:","a":"The way an object is displayed shapes how viewers understand it","w":["Ritual masks are more valuable than paintings in museum collections","Museums should let visitors handle every object in their collections","Quiet contemplation is the only proper way to respond to any artwork"],"e":"The author's claim that glass cases make used objects into 'paintings' depends on the idea that display affects understanding. The passage doesn't go as far as recommending that visitors touch everything.","id":"473e738874","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","t":"Social sciences","x":"A popular argument holds that more choice makes people less happy. Faced with forty jams instead of six, shoppers supposedly freeze, buy nothing, or regret what they chose. The idea has the appeal of a good paradox, and it flatters anyone who has stood dazed in a supermarket aisle. But a paradox is not a law, and the evidence for it has proven less consistent than its fame suggests.","q":"Which finding would most WEAKEN the 'popular argument' the author describes?","a":"Large studies of many products find no average drop in buying or satisfaction as options grow","w":["In one store, shoppers bought less jam when shown forty flavors than when shown six","Shoppers report feeling stressed and rushed when supermarkets are very crowded","Shoppers prefer stores with many cereal brands but usually buy just one box"],"e":"The argument predicts that more choice reduces buying or satisfaction. Broad evidence of no such drop undercuts it, which fits the author's doubt about its consistency.","id":"de73ab9ea8","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","t":"Social sciences","x":"On a summer afternoon, the pavement in a treeless neighborhood can run several degrees hotter than streets a short drive away under heavy shade. These differences are not random. Tree cover often tracks income and decades-old housing policy, so the cooler blocks tend to be the wealthier ones. Planting trees, then, is not only a matter of beautification but of fairness.","q":"The author mentions the temperature difference between neighborhoods mainly in order to:","a":"Provide evidence for the claim that tree cover is a fairness issue","w":["Explain the physical process by which trees cool the air","Argue that wealthy neighborhoods should give up some of their trees","Show that temperature differences between neighborhoods are random"],"e":"The temperature gap, linked to income and policy, supports the conclusion that tree planting is 'a matter of fairness.' The author says the differences are 'not random.'","id":"3390824927","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","t":"Humanities","x":"The novelist revised the ending of her best-known book four times across four editions, each time making the heroine's fate a little less certain. Critics have called this indecision. I would call it honesty. She had come to believe that the life she was describing did not resolve, and she refused to give readers a resolution she no longer believed in.","q":"The author's attitude toward the novelist's revisions is best described as:","a":"Approving","w":["Dismissive","Puzzled","Neutral"],"e":"The author rejects the critics' word 'indecision' and calls the revisions 'honesty,' a clearly favorable judgment.","id":"f61f8605bf","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","t":"Social sciences","x":"Languages divide the color spectrum differently. Some have a single word covering what English speakers call blue and green; others split English 'blue' into separate basic terms for light and dark shades. Experiments suggest that speakers tell colors apart slightly faster when the colors fall under different words in their language. Language does not stop anyone from seeing a color, but it seems to sharpen the boundaries it names.","q":"Based on the passage, which result would the author most expect?","a":"Speakers with separate words for light and dark blue tell those shades apart a bit faster","w":["Speakers of a language with one word for blue and green cannot see any difference between them","English speakers, lacking a basic term for dark blue, are unable to perceive dark blue shades","Speakers of every language distinguish all colors at the same speed, regardless of vocabulary"],"e":"The author says naming boundaries speeds discrimination slightly but 'does not stop anyone from seeing a color,' which rules out the stronger claims.","id":"f7284c1436","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","t":"Social sciences","x":"Students who take notes by hand often remember lectures better than those who type. A common explanation is that handwriting is slow: unable to copy every word, the hand-writer must decide what matters and put it in their own words, and that processing aids memory. The laptop user, able to transcribe nearly everything, can let the words pass through without thinking about them.","q":"Which finding would most STRENGTHEN the explanation given in the passage?","a":"Laptop users who are told to summarize in their own words remember as much as hand-writers","w":["Hand-writers remember more because paper is less distracting than a screen full of apps","Most students say they prefer typing notes on laptops to writing on paper","Typing is faster than handwriting for most students, so laptop notes are longer"],"e":"If the advantage disappears when typists are made to process and rephrase, that supports the claim that processing, not handwriting itself, aids memory. The first wrong option offers a competing explanation.","id":"b53a360b0f","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","t":"Humanities","x":"Jazz improvisation is often described as self-expression, but the description leaves something out. A soloist on the bandstand is answering the drummer's accents, picking up a phrase the pianist just played and leaving room for the bassist to respond. What sounds like a single voice is closer to one side of a conversation, and the best improvisers are, above all, good listeners.","q":"Which statement best captures the main idea of the passage?","a":"Jazz improvisation depends on responding to others as much as on self-expression","w":["Jazz soloists should play less often and leave more silence for others","Improvisation is purely personal expression, independent of the band","Drummers are the most important members of any jazz group on stage"],"e":"The author argues the usual description 'leaves something out' and reframes the solo as part of a conversation.","id":"1b51cf2128","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","t":"Social sciences","x":"Historians prize diaries because they seem private, written for no audience but the writer. Yet many diarists, especially public figures, expected that their pages might one day be read, and some revised entries years later. A diary is less a window into a moment than a document with its own purposes, and it must be read as critically as a speech.","q":"The author implies that historians who treat diaries as purely private records:","a":"May accept self-presentation as unfiltered truth","w":["Are reading the most reliable source available","Should rely only on public speeches","Can ignore when an entry was written"],"e":"If diarists wrote for future readers or revised entries, then treating diaries as unguarded can mistake a crafted image for fact.","id":"b73cf26ee2","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C3","t":"Humanities","x":"Stoic philosophers recommended an odd exercise: imagine, ahead of time, the misfortunes that might befall you, such as losing your job, your health or a friend. The aim was not gloom. By picturing loss calmly, they believed, a person would be less shaken if it came and would value what they had while they still had it.","q":"Which practice is most consistent with the Stoic exercise described?","a":"Before a big presentation, picturing it going badly and calmly deciding how you would respond","w":["Before a big presentation, refusing to think about anything that might go wrong","Before a big presentation, worrying for days about every possible disaster","Before a big presentation, assuming it will go well because past ones did"],"e":"The exercise is calm, deliberate anticipation of loss, not avoidance and not anxious rumination.","id":"6770ca4dad","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","t":"Humanities","x":"The slogan 'form follows function' made modern architecture sound like engineering: find the purpose, and the shape will follow. But function rarely dictates a single form. A hundred buildings can house an office equally well, and the choice among them is made on other grounds: cost, fashion, pride, the wish to impress. The slogan did not remove taste from design; it disguised it.","q":"The author would most likely DISAGREE with which statement?","a":"A building's purpose determines the one correct shape for it","w":["Taste plays some role in decisions about how buildings look","Many different designs can serve the same function equally well","Cost influences the form that new buildings end up taking"],"e":"The author argues 'function rarely dictates a single form.' The other three statements match points the author makes.","id":"2a1aa4fd87","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C1","t":"Social sciences","x":"For decades, economists warned that shared resources such as pastures, fisheries and forests would be overused unless they were privatized or controlled by the state. Elinor Ostrom spent her career documenting communities that did neither. Fishers, farmers and herders around the world had built their own rules for monitoring use and punishing cheaters, and many of these systems had lasted for generations.","q":"The passage mainly suggests that:","a":"Communities can sometimes manage shared resources well without privatization or the state","w":["Shared resources are always overused unless outsiders step in to manage them","Privatization is the most reliable way to protect fisheries and pastures","State control works better than local rules for managing shared resources"],"e":"Ostrom's examples are presented as counterevidence to the warning that only private or state ownership prevents overuse.","id":"e653046276","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C3","t":"Humanities","x":"Humor is among the hardest things to translate, because so much of it rests on what a reader already knows. A pun depends on two meanings sharing one sound, which rarely happens in another language. A joke about a local politician depends on knowing the politician. The translator of comedy must often choose between explaining the joke, which kills it, and replacing it with a different joke, which changes the book.","q":"Which situation best illustrates the translator's dilemma described in the passage?","a":"A translator swaps a pun that doesn't work in the new language for a different pun, changing the line","w":["A translator renders a weather report word for word, since nothing in it depends on wordplay","A translator quietly corrects a factual error about a city's population in the original text","A translator keeps a joke unchanged because it happens to work equally well in both languages"],"e":"The dilemma is between explaining the joke and replacing it. Swapping in a new pun 'changes the book,' which is one horn of that choice.","id":"0645b81676","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","t":"Social sciences","x":"Critics of standardized tests sometimes argue that the tests measure only test-taking skill, since students who take prep courses score higher. But the conclusion does not follow. Prep courses also review the content being tested, so higher scores after a course could just as easily reflect learning the material.","q":"The author's main criticism of the argument is that it:","a":"Ignores another explanation for the evidence it cites","w":["Relies on too small a sample of students to draw any conclusion","Uses the word 'skill' with two different meanings in one argument","Assumes that all standardized tests are the same length and format"],"e":"Higher scores after prep could come from learning the content, not just test-taking tricks. The critics overlook this alternative.","id":"152a2733d8","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","t":"Humanities","x":"The garden designer insisted that a garden is never finished. Plants grow, spread and die; a border that looks right in June looks wrong by September. Her plans, accordingly, were less like blueprints than like instructions for a performance, telling future gardeners what effect to aim for rather than exactly what to plant where.","q":"In the passage, comparing the designer's plans to 'instructions for a performance' suggests that the plans:","a":"Set goals for later gardeners to interpret, rather than fixed specifications","w":["Were meant to be read aloud to visitors touring the garden","Gave precise measurements for exactly where each plant should go","Were originally drawn up as backdrops for theatrical productions"],"e":"The contrast with 'blueprints' shows the plans guided an evolving result, like a score that each performance interprets.","id":"a68dbb3262","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","t":"Social sciences","x":"In many workplaces, the person who stays latest is assumed to be the hardest worker. Yet hours in the office measure presence, not output. A manager who rewards late nights may end up rewarding slow work, or the appearance of work, while penalizing the employee who finishes efficiently and goes home.","q":"Based on the passage, which policy would the author most likely recommend?","a":"Judging employees by what they accomplish rather than by how long they stay","w":["Requiring every employee to stay until a fixed late hour each day","Giving year-end bonuses to whichever employees stay the latest","Tracking the exact time each employee leaves the office every night"],"e":"The author argues that hours measure presence, not output, so evaluations should focus on output.","id":"62ec4dbcfb","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","q":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","a":"Since a painting's original state is uncertain, conservation should be cautious, reversible and openly interpretive","w":["Discolored varnish should always be removed so that viewers can finally see a painting's true, original colors","Leaving old varnish in place is the only way to respect what a painter intended a finished work to look like","Museums should stop cleaning old paintings entirely until stronger and more precise solvents can be developed"],"e":"The author rejects both full cleaning and leaving varnish as the 'correct' state, then argues for humility: reversible methods, small tests and public documentation. The passage explicitly says the conclusion is 'not paralysis.'","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p79ac3213","pi":0,"id":"17a0085026","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","q":"The author mentions visitors' gasps in both the first and last paragraphs most likely in order to:","a":"Suggest that a strong reaction to a cleaned painting does not prove the cleaning was faithful","w":["Show that the public strongly supports thorough cleaning of old paintings","Argue that museums should display only paintings that have not been cleaned","Prove that cleaned paintings are more beautiful than uncleaned ones are"],"e":"The first paragraph says gasps were 'taken as proof,' and the last says the gasp 'is not evidence.' The repetition frames the author's skepticism about treating delight as confirmation.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p79ac3213","pi":1,"id":"7075574a5f","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","q":"The author's attitude toward the defenders of thorough cleaning is best described as:","a":"Respectful but ultimately unconvinced","w":["Openly contemptuous of their view","Fully persuaded by their argument","Indifferent to the cleaning debate"],"e":"The author calls their reply 'not a foolish one' but then says both camps share a 'doubtful' assumption.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p79ac3213","pi":2,"id":"14de4c64b0","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","q":"Which practice would the author most likely support?","a":"Applying a removable new varnish after small, documented test cleanings","w":["Repainting faded areas to match the colors the artist described in surviving letters and notes","Removing all later retouching so that only the artist's own brushwork remains on the canvas","Leaving every painting untouched, since any cleaning would amount to an interpretation"],"e":"The final paragraph endorses reversible materials and documented small tests. Repainting and stripping all history assume a single correct state, which the author doubts.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p79ac3213","pi":3,"id":"add9212342","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C1","q":"The author's central claim is that:","a":"Libraries are valuable largely as free public spaces, which circulation figures don't capture","w":["Libraries should replace most of their books with digital media in order to stay relevant","Falling circulation figures show that public libraries have become declining institutions","Cafés and barbershops serve the same social purpose as libraries and could replace them"],"e":"The author argues that counting loans misses the library's role as a free place to be, while still keeping reading at its center.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p45692a88","pi":0,"id":"0548cf49e2","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"The author refers to Ray Oldenburg mainly in order to:","a":"Introduce a useful concept, then show how libraries differ from his examples","w":["Prove that cafés are more important to neighborhoods than libraries","Argue that every third place should operate as a commercial business","Show that sociologists have largely ignored libraries in their work"],"e":"The author borrows 'third place' but points out that Oldenburg's examples require spending money, which makes the library unusual.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p45692a88","pi":1,"id":"9bf22ab75a","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"Based on the passage, the author would most likely agree that a library that got rid of all its books would:","a":"Lose part of what makes it different from commercial spaces","w":["Serve job seekers and students better than before","Become a more successful third place for regulars","Raise its circulation figures by drawing new visitors"],"e":"The fourth paragraph says abandoning books would cost the library its 'quiet seriousness' that sets it apart from a mall.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p45692a88","pi":2,"id":"aaf5bcbcbd","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C3","q":"A city plans to cut library opening hours but keep the book budget the same, pointing to steady loan numbers. The author would most likely respond that the plan:","a":"Ignores the patrons who depend on the library as a place to be, not just a source of loans","w":["Is reasonable, because lending books is the library's main purpose and it is protected","Should cut the book budget instead, since books are less important than open hours","Will raise circulation by concentrating borrowers into fewer, busier opening hours"],"e":"The author lists long opening hours among the things that 'are the work' for many patrons. Judging the library only by loans is exactly the mistake the passage criticizes.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p45692a88","pi":3,"id":"ac83ef4ab2","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","q":"According to the author, the main flaw in the old joke is that it:","a":"Treats faithfulness as reproducing a list of meanings","w":["Underestimates how skilled good translators really are","Assumes that poems cannot be translated at all","Places too much value on the sound of a poem's lines"],"e":"The third paragraph says the joke 'goes wrong' in its 'picture of fidelity,' which imagines a poem as a fixed set of meanings.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p4d35aca9","pi":0,"id":"aec18b22db","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","q":"The discussion of rhyme in the second paragraph mainly serves to:","a":"Show why some loss in translating poetry cannot be avoided","w":["Prove that rhyming poems should not be translated","Argue that rhyme matters less than meaning","Show that skilled translators can preserve every rhyme"],"e":"The author uses rhyme to argue against the 'usual rebuttal.' A new rhyme invents a different relationship, so something is always lost.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p4d35aca9","pi":1,"id":"0445f2de03","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","q":"Which would the author most likely consider a dishonest translation?","a":"One whose preface claims it captures the original poem completely","w":["A plain prose version that admits it drops the poem's music","A version that rebuilds the rhymes and notes where the meaning shifts","A literal version meant to send readers back to the original"],"e":"The author says each approach is honest 'if it says what it is doing,' and that the dishonest translation 'pretends to have lost nothing.'","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p4d35aca9","pi":2,"id":"efc0da1e0d","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","q":"A student who can't read Russian wants to study a famous Russian poem. Based on the passage, the author would most likely advise the student to:","a":"Compare several translations that take different approaches","w":["Find the single most literal, word-for-word translation","Read only the translation with the most faithful rhymes","Avoid translations entirely and read a prose summary instead"],"e":"The last paragraph says readers are often 'best served by more than one translation,' because the poem appears 'in the space between them.'","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p4d35aca9","pi":3,"id":"6c7c1c2be4","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C1","q":"The main purpose of the passage is to:","a":"Argue that standardized time brought real benefits and new control over people's time","w":["Explain how railway engineers calculated the boundaries of the first time zones","Argue that clock time was forced on workers by greedy and deliberate factory owners","Show that local solar time was more accurate and more useful than standard time"],"e":"The author grants the benefits ('not imposed by villains') while stressing that standards reorganize power over time.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p7efa8aa5","pi":0,"id":"1908727070","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"The author mentions work 'organized around tasks' in order to:","a":"Contrast an older rhythm of work with work measured in hours","w":["Show that farmers resisted the arrival of the railways","Explain how factory owners decided on shift lengths","Argue that task-based work was more productive than shifts"],"e":"Task-based rhythms (finishing a harvest) are the 'before' picture. Clock-measured shifts are the 'after.'","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p7efa8aa5","pi":1,"id":"af39f34f43","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"The claim that 'a technical standard is never only technical' is supported in the passage mainly by:","a":"The example of factory clocks being used to measure and discipline workers","w":["The fact that neighboring towns' clocks once disagreed by several minutes","The danger of collisions on single-track lines with mixed timetables","The way phones now update themselves automatically across time zones"],"e":"The factory example shows a timekeeping standard shaping power relationships, not just coordination.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p7efa8aa5","pi":2,"id":"9ea3d2acd1","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C3","q":"A company drops fixed shifts and instead tracks employees' online activity minute by minute, at any hour. The author would most likely view this as:","a":"A new version of the control over workers' time that factory clocks once provided","w":["A return to the older, task-based rhythms of preindustrial work","A purely technical change that has no real social consequences","Proof that new technology always gives workers more free time"],"e":"The final paragraph raises the question of whether new technology 'claims more' of people's time. Constant tracking extends the factory clock's discipline beyond fixed hours.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p7efa8aa5","pi":3,"id":"6e1f282dcb","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C1","q":"Which statement best summarizes the author's argument?","a":"Ruins deserve preserving as ruins because they offer meaning a complete building can't","w":["Ruins should be restored so that they can be used again by their communities","Viollet-le-Duc was right that restoration can return a building to completeness","Ruins are valuable only as physical evidence of how buildings were once used"],"e":"The last paragraph states the thesis. The fourth paragraph adds that the ruin's appeal is 'not only documentary,' which rules out the last option.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p555c93fe","pi":0,"id":"914793266c","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C2","q":"How does the passage describe the relationship between modern heritage practice and Ruskin's views?","a":"It reaches the same conclusion as Ruskin but for different reasons","w":["It rejects Ruskin's conclusion and favors full restoration instead","It follows Ruskin's reasoning about reverence exactly","It ignores the debate between Ruskin and Viollet-le-Duc entirely"],"e":"Modern practice 'largely sided with Ruskin, though for reasons he might not recognize': evidence rather than reverence.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p555c93fe","pi":1,"id":"0928a83982","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C1","q":"In the third paragraph, 'erase the archive' most nearly means:","a":"Destroy the physical traces that record a building's history","w":["Throw away the written records kept about a building","Close a historic building to visiting researchers","Lose the original architect's drawings and plans"],"e":"The 'archive' is the worn stone and mismatched repairs, the physical record of use described in the same paragraph.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p555c93fe","pi":2,"id":"6e097f9c93","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C3","q":"A town debates rebuilding the roof of its ruined abbey so the space can host concerts. The author would most likely:","a":"Prefer stabilizing the ruin, since rebuilding would erase its evidence and its meaning","w":["Support rebuilding, because buildings exist to be used and concerts would use it","Support rebuilding, as long as the new roof closely matches the original design","Have no preference, since both stabilizing and rebuilding preserve the site"],"e":"The author values the ruin's physical record and its 'this, too, will end' meaning, both of which a new roof would take away.","t":"Humanities","s":null,"p":"p555c93fe","pi":3,"id":"d21efa02e4","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Humanities"},{"c":"C1","q":"According to the passage, Mauss's main claim was that gifts:","a":"Create obligations that build social ties","w":["Are disguised forms of selfish calculation","Are less efficient than market exchange","Should always be returned at equal value"],"e":"The first paragraph says Mauss's point 'was not that people are secretly calculating,' but that gifts weave bonds through obligation.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p28efd29e","pi":0,"id":"8610f087fa","L":"Foundations of Comprehension","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"The example of buying bread mainly serves to:","a":"Contrast a complete transaction with a gift that is left deliberately incomplete","w":["Show that market exchange is morally inferior to exchanging gifts","Illustrate each of Mauss's three obligations with a familiar case","Explain why bakers often give small gifts to their regular customers"],"e":"The bread purchase ends with no further obligation, which highlights how a gift leaves an open debt where 'the relationship lives.'","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p28efd29e","pi":1,"id":"c9f56bd59d","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C2","q":"How does the author respond to critics who see gift economies as hidden systems of power?","a":"Partly agrees, but says the criticism overlooks that all relationships involve obligations","w":["Fully agrees with them and concludes that people should avoid exchanging gifts","Rejects their criticism completely as having no truth to it whatsoever","Argues that market exchange creates far more power imbalances than gifts do"],"e":"The author grants 'there is truth in this' and then says the criticism 'proves less than it seems,' since the alternative to obligation is isolation.","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p28efd29e","pi":2,"id":"da2ef9c81c","L":"Reasoning Within the Text","T":"Social sciences"},{"c":"C3","q":"A friend thanks you for a birthday present by immediately handing you cash equal to its price. Based on the passage, this would most likely:","a":"Turn the gift into a trade and weaken the bond it was meant to build","w":["Strengthen the friendship by settling the debt fairly and quickly","Show that your friend values the relationship more than the gift","Have no effect on the friendship, since the values exchanged are equal"],"e":"The third paragraph gives nearly this exact example: exact, quick repayment 'turns the gift into a trade and quietly ends the relationship.'","t":"Social sciences","s":null,"p":"p28efd29e","pi":3,"id":"eadb2d1227","L":"Reasoning Beyond the Text","T":"Social sciences"}],"biobio":[{"c":"1A","t":"Amino acids","q":"Which amino acid is achiral?","a":"Glycine","w":["Alanine","Cysteine","Proline"],"e":"Glycine's side chain is a hydrogen, so its alpha carbon has two identical groups and is not a stereocenter.","id":"526847e1f3","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Amino acids","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Amino acids","q":"Which amino acid has a side chain that is positively charged at physiological pH (7.4)?","a":"Lysine","w":["Aspartate","Serine","Histidine"],"e":"Lysine's side-chain amine (pKa ≈ 10.5) is protonated at pH 7.4. Histidine's side chain (pKa ≈ 6) is mostly neutral at 7.4, and aspartate is negative.","id":"dfd8daee95","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Amino acids","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Amino acids","q":"An amino acid has pKa values of 2.3 (carboxyl) and 9.7 (amino) and no ionizable side chain. What is its isoelectric point?","a":"6.0","w":["4.6","7.4","12.0"],"e":"For an amino acid with no charged side chain, pI is the average of the two pKa values: (2.3 + 9.7) / 2 = 6.0.","id":"6b3dd35fc5","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Amino acids","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Amino acids","q":"Which amino acid can form disulfide bonds?","a":"Cysteine","w":["Methionine","Serine","Tyrosine"],"e":"Two cysteine thiol (–SH) groups can be oxidized to form a covalent S–S bridge. Methionine contains sulfur but as a thioether, which can't form disulfides.","id":"f6a921adc8","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Amino acids","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Amino acids","q":"Which amino acid is most likely to break an α-helix?","a":"Proline","w":["Alanine","Leucine","Glutamate"],"e":"Proline's side chain loops back to its own backbone nitrogen, which makes it rigid and leaves no N–H to donate a hydrogen bond.","id":"989840d5b5","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Amino acids","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein structure","q":"The α-helices and β-pleated sheets of a protein are held together mainly by:","a":"Hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl and amide groups","w":["Disulfide bonds between cysteine side chains on nearby strands","Hydrophobic interactions between nonpolar side chains in the core","Ionic bonds between oppositely charged side chains along the chain"],"e":"Secondary structure comes from backbone hydrogen bonding. Side-chain interactions shape tertiary structure.","id":"4bc22b8925","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein structure","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein structure","q":"What drives a globular protein to fold with its hydrophobic side chains buried in the core?","a":"An increase in the entropy of the surrounding water","w":["Covalent bonds between hydrophobic side chains","A decrease in the protein's enthalpy only","The energy released by ATP hydrolysis"],"e":"Exposed nonpolar groups force water into ordered cages. Burying them frees those water molecules, raising entropy (the hydrophobic effect).","id":"61bf541c96","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein structure","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein structure","q":"Hemoglobin is made of four polypeptide subunits. The arrangement of these subunits is its:","a":"Quaternary structure","w":["Tertiary structure","Secondary structure","Primary structure"],"e":"Quaternary structure describes how multiple chains fit together. Single-chain proteins like myoglobin have no quaternary structure.","id":"b59b8f017c","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein structure","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein structure","q":"Urea and heat denature a protein. Which level of structure stays intact?","a":"Primary structure","w":["Secondary structure","Tertiary structure","Quaternary structure"],"e":"Denaturation disrupts noncovalent interactions but leaves the peptide bonds, and therefore the amino acid sequence, intact.","id":"22004a6101","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein structure","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"A competitive inhibitor affects an enzyme's kinetics by:","a":"Raising the apparent Km while leaving Vmax unchanged","w":["Lowering Vmax while leaving the apparent Km unchanged","Lowering both the apparent Km and Vmax by the same factor","Raising Vmax while lowering the apparent Km of the enzyme"],"e":"The inhibitor competes for the active site, so more substrate is needed for half-maximal speed. Enough substrate outcompetes it, so Vmax is still reachable.","id":"6d238aa951","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"A noncompetitive inhibitor binds equally well to the free enzyme and to the enzyme–substrate complex. It will:","a":"Lower Vmax without changing Km","w":["Raise Km without changing Vmax","Raise both Km and Vmax","Lower Km without changing Vmax"],"e":"It removes active enzyme from play regardless of substrate, so Vmax falls. Because it doesn't change substrate binding, Km is unchanged.","id":"ec0d9c52c1","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"On a Lineweaver–Burk (double-reciprocal) plot, the y-intercept equals:","a":"1/Vmax","w":["−1/Km","Km/Vmax","Vmax"],"e":"The plot is 1/v = (Km/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax. The x-intercept is −1/Km and the slope is Km/Vmax.","id":"5b71bfbadb","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"An enzyme has a lower Km for substrate X than for substrate Y. This means the enzyme:","a":"Has a higher apparent affinity for X","w":["Has a higher apparent affinity for Y","Works faster on X at all concentrations","Cannot bind Y"],"e":"Km is the substrate concentration giving half of Vmax. Reaching it at a lower concentration suggests tighter binding.","id":"88d4b67ff7","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"Which statement about enzymes is FALSE?","a":"They shift the equilibrium of the reactions they catalyze","w":["They lower the activation energy of the reactions they speed up","They are not consumed or permanently changed by the reaction","They can be regulated by allosteric effectors and phosphorylation"],"e":"Enzymes speed up both directions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but the equilibrium constant is unchanged.","id":"aa221206fb","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"An allosteric enzyme shows a sigmoidal plot of velocity against substrate concentration. This shape indicates:","a":"Cooperative substrate binding among subunits","w":["Competitive inhibition by the product of the reaction","Simple Michaelis–Menten kinetics with a single binding site","That each of the enzyme's subunits binds substrate independently"],"e":"Binding at one site increases affinity at the others, just as with oxygen binding to hemoglobin.","id":"b7e3929131","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","q":"Zymogens like pepsinogen and trypsinogen are made inactive mainly so that they:","a":"Do not digest the cells that make them","w":["Can cross the cell membrane more easily","Are more stable at low pH","Can bind more substrate"],"e":"These proteases are activated only after secretion. For example, enteropeptidase in the small intestine converts trypsinogen to trypsin.","id":"37f109f0d6","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein function","q":"Compared with hemoglobin, myoglobin's oxygen dissociation curve is:","a":"Hyperbolic, with higher affinity for O₂ at low partial pressures","w":["Sigmoidal, with lower affinity for O₂ at low partial pressures","Sigmoidal, with higher affinity for O₂ only at high partial pressures","Hyperbolic, with lower affinity for O₂ at every partial pressure"],"e":"Myoglobin has one subunit and no cooperativity. It binds O₂ tightly, so it can take oxygen from hemoglobin in muscle.","id":"f3a691fadc","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein function","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein function","q":"Which change shifts hemoglobin's O₂ dissociation curve to the right?","a":"Higher CO₂, lower pH and higher temperature","w":["Lower CO₂, higher pH and lower temperature","Lower 2,3-BPG levels","Fetal hemoglobin replacing adult hemoglobin"],"e":"A right shift means lower affinity, which unloads more O₂ in active tissues. CO₂, H⁺ (the Bohr effect), heat and 2,3-BPG all cause it.","id":"5c553b5ce5","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein function","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1A","t":"Protein function","q":"G protein–coupled receptors are activated when a ligand binds and the associated G protein:","a":"Exchanges GDP for GTP on its α subunit","w":["Phosphorylates tyrosines on the receptor","Opens an ion channel directly","Hydrolyzes ATP to cAMP"],"e":"The active Gα–GTP dissociates and regulates targets such as adenylyl cyclase, which then converts ATP to cAMP.","id":"8eb22970a8","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Protein function","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"DNA structure and replication","q":"Which base pair is held together by three hydrogen bonds?","a":"Guanine–cytosine","w":["Adenine–thymine","Adenine–uracil","Guanine–thymine"],"e":"G–C pairs form three hydrogen bonds and A–T pairs form two, so GC-rich DNA melts at a higher temperature.","id":"ea552abf4b","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"DNA structure and replication","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"DNA structure and replication","q":"DNA polymerase adds new nucleotides only to which end of a growing strand?","a":"The 3′ end","w":["The 5′ end","Either end","The end closest to the origin"],"e":"Polymerases need a free 3′-OH to attack the incoming nucleotide, so synthesis runs 5′ → 3′.","id":"97bbf67703","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"DNA structure and replication","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"DNA structure and replication","q":"Why are Okazaki fragments formed during DNA replication?","a":"Polymerase only works 5′ → 3′, so the lagging strand is made in pieces","w":["Topoisomerase repeatedly nicks the leading strand as the fork advances","Helicase can only unwind short stretches, so both strands are copied in pieces","Primase lays down primers on both strands, which blocks continuous synthesis"],"e":"The lagging strand runs the opposite way from fork movement, so it is built in short pieces that ligase later joins.","id":"49f3918333","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"DNA structure and replication","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"DNA structure and replication","q":"Which enzyme relieves the supercoiling that builds up ahead of the replication fork?","a":"Topoisomerase","w":["Helicase","Primase","DNA ligase"],"e":"Helicase unwinds the double helix, which overwinds the DNA ahead of it. Topoisomerases cut and rejoin strands to release that strain.","id":"ec6697e45e","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"DNA structure and replication","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"DNA structure and replication","q":"DNA replication is described as semiconservative because:","a":"Each new double helix contains one original strand and one new strand","w":["Half of the genome is copied during each round of the cell cycle","Only one of the two strands is copied, and the other is conserved","Both original strands end up together in the same daughter cell"],"e":"This was shown by the Meselson–Stahl experiment using heavy and light nitrogen isotopes.","id":"e8436eefd2","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"DNA structure and replication","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Transcription","q":"In eukaryotes, which modification is NOT part of processing pre-mRNA into mature mRNA?","a":"Adding a Shine–Dalgarno sequence","w":["Adding a 5′ cap","Adding a poly-A tail","Removing introns by splicing"],"e":"The Shine–Dalgarno sequence is a prokaryotic ribosome-binding site. Eukaryotic mRNA is capped, polyadenylated and spliced.","id":"aae61c0555","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Transcription","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Transcription","q":"Which eukaryotic RNA polymerase transcribes protein-coding genes into mRNA?","a":"RNA polymerase II","w":["RNA polymerase I","RNA polymerase III","DNA polymerase III"],"e":"Pol I makes most rRNA, Pol II makes mRNA (and some small RNAs), and Pol III makes tRNA and 5S rRNA.","id":"e7e64ec83d","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Transcription","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Transcription","q":"Alternative splicing allows one gene to:","a":"Encode several different proteins by including different exons","w":["Be transcribed by several different RNA polymerases","Be translated starting from several different start codons","Skip transcription and be translated directly from DNA"],"e":"By combining exons differently, a cell can make several protein variants (isoforms) from a single gene.","id":"b540caaf61","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Transcription","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Translation","q":"A tRNA anticodon reads 5′-CAU-3′. Which mRNA codon does it pair with?","a":"5′-AUG-3′","w":["5′-GUA-3′","5′-CAU-3′","5′-UAC-3′"],"e":"Pairing is antiparallel. Matching the anticodon's 3′ U with A, A with U and 5′ C with G gives the codon 5′-AUG-3′ (methionine, the start codon).","id":"1dd797a90c","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Translation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Translation","q":"Which is a stop codon?","a":"UAG","w":["AUG","UGG","GAU"],"e":"The three stop codons are UAA, UAG and UGA. AUG is the start codon, and UGG codes for tryptophan.","id":"30080f299a","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Translation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Translation","q":"A peptide bond forms when the growing chain is transferred onto the amino acid in which ribosomal site?","a":"The A site","w":["The P site","The E site","The small subunit's mRNA channel"],"e":"The incoming aminoacyl-tRNA enters the A site. Peptidyl transferase (rRNA in the large subunit) moves the chain from the P-site tRNA onto it.","id":"d8d2aff559","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Translation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Translation","q":"The genetic code is called degenerate because:","a":"Most amino acids are specified by more than one codon","w":["Some codons code for more than one amino acid in different cells","Codons can overlap with each other within a single reading frame","It differs widely between species, so each needs its own codon table"],"e":"There are 61 sense codons for 20 amino acids. The code is redundant but unambiguous: each codon specifies only one amino acid.","id":"49097ae1c0","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Translation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Mutations","q":"A single base deletion in the middle of a gene's coding sequence most likely causes:","a":"A frameshift that changes all downstream codons","w":["A silent mutation","A single amino acid substitution","No change, because of the degenerate code"],"e":"Deleting a number of bases that isn't a multiple of 3 shifts the reading frame and often creates an early stop codon.","id":"636aaf2fd0","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Mutations","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Mutations","q":"A point mutation changes a codon for an amino acid into a stop codon. This is called a:","a":"Nonsense mutation","w":["Missense mutation","Silent mutation","Frameshift mutation"],"e":"Nonsense mutations end translation early and usually produce a shortened, nonfunctional protein.","id":"7db0873e8e","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Mutations","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Gene regulation","q":"In the lac operon, when lactose is present and glucose is absent:","a":"The repressor is released, CAP–cAMP binds, and transcription is high","w":["The repressor stays bound to the operator, so transcription remains off","CAP–cAMP is released from the promoter, so transcription stays low","The repressor and CAP–cAMP both bind and block RNA polymerase together"],"e":"Allolactose pulls the repressor off the operator. Low glucose raises cAMP, and CAP–cAMP helps RNA polymerase bind for strong transcription.","id":"609aab5de1","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Gene regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Gene regulation","q":"Histone acetylation generally:","a":"Loosens chromatin and increases transcription","w":["Tightens chromatin and silences genes","Adds methyl groups to cytosines in DNA","Removes histones from DNA permanently"],"e":"Acetyl groups neutralize lysines' positive charges, which weakens histone binding to negatively charged DNA.","id":"33e6f3277e","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Gene regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Gene regulation","q":"In eukaryotes, DNA methylation at CpG sites in a promoter usually:","a":"Represses transcription of that gene","w":["Activates transcription of that gene","Causes a frameshift mutation","Speeds up DNA replication"],"e":"Methylated promoters attract proteins that compact chromatin and block transcription factors. This is a common way to silence genes epigenetically.","id":"e596b24ef1","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Gene regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Biotechnology","q":"In PCR, the annealing step happens at a moderate temperature (about 55 °C) so that:","a":"Primers can bind to complementary sequences on the single strands","w":["The hydrogen bonds between the two template strands break apart","Taq polymerase can extend new strands at its optimal temperature","Newly made fragments can be sealed together by heat-stable DNA ligase"],"e":"PCR cycles through denaturation (about 95 °C), annealing (about 50–65 °C) and extension (about 72 °C).","id":"a528e4a457","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Biotechnology","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1B","t":"Biotechnology","q":"Reverse transcriptase is used to make cDNA libraries because it:","a":"Builds DNA from an mRNA template, giving sequences without introns","w":["Copies genomic DNA into RNA, keeping the introns in place","Cuts genomic DNA at palindromic sites into gene-sized pieces","Joins mRNA molecules end to end into a single long strand"],"e":"cDNA comes from processed mRNA, so it lacks introns. That lets eukaryotic genes be expressed in bacteria, which can't splice.","id":"4340607df2","L":"1B · Gene to protein","T":"Biotechnology","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","q":"Two heterozygous parents (Aa × Aa) have a child. What is the chance the child shows the recessive phenotype?","a":"1/4","w":["1/2","3/4","1/16"],"e":"The cross gives 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa. Only aa shows the recessive trait.","id":"9c9035fc0e","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","q":"In an AaBb × AaBb cross with independent assortment, what fraction of offspring show both recessive phenotypes?","a":"1/16","w":["1/4","9/16","3/16"],"e":"(1/4 for aa) × (1/4 for bb) = 1/16. The classic dihybrid ratio is 9:3:3:1.","id":"8517817a5f","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","q":"A mother who carries an X-linked recessive disorder has a son with an unaffected father. What is the probability the son is affected?","a":"1/2","w":["1/4","0","1"],"e":"Sons get their only X from the mother. She passes the affected X half the time.","id":"253faeae18","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","q":"A cross between red and white snapdragons gives all pink offspring. This is an example of:","a":"Incomplete dominance","w":["Codominance","Epistasis","Complete dominance"],"e":"The heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype. In codominance, both traits show fully, as in AB blood type.","id":"d08c7e8053","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","q":"A person with type AB blood shows both A and B antigens on their red blood cells. This is an example of:","a":"Codominance","w":["Incomplete dominance","Pleiotropy","Polygenic inheritance"],"e":"Both alleles are fully expressed in the heterozygote.","id":"3fd1165415","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Meiosis and diversity","q":"Crossing over between homologous chromosomes happens during:","a":"Prophase I of meiosis","w":["Metaphase II of meiosis","Anaphase of mitosis","S phase of interphase"],"e":"Homologs pair into tetrads (synapsis) in prophase I and swap segments at chiasmata.","id":"519a9baf00","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Meiosis and diversity","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Meiosis and diversity","q":"Homologous chromosomes separate during:","a":"Anaphase I","w":["Anaphase II","Metaphase I","Telophase II"],"e":"Meiosis I separates homologs (reductional division). Meiosis II separates sister chromatids, much like mitosis.","id":"3e350fe0f1","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Meiosis and diversity","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Meiosis and diversity","q":"Nondisjunction during meiosis can produce trisomy 21. Nondisjunction means:","a":"Chromosomes or chromatids fail to separate properly","w":["A chromosome segment is reversed end to end","Two non-homologous chromosomes swap segments","A gene is duplicated on the same chromosome"],"e":"If a pair fails to separate, one gamete gets two copies and another gets none. Fertilization then gives a trisomy or monosomy.","id":"e869df80d0","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Meiosis and diversity","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Meiosis and diversity","q":"Two genes are 20 map units apart. What percentage of offspring from a test cross are expected to be recombinant?","a":"20%","w":["40%","10%","80%"],"e":"One map unit (centimorgan) equals 1% recombination frequency.","id":"de88f56e12","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Meiosis and diversity","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Population genetics","q":"In a population in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, the recessive allele frequency is 0.3. What fraction of people are heterozygous?","a":"0.42","w":["0.09","0.49","0.21"],"e":"p = 0.7 and q = 0.3. Heterozygotes are 2pq = 2 × 0.7 × 0.3 = 0.42.","id":"4d6e54890e","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Population genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Population genetics","q":"A recessive disorder affects 1 in 10,000 people in a population in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium. About what fraction are carriers?","a":"About 1 in 50","w":["About 1 in 100","About 1 in 5,000","About 1 in 10"],"e":"q² = 1/10,000, so q = 0.01 and p ≈ 0.99. Carriers = 2pq ≈ 0.02, or about 1 in 50.","id":"480468e4a1","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Population genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Population genetics","q":"Which condition is NOT required for Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium?","a":"Small population size","w":["Random mating","No mutation","No migration"],"e":"Hardy–Weinberg requires a very large population. In small populations, genetic drift changes allele frequencies by chance.","id":"16fa348be6","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Population genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Evolution","q":"A small group colonizes an island, and its allele frequencies differ from the mainland population by chance. This is called:","a":"The founder effect","w":["Natural selection","Gene flow","Balancing selection"],"e":"The founder effect is a kind of genetic drift: a small sample from a larger population doesn't represent its genetic diversity.","id":"7468dc3396","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Evolution","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1C","t":"Evolution","q":"The sickle-cell allele stays common in areas with malaria because heterozygotes resist malaria. This is an example of:","a":"Heterozygote advantage","w":["Directional selection for the sickle allele","Genetic drift","Inbreeding depression"],"e":"Heterozygotes have higher fitness than either homozygote, which keeps both alleles in the population (balancing selection).","id":"0a6a9c6976","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Evolution","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Glycolysis","q":"What is the net ATP yield of glycolysis per glucose molecule?","a":"2 ATP","w":["4 ATP","36 ATP","0 ATP"],"e":"Glycolysis uses 2 ATP in the investment phase and makes 4 in the payoff phase. It also produces 2 NADH and 2 pyruvate.","id":"916d86ceab","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Glycolysis","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Glycolysis","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the main regulated, committed step of glycolysis?","a":"Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1)","w":["Hexokinase","Pyruvate kinase","Aldolase"],"e":"PFK-1 converts fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. It is inhibited by ATP and citrate and activated by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate.","id":"def01bd9be","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Glycolysis","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Glycolysis","q":"During intense exercise, muscle converts pyruvate to lactate mainly to:","a":"Regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue","w":["Produce extra ATP directly","Store glucose for later","Send pyruvate into the mitochondria"],"e":"Without enough oxygen, the electron transport chain can't reoxidize NADH. Lactate dehydrogenase does it instead, keeping glycolysis going.","id":"b45f71a217","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Glycolysis","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Citric acid cycle","q":"Where does the citric acid cycle take place in eukaryotic cells?","a":"The mitochondrial matrix","w":["The cytosol","The intermembrane space","The inner mitochondrial membrane"],"e":"The only exception is succinate dehydrogenase, which is embedded in the inner membrane and doubles as Complex II of the electron transport chain.","id":"973311c034","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Citric acid cycle","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Citric acid cycle","q":"One turn of the citric acid cycle produces:","a":"3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP (or ATP) and 2 CO₂","w":["2 NADH, 2 FADH₂, 2 ATP (or GTP) and 1 CO₂","1 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 3 ATP (or GTP) and 2 CO₂","3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 3 ATP (or GTP) and no CO₂"],"e":"Each glucose gives 2 acetyl-CoA, so the cycle turns twice per glucose.","id":"7137a07e56","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Citric acid cycle","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Citric acid cycle","q":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate into:","a":"Acetyl-CoA, CO₂ and NADH","w":["Lactate and NAD⁺","Oxaloacetate and ATP","Glucose-6-phosphate"],"e":"This link reaction connects glycolysis to the citric acid cycle and happens in the mitochondrial matrix.","id":"a1afb683da","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Citric acid cycle","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","q":"What is the final electron acceptor of the electron transport chain?","a":"Oxygen (O₂)","w":["NAD⁺","FAD","Cytochrome c"],"e":"Complex IV passes electrons to O₂, which picks up protons to form water.","id":"19acb4e9ed","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","q":"ATP synthase makes ATP using energy from:","a":"Protons flowing from the intermembrane space back into the matrix","w":["Electrons passing directly through ATP synthase from NADH to oxygen","Protons being pumped from the matrix out into the intermembrane space","GTP hydrolysis from substrate-level phosphorylation in the cycle"],"e":"The electron transport chain pumps H⁺ out into the intermembrane space. Their return through ATP synthase drives its rotation (chemiosmosis).","id":"722d9a6ecb","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","q":"An uncoupler such as 2,4-dinitrophenol makes the inner mitochondrial membrane leaky to protons. What happens?","a":"Oxygen use rises, ATP production falls and more energy is lost as heat","w":["Oxygen use stops, ATP production rises and less heat is released","Oxygen use falls, ATP production falls and less heat is released","Oxygen use rises, ATP production rises and the gradient grows larger"],"e":"The proton gradient leaks away, so electron transport runs fast to try to rebuild it, but ATP synthase is bypassed. Brown fat uses a natural uncoupler (thermogenin) to make heat.","id":"814988aaf1","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","q":"Cyanide is lethal because it:","a":"Blocks Complex IV, stopping electron flow to oxygen","w":["Blocks hexokinase, which stops glycolysis in every cell","Makes the inner membrane leaky to protons, wasting the gradient","Binds the ATP synthase rotor and stops it from turning"],"e":"With Complex IV blocked, the whole chain backs up, the proton gradient collapses and aerobic ATP production stops.","id":"fc4e086147","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Metabolic regulation","q":"In the fasting state, which hormone stimulates the liver to break down glycogen and make new glucose?","a":"Glucagon","w":["Insulin","Aldosterone","Calcitonin"],"e":"Glucagon from pancreatic α-cells raises blood glucose through glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Insulin does the opposite.","id":"3448b9fe4d","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Metabolic regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Metabolic regulation","q":"Gluconeogenesis bypasses three irreversible steps of glycolysis. Which enzyme bypasses pyruvate kinase together with PEP carboxykinase?","a":"Pyruvate carboxylase","w":["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase","Phosphofructokinase-1"],"e":"Pyruvate carboxylase turns pyruvate into oxaloacetate in the mitochondria. PEPCK then converts oxaloacetate into phosphoenolpyruvate.","id":"6da73ccbc0","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Metabolic regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Metabolic regulation","q":"Fatty acid β-oxidation takes place in the:","a":"Mitochondrial matrix","w":["Cytosol","Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Nucleus"],"e":"Long-chain fatty acids enter the mitochondria via the carnitine shuttle. Each round of β-oxidation removes two carbons as acetyl-CoA. Fatty acid synthesis happens in the cytosol.","id":"60a629e689","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Metabolic regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Metabolic regulation","q":"During prolonged starvation, the liver produces ketone bodies mainly to:","a":"Provide an alternate fuel for the brain and other tissues","w":["Store excess glucose as a compact fuel reserve","Convert fatty acids back into new glucose molecules","Raise blood pH to counter acid from muscle breakdown"],"e":"With glucose scarce, acetyl-CoA from fat breakdown becomes ketone bodies (acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate), which the brain can adapt to use.","id":"6a5c56101e","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Metabolic regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Metabolic regulation","q":"The pentose phosphate pathway's main products are:","a":"NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate","w":["ATP and pyruvate","NADH and acetyl-CoA","FADH₂ and glucose"],"e":"NADPH supports biosynthesis and protects against oxidative damage. Ribose-5-phosphate is used to make nucleotides.","id":"40ef961051","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Metabolic regulation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"1D","t":"Bioenergetics","q":"Under cellular conditions, ATP hydrolysis can drive an unfavorable reaction because:","a":"Coupling the two reactions makes the overall ΔG negative","w":["ATP lowers the activation energy of the unfavorable reaction","ATP hydrolysis has a positive ΔG","The unfavorable reaction becomes favorable at high temperature"],"e":"ΔG values add for coupled reactions. ATP hydrolysis releases enough free energy to make the overall process spontaneous.","id":"3a8edc25b5","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Bioenergetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"c":"2A","t":"Membranes and transport","q":"Which molecule crosses a phospholipid bilayer most easily by simple diffusion?","a":"O₂","w":["Glucose","Na⁺","Water-soluble proteins"],"e":"Small, nonpolar molecules like O₂ and CO₂ pass straight through the hydrophobic core. Ions and large polar molecules need channels or carriers.","id":"f568c7c3e2","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Membranes and transport","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Membranes and transport","q":"The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase moves ions in which way for each ATP it uses?","a":"3 Na⁺ out of the cell and 2 K⁺ into the cell","w":["2 Na⁺ out of the cell and 3 K⁺ into the cell","3 Na⁺ into the cell and 2 K⁺ out of the cell","3 K⁺ out of the cell and 2 Na⁺ into the cell"],"e":"This is primary active transport. Because it moves net positive charge out, it also contributes slightly to the negative resting membrane potential.","id":"5ae2acb0cb","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Membranes and transport","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Membranes and transport","q":"The SGLT transporter brings glucose into intestinal cells against its gradient by coupling it to Na⁺ moving down its gradient. This is:","a":"Secondary active transport","w":["Primary active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion"],"e":"The transporter uses no ATP itself. It relies on the Na⁺ gradient that the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase builds with ATP.","id":"f57afe93eb","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Membranes and transport","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Membranes and transport","q":"Adding cholesterol to a membrane at body temperature generally:","a":"Reduces fluidity by restricting phospholipid movement","w":["Greatly increases fluidity by spreading phospholipids apart","Makes the membrane far more permeable to small ions","Has no effect, because cholesterol stays outside the bilayer"],"e":"Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer: it stiffens membranes at high temperature and prevents tight packing at low temperature.","id":"51d0be8261","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Membranes and transport","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Membranes and transport","q":"Phospholipids with unsaturated fatty acid tails make membranes:","a":"More fluid, because the cis double bonds create kinks that prevent tight packing","w":["Less fluid, because double bonds hold neighboring tails more tightly together","More rigid, because the straight tails can line up and pack closely together","Impermeable to water, because double bonds repel the polar water molecules"],"e":"Kinked tails can't line up closely, so the membrane stays fluid at lower temperatures.","id":"a12d526632","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Membranes and transport","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Organelles","q":"Proteins destined for secretion are made on ribosomes attached to the:","a":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","w":["Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Mitochondrial membrane"],"e":"A signal sequence directs the ribosome to the rough ER. Proteins then move to the Golgi for modification and packaging into vesicles.","id":"1b38940576","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Organelles","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Organelles","q":"Which organelle detoxifies drugs in liver cells and makes steroids in gland cells?","a":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","w":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Lysosome","Nucleolus"],"e":"The smooth ER handles lipid synthesis, detoxification and calcium storage. It has no ribosomes.","id":"9504b9f2d0","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Organelles","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Organelles","q":"Peroxisomes are best known for:","a":"Breaking down very long-chain fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide","w":["Synthesizing and assembling ribosomal RNA subunits","Sorting and packaging proteins into secretory vesicles","Producing most of the cell's ATP by oxidative phosphorylation"],"e":"Peroxisomes run oxidation reactions that make H₂O₂, then use catalase to break it down.","id":"4056094848","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Organelles","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Organelles","q":"Lysosomal enzymes work best at about pH 5. What is the advantage of this?","a":"Enzymes that leak into the neutral cytosol are much less active","w":["The enzymes stay equally active anywhere they end up in the cell","The acidic interior lets the lysosome make its own ATP from protons","Low pH stops the enzymes from being made in the rough ER by mistake"],"e":"Acid hydrolases are largely inactive at the cytosol's pH of about 7.2, which protects the cell from digesting itself.","id":"09c647b512","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Organelles","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Cytoskeleton","q":"Which cytoskeletal element forms the mitotic spindle and the core of cilia and flagella?","a":"Microtubules","w":["Actin filaments","Intermediate filaments","Keratin fibers"],"e":"Microtubules are tubulin polymers. Eukaryotic cilia and flagella use a 9 + 2 arrangement driven by dynein.","id":"e0b9133f61","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Cytoskeleton","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Cell junctions","q":"Which cell junction prevents fluid from leaking between epithelial cells?","a":"Tight junctions","w":["Gap junctions","Desmosomes","Hemidesmosomes"],"e":"Tight junctions seal neighboring cells together. Gap junctions let small molecules pass between cells, and desmosomes anchor cells mechanically.","id":"3a773baff6","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Cell junctions","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2A","t":"Tissues","q":"Which of these is a connective tissue?","a":"Blood","w":["Skin epidermis","Cardiac muscle","The lining of the gut"],"e":"Connective tissues, including blood, bone, cartilage and adipose tissue, are cells surrounded by extracellular matrix. Epidermis and gut lining are epithelia.","id":"b9c227728b","L":"2A · Cells and tissues","T":"Tissues","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","q":"Which feature do prokaryotic cells have that eukaryotic cells lack?","a":"70S ribosomes in the cytoplasm","w":["A plasma membrane","DNA as genetic material","Ribosomes in general"],"e":"Prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes (30S + 50S). Eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, which is why some antibiotics can target bacteria selectively.","id":"85b8927e6a","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","q":"Gram-positive bacteria stain purple in a Gram stain because they have:","a":"A thick peptidoglycan cell wall that holds the crystal violet dye","w":["An outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide that traps the dye","No cell wall at all, so the dye stains the membrane directly","A thin peptidoglycan layer sandwiched between two cell membranes"],"e":"Gram-negative bacteria have a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane. They lose the dye and stain pink with the counterstain.","id":"3dc356ea4b","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","q":"Transfer of a plasmid between bacteria through a sex pilus is called:","a":"Conjugation","w":["Transformation","Transduction","Binary fission"],"e":"Transformation is taking up free DNA from the environment. Transduction is DNA transfer carried by a bacteriophage.","id":"a4afbbdaf2","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","q":"Bacteria reproduce asexually by:","a":"Binary fission","w":["Mitosis","Meiosis","Budding of spores"],"e":"The circular chromosome is copied and the cell divides in two. It's faster than mitosis and has no spindle.","id":"f61c56a1bb","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Viruses","q":"A retrovirus like HIV inserts its genes into the host genome by:","a":"Using reverse transcriptase to copy its RNA into DNA, then integrase to insert it","w":["Inserting its single-stranded RNA genome directly into a host chromosome","Having host ribosomes translate its RNA genome into a matching DNA copy","Using host RNA polymerase II to copy its RNA genome into double-stranded DNA"],"e":"Reverse transcription makes a DNA copy, and integrase splices it into host DNA as a provirus.","id":"c8c7853945","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Viruses","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Viruses","q":"In the lysogenic cycle, a bacteriophage:","a":"Integrates its genome into the host chromosome and is copied with it","w":["Immediately makes new phages and then bursts the host cell open","Degrades the host chromosome and reuses its nucleotides for copies","Stays outside the cell, attached to the cell wall, until conditions change"],"e":"The integrated prophage is passed to daughter cells. Stress can later trigger it to enter the lytic cycle.","id":"8eb1c3d9e1","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Viruses","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2B","t":"Viruses","q":"Which statement about viruses is true?","a":"They need a host cell's machinery to replicate","w":["They make ATP through their own metabolism","They all contain both DNA and RNA","They divide by binary fission"],"e":"Viruses have DNA or RNA (not both) in a protein coat, have no metabolism, and replicate only inside host cells.","id":"d0f28c0ef6","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Viruses","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Cell cycle","q":"DNA replication occurs during which phase of the cell cycle?","a":"S phase","w":["G₁ phase","G₂ phase","M phase"],"e":"Interphase is G₁ (growth), S (DNA synthesis) and G₂ (preparation for mitosis).","id":"b3377473ba","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Cell cycle","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Cell cycle","q":"During which phase of mitosis do sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles?","a":"Anaphase","w":["Metaphase","Prophase","Telophase"],"e":"In anaphase, cohesin is cleaved and kinetochore microtubules shorten, pulling chromatids apart.","id":"724aaccac1","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Cell cycle","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Cell cycle","q":"The tumor suppressor p53 helps prevent cancer mainly by:","a":"Halting the cell cycle or triggering apoptosis when DNA is damaged","w":["Pushing cells with damaged DNA past the G₁ checkpoint into S phase","Lengthening telomeres so damaged chromosomes stay stable after division","Speeding up mitosis so that cells with damaged DNA are diluted out"],"e":"p53 is mutated in many cancers. Without it, damaged cells keep dividing.","id":"a815b5c785","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Cell cycle","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Cell cycle","q":"Proto-oncogenes become oncogenes through mutations that typically:","a":"Increase their activity, promoting uncontrolled division","w":["Destroy their function, the way tumor suppressors are lost","Silence them completely so that division can't be controlled","Make them recessive, so both copies must be mutated first"],"e":"Oncogene mutations are usually gain-of-function and dominant: one mutated copy can push division. Tumor suppressors usually need both copies lost.","id":"d0197eb98e","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Cell cycle","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Development","q":"The three germ layers form during which stage of embryonic development?","a":"Gastrulation","w":["Cleavage","Neurulation","Implantation"],"e":"Gastrulation turns the blastula into a gastrula with ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm.","id":"62b7c74756","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Development","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Development","q":"Which germ layer gives rise to the nervous system and the epidermis?","a":"Ectoderm","w":["Mesoderm","Endoderm","Trophoblast"],"e":"Ectoderm forms the nervous system and skin epidermis. Mesoderm forms muscle, bone, blood and kidneys. Endoderm lines the gut and forms the lungs, liver and pancreas.","id":"9efcc24a8c","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Development","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Development","q":"Which germ layer gives rise to the heart, skeletal muscle and kidneys?","a":"Mesoderm","w":["Ectoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"],"e":"Mesoderm is the middle layer and forms most muscle, the circulatory system, bones and the urogenital system.","id":"ed08ebbcb1","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Development","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Development","q":"Apoptosis differs from necrosis in that apoptosis:","a":"Is an orderly, programmed process that does not trigger inflammation","w":["Causes cells to swell, burst and release their contents into the tissue","Only happens after a physical injury or a sudden loss of blood flow","Triggers a strong inflammatory response that recruits immune cells nearby"],"e":"Apoptotic cells shrink and break into membrane-bound blebs that are quietly engulfed. Necrotic cells burst and release contents that cause inflammation.","id":"75da520ef0","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Development","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"2C","t":"Stem cells","q":"Embryonic stem cells from the inner cell mass are described as:","a":"Pluripotent: they can become any cell of the body but not the placenta","w":["Totipotent: they can become any cell of the body including the placenta","Unipotent: they can become only one cell type, like a skin precursor","Multipotent: they can become cells of only one tissue family, like blood"],"e":"Only the zygote and very early blastomeres are totipotent. Adult stem cells, like blood stem cells, are usually multipotent.","id":"76e91fdd72","L":"2C · Cell division and differentiation","T":"Stem cells","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"c":"3A","t":"Neurons","q":"The depolarization phase of a neuron's action potential is caused by:","a":"Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels opening and Na⁺ rushing in","w":["Voltage-gated K⁺ channels opening and K⁺ rushing out","The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumping Na⁺ in","Cl⁻ entering through ligand-gated channels"],"e":"Na⁺ influx drives the membrane toward +30 mV or so. Repolarization follows as Na⁺ channels inactivate and K⁺ channels open.","id":"951dd6c8c2","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Neurons","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Neurons","q":"The absolute refractory period exists mainly because:","a":"Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are inactivated and cannot reopen yet","w":["Voltage-gated K⁺ channels have all closed and cannot reopen","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump stops working until the gradient recovers","The axon terminal has run out of neurotransmitter vesicles"],"e":"No stimulus, however strong, can fire another action potential until Na⁺ channels recover from inactivation. This also makes signals travel one way.","id":"1951d9be1f","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Neurons","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Neurons","q":"Myelin speeds up action potential conduction by:","a":"Insulating the axon so the signal jumps between nodes of Ranvier","w":["Adding many more Na⁺ channels along the entire length of the axon","Narrowing the axon so that current flows through it much faster","Releasing neurotransmitter at each node of Ranvier to relay the signal"],"e":"This is saltatory conduction. Schwann cells make myelin in the peripheral nervous system, and oligodendrocytes make it in the CNS.","id":"2a7468ae66","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Neurons","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Neurons","q":"Arrival of an action potential at the axon terminal triggers neurotransmitter release by opening:","a":"Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels","w":["Ligand-gated Na⁺ channels","Voltage-gated Cl⁻ channels","Gap junction channels"],"e":"Ca²⁺ influx causes synaptic vesicles to fuse with the membrane and release their contents by exocytosis.","id":"2e0d4011b7","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Neurons","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Neurons","q":"Which neurotransmitter is released at the vertebrate neuromuscular junction?","a":"Acetylcholine","w":["Dopamine","GABA","Norepinephrine"],"e":"Acetylcholine binds nicotinic receptors on skeletal muscle. Acetylcholinesterase quickly breaks it down to end the signal.","id":"96dc75dd67","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Neurons","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Nervous system organization","q":"Which division of the autonomic nervous system increases heart rate and dilates the pupils?","a":"Sympathetic","w":["Parasympathetic","Somatic","Enteric"],"e":"Sympathetic is fight-or-flight. Parasympathetic is rest-and-digest: it slows the heart, constricts pupils and promotes digestion.","id":"5e1fa89d01","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Nervous system organization","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Nervous system organization","q":"In a simple knee-jerk reflex, the sensory neuron synapses directly with:","a":"A motor neuron in the spinal cord","w":["An interneuron in the brain","The muscle fiber itself","A neuron in the cerebral cortex"],"e":"The patellar reflex is monosynaptic: the signal goes from muscle spindle to spinal cord to motor neuron, without involving the brain.","id":"2360b64101","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Nervous system organization","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","q":"Steroid hormones such as cortisol act on target cells mainly by:","a":"Crossing the membrane and binding intracellular receptors that alter transcription","w":["Binding receptors on the cell surface that activate adenylyl cyclase and cAMP","Binding ligand-gated ion channels that directly depolarize the cell membrane","Being taken in by endocytosis and broken down to release their amino acids"],"e":"Steroids are lipid-soluble. Their effects are slower but longer lasting than those of peptide hormones, which use surface receptors and second messengers.","id":"3fbf83c1de","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","q":"Which hormone is released by the posterior pituitary?","a":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","w":["Growth hormone","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Prolactin"],"e":"The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin, which are made in the hypothalamus. The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones, including GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH and prolactin.","id":"6b93c9e3bb","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","q":"High levels of thyroid hormone in the blood reduce TSH secretion. This is an example of:","a":"Negative feedback","w":["Positive feedback","Feedforward regulation","Paracrine signaling"],"e":"T₃ and T₄ inhibit TRH release from the hypothalamus and TSH release from the anterior pituitary, keeping levels steady.","id":"f493c729f0","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","q":"Which hormone lowers blood calcium?","a":"Calcitonin","w":["Parathyroid hormone","Calcitriol (active vitamin D)","Cortisol"],"e":"Calcitonin from the thyroid's C cells inhibits osteoclasts. Parathyroid hormone and calcitriol raise blood calcium.","id":"c3a9014d7b","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","q":"The adrenal medulla secretes:","a":"Epinephrine and norepinephrine","w":["Cortisol and aldosterone","Insulin and glucagon","T₃ and T₄"],"e":"The medulla acts as part of the sympathetic nervous system. The adrenal cortex makes steroids such as cortisol and aldosterone.","id":"054abbcb3a","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Cardiovascular","q":"Oxygenated blood returning from the lungs enters which chamber of the heart first?","a":"Left atrium","w":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle"],"e":"Pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood to the left atrium. It then passes to the left ventricle and out the aorta.","id":"7e027bb320","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Cardiovascular","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Cardiovascular","q":"Where is the heart's normal pacemaker located?","a":"The sinoatrial (SA) node","w":["The atrioventricular (AV) node","The bundle of His","The Purkinje fibers"],"e":"The SA node in the right atrium fires fastest and sets the rhythm. The AV node delays the signal so the atria finish contracting before the ventricles do.","id":"ae4aef1135","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Cardiovascular","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Cardiovascular","q":"Cardiac output equals:","a":"Heart rate × stroke volume","w":["Stroke volume ÷ heart rate","Blood pressure × heart rate","End-diastolic volume − heart rate"],"e":"At rest, about 70 beats/min × 70 mL/beat ≈ 5 L/min.","id":"a1b9d7b4ff","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Cardiovascular","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Cardiovascular","q":"Which type of blood vessel has the greatest total cross-sectional area?","a":"Capillaries","w":["Aorta","Arteries","Veins"],"e":"Capillaries are individually tiny, but there are billions. Their huge combined area means blood moves slowest there, giving time for exchange.","id":"500b783212","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Cardiovascular","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Respiratory","q":"Gas exchange in the lungs takes place across the walls of the:","a":"Alveoli","w":["Bronchi","Trachea","Bronchioles"],"e":"Alveoli have thin walls surrounded by capillaries. The airways before them form the conducting zone.","id":"1a6c19b770","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Respiratory","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Respiratory","q":"Surfactant in the alveoli helps breathing by:","a":"Lowering surface tension so alveoli don't collapse","w":["Raising surface tension to keep alveoli open","Killing bacteria in the airways","Transporting O₂ across the alveolar wall"],"e":"Premature babies may lack enough surfactant, which leads to respiratory distress syndrome.","id":"a506028d99","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Respiratory","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Respiratory","q":"Most CO₂ is carried in the blood as:","a":"Bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻) in plasma","w":["CO₂ dissolved in plasma","CO₂ bound to the heme iron of hemoglobin","Carbonic acid inside platelets"],"e":"About 70% travels as bicarbonate after carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells converts it. Some binds to globin as carbamino groups, not to the iron.","id":"f00e88d802","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Respiratory","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Respiratory","q":"During inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and:","a":"Chest volume increases, so pressure in the lungs drops below atmospheric pressure","w":["Chest volume decreases, so pressure in the lungs rises and air is pushed out","Chest volume increases, so pressure in the lungs rises above atmospheric","The pleural space fills with air, which pulls the lungs outward to inflate"],"e":"By Boyle's law, larger volume means lower pressure, so air flows in. Quiet expiration is mostly passive recoil.","id":"7d18961b3d","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Respiratory","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Renal","q":"Most reabsorption of filtered water, glucose and amino acids happens in the:","a":"Proximal convoluted tubule","w":["Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Glomerulus"],"e":"The PCT reabsorbs about two-thirds of filtered Na⁺ and water and nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids.","id":"fa456bf797","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Renal","q":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) increases water reabsorption by:","a":"Inserting aquaporins into the collecting duct","w":["Raising the glomerular filtration rate","Increasing Na⁺ excretion","Blocking aquaporins in the loop of Henle"],"e":"ADH lets the collecting duct absorb more water from the urine, which concentrates the urine and reduces water loss.","id":"388b6ecefc","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Renal","q":"Aldosterone acts on the distal nephron to:","a":"Increase Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion","w":["Decrease Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion","Increase glucose reabsorption","Block water reabsorption"],"e":"Water follows the reabsorbed sodium, which raises blood volume and blood pressure. Aldosterone release is triggered by angiotensin II and high blood K⁺.","id":"3358764128","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Renal","q":"The loop of Henle's main job is to:","a":"Build a salt gradient in the medulla so urine can be concentrated","w":["Filter blood plasma into Bowman's capsule under high pressure","Secrete renin into the blood when kidney blood pressure falls","Reabsorb nearly all of the filtered glucose and amino acids"],"e":"The descending limb lets water out, and the ascending limb pumps salt out without water. Together they form a countercurrent multiplier.","id":"69e5ba8994","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Digestive","q":"Which enzyme begins starch digestion in the mouth?","a":"Salivary amylase","w":["Pepsin","Lipase","Trypsin"],"e":"Amylase breaks starch into smaller sugars. Pepsin starts protein digestion in the stomach, and pancreatic enzymes finish most digestion in the small intestine.","id":"aac323806b","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Digestive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Digestive","q":"Bile helps digest fats by:","a":"Emulsifying fat into small droplets so lipase can reach more of it","w":["Hydrolyzing triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol","Breaking peptide bonds between amino acids in dietary protein","Neutralizing stomach acid with large amounts of bicarbonate ions"],"e":"Bile salts are amphipathic detergents. Bile is made in the liver and stored in the gallbladder, and it contains no digestive enzymes.","id":"09219e5fa7","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Digestive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Digestive","q":"Which hormone is released when acidic chyme enters the duodenum and stimulates the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate?","a":"Secretin","w":["Gastrin","Cholecystokinin (CCK)","Ghrelin"],"e":"Secretin raises pancreatic HCO₃⁻ to neutralize acid. CCK responds to fats and proteins by triggering enzyme release and gallbladder contraction.","id":"edb9c5b773","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Digestive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Digestive","q":"Where are most nutrients absorbed?","a":"The small intestine","w":["The stomach","The large intestine","The esophagus"],"e":"The small intestine's villi and microvilli give it a huge surface area. The large intestine mainly absorbs water and electrolytes.","id":"4c46c57e96","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Digestive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Immune","q":"Which cells produce antibodies?","a":"Plasma cells, which develop from B cells","w":["Helper T cells, after activation","Cytotoxic T cells, after activation","Neutrophils, at sites of infection"],"e":"B cells activated by antigen (often with T-helper help) become plasma cells that secrete antibodies, or memory B cells.","id":"e53b051422","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Immune","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Immune","q":"Cytotoxic (CD8⁺) T cells recognize antigens presented on:","a":"MHC class I molecules","w":["MHC class II molecules","Free antibodies","Complement proteins"],"e":"Nearly all nucleated cells show internal peptides on MHC I, so CD8⁺ T cells can find infected or cancerous cells. MHC II, found on antigen-presenting cells, is read by CD4⁺ helper T cells.","id":"f2d3991817","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Immune","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Immune","q":"Which is part of the innate immune system?","a":"Macrophages","w":["Memory B cells","Plasma cells","Cytotoxic T cells"],"e":"Innate defenses respond quickly and nonspecifically, and include skin, macrophages, neutrophils, NK cells and complement. B and T cells are adaptive.","id":"471cdb66de","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Immune","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Musculoskeletal","q":"In skeletal muscle, calcium triggers contraction by binding to:","a":"Troponin, which moves tropomyosin off myosin-binding sites on actin","w":["Myosin heads, which then attach directly to binding sites on actin","Tropomyosin, which is cut and released from the actin filament","Actin, which then hydrolyzes ATP to pull itself along the myosin"],"e":"With the binding sites exposed, myosin heads form cross-bridges and pull actin filaments toward the center of the sarcomere.","id":"79091d9de8","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Musculoskeletal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Musculoskeletal","q":"During skeletal muscle contraction, which part of the sarcomere stays the same length?","a":"The A band","w":["The H zone","The I band","The distance between Z lines"],"e":"The A band is the length of the thick myosin filaments, which don't shorten. The H zone, I band and sarcomere all shorten as filaments slide.","id":"57c123789d","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Musculoskeletal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Musculoskeletal","q":"In muscle contraction, ATP binding to the myosin head is needed to:","a":"Release myosin from actin","w":["Let calcium bind troponin","Pump sodium into the muscle cell","Lengthen the actin filament"],"e":"Without ATP, myosin stays locked to actin. That's why muscles stiffen in rigor mortis.","id":"f47d1935c3","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Musculoskeletal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Musculoskeletal","q":"Which cells break down bone matrix and release calcium into the blood?","a":"Osteoclasts","w":["Osteoblasts","Osteocytes","Chondrocytes"],"e":"Osteoclasts resorb bone. Osteoblasts build new bone and become osteocytes once surrounded by matrix.","id":"63c6e2935c","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Musculoskeletal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Reproductive","q":"The LH surge in the middle of the menstrual cycle directly triggers:","a":"Ovulation","w":["Menstruation","Implantation","Formation of new primary follicles"],"e":"Rising estrogen from the developing follicle switches to positive feedback and causes an LH surge. The leftover follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which makes progesterone.","id":"1c9304e1d1","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Reproductive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Reproductive","q":"Sperm mature and gain motility in the:","a":"Epididymis","w":["Seminiferous tubules","Vas deferens","Prostate gland"],"e":"Sperm are made in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, then mature in the epididymis.","id":"848e2a3497","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Reproductive","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"c":"3B","t":"Skin and homeostasis","q":"When body temperature rises, the skin helps cool the body by:","a":"Dilating blood vessels near the surface and producing sweat","w":["Constricting blood vessels and making the hairs stand up","Shivering","Reducing blood flow to the skin"],"e":"Vasodilation brings warm blood to the surface to lose heat, and evaporating sweat carries heat away.","id":"1470a5f6f1","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Skin and homeostasis","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Inhibitor X is most likely a:","a":"Competitive inhibitor","w":["Noncompetitive inhibitor","Uncompetitive inhibitor","Irreversible inhibitor that destroys the enzyme"],"e":"Km rises while Vmax stays the same, and high substrate overcomes the inhibition, which is the signature of competitive inhibition at the active site.","c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","s":null,"p":"p864f489b","pi":0,"id":"ba5fbf63de","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Inhibitor Y most likely:","a":"Binds equally well to the free enzyme and the enzyme–substrate complex","w":["Competes with the substrate for binding at the enzyme's active site","Binds only to the enzyme–substrate complex and never to free enzyme","Increases the enzyme's apparent affinity for its substrate at all levels"],"e":"Vmax drops while Km is unchanged, which is the pattern of a (pure) noncompetitive inhibitor.","c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","s":null,"p":"p864f489b","pi":1,"id":"2d825c1da6","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"With no inhibitor and [S] = 2.0 mM, what is the initial reaction rate?","a":"50 µmol/min","w":["100 µmol/min","25 µmol/min","200 µmol/min"],"e":"When [S] = Km, v = Vmax / 2 = 50 µmol/min.","c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","s":null,"p":"p864f489b","pi":2,"id":"a24803cc65","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Why does inhibitor Z lower both apparent Km and apparent Vmax?","a":"It binds only the ES complex, pulling binding toward ES while blocking product formation","w":["It competes with the substrate at the active site, so more substrate is needed to reach Vmax","It binds only the free enzyme, which blocks substrate entry and slows the overall reaction","It denatures the enzyme permanently, removing active enzyme and changing its binding site"],"e":"Uncompetitive inhibitors remove ES from the pathway. By Le Châtelier's principle, more E + S form ES (apparent Km falls), while fewer complexes can make product (Vmax falls).","c":"1A","t":"Enzymes","s":null,"p":"p864f489b","pi":3,"id":"09bf088f9f","L":"1A · Proteins and amino acids","T":"Enzymes","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Which mode of inheritance best fits the family data?","a":"Autosomal recessive","w":["Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant"],"e":"Unaffected parents having affected children points to a recessive trait. Equal rates in males and females points to an autosome. Marriage between cousins raises the chance both parents carry the same rare allele.","c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","s":null,"p":"p9cfe476d","pi":0,"id":"e44fc11221","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Two unaffected parents have one affected child. Their next child is born unaffected. What is the chance that this unaffected child is a carrier?","a":"2/3","w":["1/2","1/4","3/4"],"e":"Both parents must be carriers (Aa × Aa). Among unaffected children (AA, Aa, Aa), 2 out of 3 are carriers.","c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","s":null,"p":"p9cfe476d","pi":1,"id":"8e18999087","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Why does the 3-nucleotide deletion NOT cause a frameshift?","a":"Removing exactly one codon keeps the downstream reading frame in register","w":["The deletion falls in an intron that is later spliced out of the mRNA","The degenerate code lets the ribosome skip over the three missing bases","Chloride channel genes are translated without a fixed reading frame"],"e":"Deleting a multiple of three bases removes whole codons, so the amino acids after the deletion are still read correctly.","c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","s":null,"p":"p9cfe476d","pi":2,"id":"2da429057d","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"The misfolded channel is destroyed before reaching the cell membrane. Which cellular system is most likely responsible?","a":"Quality control in the ER, followed by degradation in the proteasome","w":["Lysosomal enzymes after the channel is inserted into the plasma membrane","Peroxisomal enzymes that oxidize misfolded proteins in the cytosol","Nuclear quality control that destroys the mRNA before it is exported"],"e":"Membrane proteins fold in the ER. Misfolded ones are held back, tagged with ubiquitin and sent to the proteasome.","c":"1C","t":"Mendelian genetics","s":null,"p":"p9cfe476d","pi":3,"id":"95e346ee22","L":"1C · Heredity and genetic diversity","T":"Mendelian genetics","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Which patient most likely has a problem with the anterior pituitary?","a":"Patient 3","w":["Patient 1","Patient 2","None of the patients"],"e":"Low TSH should cause low T₄, and low T₄ should normally push TSH up. Both being low points to a pituitary (or hypothalamic) failure to make TSH.","c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","s":null,"p":"p87b74dad","pi":0,"id":"38beedbcea","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Patient 2 has antibodies that bind and activate the TSH receptor. Which symptoms would you expect?","a":"Heat intolerance, weight loss and a rapid heart rate","w":["Cold intolerance, weight gain and constant fatigue","Low blood calcium, muscle cramps and tingling","High blood sugar, frequent urination and thirst"],"e":"The antibodies drive the thyroid regardless of TSH (as in Graves' disease). High T₄ raises metabolism, and negative feedback suppresses TSH.","c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","s":null,"p":"p87b74dad","pi":1,"id":"ddcfe9f5bb","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Long-term dietary iodine deficiency would most likely give lab results like those of:","a":"Patient 1","w":["Patient 2","Patient 3","No patient, because iodine doesn't affect T₄"],"e":"T₄ needs iodine. Without it, T₄ falls, and the loss of negative feedback raises TSH, which can enlarge the thyroid into a goiter.","c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","s":null,"p":"p87b74dad","pi":2,"id":"001cbb71ea","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"T₃ acts on target cells mainly by:","a":"Binding nuclear receptors that change gene transcription","w":["Binding a G protein–coupled receptor on the cell surface","Opening calcium channels in the membrane","Being converted into cAMP"],"e":"Although thyroid hormones are made from amino acids, they are lipophilic and act like steroids, through intracellular receptors.","c":"3A","t":"Endocrine system","s":null,"p":"p87b74dad","pi":3,"id":"f14a304488","L":"3A · Nervous and endocrine systems","T":"Endocrine system","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Taking up DNA from the surrounding solution, as the E. coli cells did here, is called:","a":"Transformation","w":["Conjugation","Transduction","Transposition"],"e":"Transformation is uptake of free DNA. Heat shock and competence treatments make the membrane more permeable to plasmids.","c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","s":null,"p":"p644e5969","pi":0,"id":"a1135c1259","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"q":"Which colonies most likely contain a plasmid with the inserted fragment?","a":"The white colonies","w":["The blue colonies","Both blue and white colonies equally","Neither, because the insert blocks ampicillin resistance"],"e":"An insert disrupts lacZ, so those cells can't cleave X-gal and stay white. Blue colonies have plasmids with an intact lacZ (no insert).","c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","s":null,"p":"p644e5969","pi":1,"id":"6cffe4c131","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"q":"What does the control plate show?","a":"That the ampicillin kills cells that lack the plasmid","w":["That the X-gal is being cleaved into a blue product","That the DNA fragment was inserted in the right orientation","That heat shock kills E. coli that take up no plasmid"],"e":"No colonies without plasmid means only plasmid-carrying cells survive on ampicillin, so colonies on the experimental plate must contain the plasmid.","c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","s":null,"p":"p644e5969","pi":2,"id":"a76ed27206","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"q":"Ampicillin kills susceptible bacteria by blocking cell-wall synthesis. Why doesn't it harm human cells?","a":"Human cells have no peptidoglycan cell wall","w":["Human ribosomes are 80S instead of 70S","Human cells pump ampicillin out","Human DNA lacks the ampicillin target sequence"],"e":"β-lactams like ampicillin block cross-linking of peptidoglycan, which only bacteria make. The ribosome difference is the basis for other antibiotics, such as tetracyclines.","c":"2B","t":"Prokaryotes","s":null,"p":"p644e5969","pi":3,"id":"acfe81e397","L":"2B · Prokaryotes and viruses","T":"Prokaryotes","g":"Foundation 2 · Cells and organisms"},{"q":"Compared with before treatment, blood renin levels in these patients most likely:","a":"Increased, because angiotensin II no longer inhibits renin release","w":["Decreased, because ACE inhibitors also block renin release","Stayed the same, because ACE acts after renin in the pathway","Decreased, because lower blood pressure suppresses renin release"],"e":"Less angiotensin II means less negative feedback on juxtaglomerular cells, so renin rises. Lower blood pressure also stimulates renin release.","c":"3B","t":"Renal","s":null,"p":"p6fa82069","pi":0,"id":"4dbd8157dd","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"How would the ACE inhibitor most likely affect the kidney's handling of sodium?","a":"Less Na⁺ reabsorption, because aldosterone levels fall","w":["More Na⁺ reabsorption, because aldosterone levels rise","No change, because ACE acts in the lungs","More Na⁺ reabsorption, because renin rises"],"e":"Less angiotensin II means less aldosterone. Less Na⁺ (and water) is reabsorbed in the distal nephron, which lowers blood volume and pressure.","c":"3B","t":"Renal","s":null,"p":"p6fa82069","pi":1,"id":"9effbe2a8c","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Some patients developed a dry cough. According to the passage, the most likely cause is:","a":"Bradykinin building up because ACE no longer breaks it down","w":["Angiotensin II building up and constricting the airways","Aldosterone building up and drying out the airway lining","Renin building up and damaging capillaries in the lungs"],"e":"The passage says ACE breaks down bradykinin, which can irritate the airways. Blocking ACE lets bradykinin accumulate.","c":"3B","t":"Renal","s":null,"p":"p6fa82069","pi":2,"id":"9ee7a2b124","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Where does the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II mainly take place?","a":"On the capillaries of the lungs","w":["In the liver","In the juxtaglomerular cells","In the adrenal cortex"],"e":"The passage says ACE is found mostly on lung capillaries. The liver makes angiotensinogen, and juxtaglomerular cells release renin.","c":"3B","t":"Renal","s":null,"p":"p6fa82069","pi":3,"id":"0913796919","L":"3B · Organ systems","T":"Renal","g":"Foundation 3 · Organ systems"},{"q":"Why did adding ADP raise oxygen consumption?","a":"ADP lets ATP synthase run, which lets protons back into the matrix so electron transport can speed up","w":["ADP donates electrons to Complex I, adding more electrons to the chain so more oxygen is reduced","ADP binds Complex IV directly and raises its affinity for oxygen, so oxygen is reduced faster","ADP makes the inner membrane leaky to protons, so the gradient collapses and respiration speeds up"],"e":"Respiratory control: without ADP, the proton gradient builds up and slows electron transport. Using the gradient to make ATP lets respiration speed up.","c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","s":null,"p":"p43d02800","pi":0,"id":"6bef15650a","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Why did oligomycin lower oxygen consumption almost to the starting rate?","a":"Blocking ATP synthase let the gradient build back up, slowing electron flow","w":["Oligomycin binds Complex IV and stops it from passing electrons to oxygen","Oligomycin reacts with succinate and removes the fuel for the electron chain","Oligomycin makes the inner membrane leaky, so protons can't build a gradient"],"e":"Electron transport is coupled to ATP synthesis through the proton gradient. Stopping proton reentry backs up the whole chain.","c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","s":null,"p":"p43d02800","pi":1,"id":"c8c6b27b4a","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"After DNP was added, ATP production would most likely be:","a":"Very low, even though oxygen consumption was highest","w":["Highest of all the conditions, because O₂ use was highest","The same as with succinate and ADP, since ADP was still present","Higher, because DNP activates ATP synthase as it carries protons"],"e":"DNP lets protons leak back across the membrane without going through ATP synthase (which oligomycin also blocks here). Electron transport races, but the energy is lost as heat.","c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","s":null,"p":"p43d02800","pi":2,"id":"e33c85a2bc","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"},{"q":"Electrons from succinate enter the chain at Complex II rather than Complex I. Compared with electrons from NADH, they:","a":"Pump fewer protons, so they support less ATP synthesis","w":["Pump more protons, so they support more ATP synthesis","Pump the same number of protons","Bypass Complex IV"],"e":"Complex II pumps no protons. FADH₂-linked electrons skip Complex I's pumping, so they yield about 1.5 ATP per pair versus about 2.5 for NADH.","c":"1D","t":"Oxidative phosphorylation","s":null,"p":"p43d02800","pi":3,"id":"f4b5bfba27","L":"1D · Bioenergetics and metabolism","T":"Oxidative phosphorylation","g":"Foundation 1 · Biomolecules"}],"psychsoc":[{"c":"6A","t":"Sensation","q":"The smallest difference between two stimuli that a person can detect 50% of the time is called the:","a":"Just-noticeable difference (difference threshold)","w":["Absolute threshold of sensation","Signal detection response criterion","Threshold of sensory adaptation"],"e":"The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus detectable at all. The just-noticeable difference is the smallest detectable change between stimuli.","id":"a4bd21e8be","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Sensation","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Sensation","q":"Weber's law states that the just-noticeable difference is:","a":"A constant proportion of the original stimulus","w":["A fixed amount, regardless of the original stimulus","Larger for weak stimuli than for strong ones in absolute terms","The same for every sense"],"e":"If you can tell 100 g from 102 g, you'd need about 204 g to tell from 200 g. The ratio (2%) stays constant.","id":"235719e9f0","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Sensation","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Sensation","q":"In signal detection theory, saying a tone was present when it actually wasn't is called a:","a":"False alarm","w":["Miss","Hit","Correct rejection"],"e":"Responses fall into four outcomes: hit, miss, false alarm and correct rejection. A person's response bias shifts the balance between misses and false alarms.","id":"dfa5f2600f","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Sensation","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Sensation","q":"After a few minutes in a room, you stop noticing the hum of the refrigerator. This is:","a":"Sensory adaptation","w":["Habituation of a learned fear","Perceptual constancy","Selective attention to the hum"],"e":"Sensory receptors and neurons respond less to an unchanging stimulus over time.","id":"d30eec3519","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Sensation","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Vision","q":"Which photoreceptors are most concentrated in the fovea and are responsible for color vision?","a":"Cones","w":["Rods","Ganglion cells","Bipolar cells"],"e":"Cones give sharp color vision in bright light. Rods are concentrated in the periphery and handle dim-light vision.","id":"7552bc79ff","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Vision","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Vision","q":"Information from the right visual field of both eyes is first processed in which hemisphere's visual cortex?","a":"The left hemisphere","w":["The right hemisphere","Both hemispheres equally","The hemisphere on the same side as the dominant eye"],"e":"Fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross at the optic chiasm, so each visual field goes to the opposite hemisphere's occipital lobe.","id":"7de35ef969","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Vision","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Vision","q":"Feature detectors in the primary visual cortex respond to specific:","a":"Edges, lines and orientations","w":["Faces and objects","Colors only","Sounds paired with images"],"e":"Hubel and Wiesel found cortical cells tuned to lines at particular angles. More complex features like faces are processed in later areas.","id":"06bb2bb32d","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Vision","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Hearing and other senses","q":"Where are the hair cells that turn sound vibrations into neural signals?","a":"The cochlea","w":["The semicircular canals","The tympanic membrane","The ossicles"],"e":"Hair cells on the basilar membrane bend as fluid moves. High frequencies stimulate the base of the cochlea and low frequencies the apex (place theory).","id":"fecdca694a","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Hearing and other senses","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Hearing and other senses","q":"The vestibular system, which senses balance and head movement, is in the:","a":"Semicircular canals and otolith organs of the inner ear","w":["Cochlea and basilar membrane of the inner ear","Ossicles and tympanic membrane of the middle ear","Cerebellum and brainstem, without inner-ear input"],"e":"Semicircular canals sense rotation, and the utricle and saccule sense linear acceleration and head tilt.","id":"d56ef64c6a","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Hearing and other senses","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6A","t":"Hearing and other senses","q":"Which sense sends signals to the cortex without first passing through the thalamus?","a":"Smell (olfaction)","w":["Vision","Hearing","Touch"],"e":"Olfactory signals go from the olfactory bulb to the olfactory cortex and limbic structures, which may be why smells trigger strong memories and emotions.","id":"408c84680c","L":"6A · Sensing the environment","T":"Hearing and other senses","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Perception","q":"Using your knowledge and expectations to interpret a blurry word in a sentence is an example of:","a":"Top-down processing","w":["Bottom-up processing","Feature detection","Sensory adaptation"],"e":"Top-down processing starts from prior knowledge. Bottom-up processing builds a percept from the raw sensory features.","id":"87466f05bf","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Perception","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Perception","q":"A door looks rectangular to you even when it's open at an angle and casts a trapezoid on your retina. This is:","a":"Shape constancy","w":["Size constancy","The Gestalt principle of closure","Motion parallax"],"e":"Perceptual constancies keep objects looking stable despite changing retinal images.","id":"f92e0bb991","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Perception","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Perception","q":"Seeing a complete circle when a drawing has small gaps reflects which Gestalt principle?","a":"Closure","w":["Similarity","Proximity","Continuity"],"e":"Closure means filling in gaps to see a whole object. Similarity groups like items, proximity groups nearby items, and continuity follows smooth lines.","id":"b458699ef8","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Perception","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Perception","q":"Which depth cue requires both eyes?","a":"Retinal disparity","w":["Linear perspective","Relative size","Interposition"],"e":"Each eye sees a slightly different image, and the brain uses the difference to judge depth. The others are monocular cues.","id":"9f4deae9c3","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Perception","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Attention","q":"Hearing your name across a noisy party, even though you weren't listening to that conversation, illustrates:","a":"The cocktail party effect","w":["Inattentional blindness","Change blindness","Divided attention"],"e":"Some unattended information is still processed, and personally important stimuli can grab attention.","id":"2a2591d909","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Attention","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Attention","q":"Participants counting basketball passes fail to notice a person in a gorilla suit walking through the scene. This shows:","a":"Inattentional blindness","w":["Change blindness","Sensory adaptation","Retroactive interference"],"e":"Focusing attention on one task can make people miss unexpected but plainly visible events.","id":"086f3adedf","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Attention","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Information that is rehearsed for only a few seconds and then lost was most likely held in:","a":"Short-term (working) memory","w":["Long-term memory","Iconic memory only","Procedural memory"],"e":"Short-term memory holds a few items for about 20–30 seconds without rehearsal. Sensory memory lasts under a second for vision.","id":"f674a5b01b","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Grouping the digits 1-9-4-5-2-0-2-6 as '1945' and '2026' to remember them is called:","a":"Chunking","w":["Priming","Elaborative rehearsal","The method of loci"],"e":"Chunking packs items into meaningful units, getting around the limited capacity of short-term memory (about 7 ± 2 items).","id":"bb3156bf2f","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"After learning a new phone number, you struggle to recall your old one. This is:","a":"Retroactive interference","w":["Proactive interference","Decay","Source monitoring error"],"e":"Retroactive interference means new learning disrupts old memories. Proactive interference means old learning disrupts new memories.","id":"643c6c01a0","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Knowing how to ride a bike is an example of:","a":"Procedural (implicit) memory","w":["Episodic memory","Semantic memory","Flashbulb memory"],"e":"Procedural memories are skills performed without conscious recall. Episodic and semantic memories are explicit (declarative).","id":"898316789a","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Patient H.M. had his hippocampus removed and could no longer form new long-term declarative memories. This is called:","a":"Anterograde amnesia","w":["Retrograde amnesia","Korsakoff syndrome","Source amnesia"],"e":"Anterograde amnesia blocks new memories after the injury. Retrograde amnesia erases memories from before it. H.M. could still learn new motor skills.","id":"6182e29b53","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Recalling the first and last items on a list better than the middle ones is called the:","a":"Serial position effect","w":["Spacing effect","Misinformation effect","Testing effect"],"e":"Early items benefit from rehearsal (primacy) and late items are still in short-term memory (recency).","id":"4c1b670bd0","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Memory","q":"Studying in shorter sessions over several days usually beats cramming the same total time into one session. This is the:","a":"Spacing effect","w":["Serial position effect","Primacy effect","Mere exposure effect"],"e":"Distributed practice produces stronger long-term retention than massed practice, which is also why this app spaces your reviews.","id":"208550497b","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Cognition","q":"Judging how common something is by how easily examples come to mind is the:","a":"Availability heuristic","w":["Representativeness heuristic","Anchoring effect","Confirmation bias"],"e":"Vivid or recent examples, like plane crashes in the news, can make events seem more common than they are.","id":"a50662796b","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Cognition","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Cognition","q":"Only seeing a pair of pliers as a gripping tool, and not as a weight for a pendulum, is an example of:","a":"Functional fixedness","w":["Mental set","Belief perseverance","Overconfidence"],"e":"Functional fixedness is being stuck on an object's usual use. A mental set is sticking with a problem-solving approach that worked before.","id":"ee713dfe2e","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Cognition","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Cognition","q":"According to Piaget, children first understand that an amount of liquid stays the same when poured into a taller glass during the:","a":"Concrete operational stage","w":["Preoperational stage","Sensorimotor stage","Formal operational stage"],"e":"Conservation develops around ages 7–11. Preoperational children (about 2–7) focus on one dimension, such as the height of the liquid.","id":"a9dfc5f7c7","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Cognition","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Cognition","q":"In Piaget's theory, object permanence develops during the:","a":"Sensorimotor stage","w":["Preoperational stage","Concrete operational stage","Formal operational stage"],"e":"During the sensorimotor stage (birth to about age 2), infants learn that objects still exist when out of sight.","id":"afa29d69e8","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Cognition","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Consciousness","q":"Most vivid dreaming occurs during which stage of sleep?","a":"REM sleep","w":["Stage 1 (N1)","Stage 2 (N2)","Slow-wave sleep (N3)"],"e":"REM sleep has fast, wake-like brain waves and near-paralysis of skeletal muscles. Slow-wave sleep shows delta waves.","id":"af643f04bf","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Consciousness","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Consciousness","q":"Which brain wave pattern is typical of deep, slow-wave (N3) sleep?","a":"Delta waves","w":["Beta waves","Alpha waves","Sleep spindles only"],"e":"Delta waves are high-amplitude and low-frequency. Beta waves mark alert wakefulness, alpha waves relaxed wakefulness, and sleep spindles and K-complexes mark stage N2.","id":"840b686aa3","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Consciousness","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Consciousness","q":"Needing more of a drug to get the same effect is called:","a":"Tolerance","w":["Withdrawal","Dependence","Sensitization"],"e":"Tolerance develops as the body adapts. Withdrawal is the set of symptoms that appears when someone dependent stops using the drug.","id":"d5c283293b","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Consciousness","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6B","t":"Language","q":"Damage to Broca's area typically causes:","a":"Slow, effortful speech with relatively intact comprehension","w":["Fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension","Inability to read but normal speech","Loss of hearing"],"e":"Broca's area (frontal lobe) supports speech production. Wernicke's area (temporal lobe) supports comprehension, and damage there causes fluent but meaningless speech.","id":"9f6a0f1dd3","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Language","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6C","t":"Emotion","q":"The James–Lange theory says that emotion:","a":"Follows from noticing the body's physiological response","w":["Happens at the same time as the physiological response","Depends on how we label our arousal","Comes before any physiological change"],"e":"'We feel afraid because we tremble.' Cannon–Bard says arousal and emotion happen together. Schachter–Singer adds a cognitive label for the arousal.","id":"fbbc408c13","L":"6C · Responding to the world","T":"Emotion","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6C","t":"Emotion","q":"According to the Schachter–Singer two-factor theory, emotion requires:","a":"Physiological arousal plus a cognitive label for that arousal","w":["Physiological arousal alone, without any interpretation of it","A cognitive appraisal alone, without any bodily arousal at all","Activation of the amygdala before any physical arousal occurs"],"e":"The same arousal can be felt as fear or excitement depending on how the person explains it.","id":"c6e0a4fe09","L":"6C · Responding to the world","T":"Emotion","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6C","t":"Emotion","q":"Which brain structure is most strongly linked to fear and to detecting threats?","a":"The amygdala","w":["The hippocampus","The cerebellum","The occipital lobe"],"e":"The amygdala is part of the limbic system. It helps form fear memories and triggers rapid responses to threats.","id":"e39fcff185","L":"6C · Responding to the world","T":"Emotion","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6C","t":"Stress","q":"In Selye's general adaptation syndrome, the stages occur in which order?","a":"Alarm, resistance, exhaustion","w":["Resistance, alarm, exhaustion","Exhaustion, alarm, resistance","Alarm, exhaustion, resistance"],"e":"The body first mobilizes (alarm), then sustains its effort (resistance), then wears down if stress continues (exhaustion).","id":"d82750bbd7","L":"6C · Responding to the world","T":"Stress","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"6C","t":"Stress","q":"Long-term activation of the HPA axis raises levels of which hormone, which can suppress immune function?","a":"Cortisol","w":["Oxytocin","Melatonin","Insulin"],"e":"Hypothalamus (CRH) → pituitary (ACTH) → adrenal cortex (cortisol). Chronic cortisol suppresses immunity and can impair memory.","id":"f8a427e9a6","L":"6C · Responding to the world","T":"Stress","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"c":"7A","t":"Personality","q":"Which of these is one of the Big Five personality traits?","a":"Conscientiousness","w":["Psychoticism","Self-efficacy","Locus of control"],"e":"The Big Five (OCEAN) are openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. Psychoticism belongs to Eysenck's model.","id":"4a08832424","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Personality","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Personality","q":"Believing that outcomes in your life are mostly caused by your own effort shows:","a":"An internal locus of control","w":["An external locus of control","Learned helplessness","Low self-efficacy"],"e":"An internal locus of control credits yourself. An external one credits luck, fate or other people.","id":"8a6c110b9f","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Personality","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Personality","q":"In Freud's theory, the part of personality that works on the pleasure principle is the:","a":"Id","w":["Ego","Superego","Preconscious"],"e":"The id seeks immediate gratification, the ego works on the reality principle, and the superego holds internalized moral standards.","id":"3404a12734","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Personality","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Personality","q":"A student angry at their professor yells at their roommate instead. Which defense mechanism is this?","a":"Displacement","w":["Projection","Sublimation","Reaction formation"],"e":"Displacement redirects feelings to a safer target. Projection attributes your own feelings to others. Sublimation channels urges into acceptable activities.","id":"d0edd5c473","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Personality","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Motivation","q":"In Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which needs must be met first?","a":"Physiological needs such as food and water","w":["Esteem needs such as respect","Belongingness and love from others","Self-actualization and personal growth"],"e":"The order is physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, then self-actualization at the top.","id":"3c05d309e5","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Motivation","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Motivation","q":"According to the Yerkes–Dodson law, performance on a complex task is best at:","a":"A moderate level of arousal","w":["The lowest level of arousal","The highest level of arousal","Any level, as long as the task is familiar"],"e":"Performance follows an inverted U. The best arousal level is lower for hard tasks and higher for simple ones.","id":"cccccec828","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Motivation","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Motivation","q":"A child who used to draw for fun loses interest after being paid for each drawing. This illustrates the:","a":"Overjustification effect","w":["Yerkes–Dodson law","Drive-reduction theory","Self-fulfilling prophecy"],"e":"External rewards can undercut intrinsic motivation for a task the person already enjoyed.","id":"7752808299","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Motivation","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Psychological disorders","q":"Which neurotransmitter is most associated with the positive symptoms of schizophrenia?","a":"Dopamine","w":["Serotonin","GABA","Acetylcholine"],"e":"The dopamine hypothesis links excess dopamine activity to hallucinations and delusions. Most antipsychotics block D₂ receptors.","id":"b1b6c0efb3","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Psychological disorders","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Psychological disorders","q":"Hallucinations and delusions are classified as which kind of symptom in schizophrenia?","a":"Positive symptoms","w":["Negative symptoms","Cognitive symptoms","Mood symptoms"],"e":"Positive symptoms add something not normally present. Negative symptoms, such as flat affect or reduced speech, take something away.","id":"a121710cb7","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Psychological disorders","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Psychological disorders","q":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) treat depression by:","a":"Blocking reuptake of serotonin, which leaves more in the synapse","w":["Blocking postsynaptic serotonin receptors, which reduces overactive signaling","Speeding up serotonin breakdown, which resets receptor sensitivity","Releasing more dopamine from presynaptic terminals in the reward pathway"],"e":"SSRIs block the serotonin transporter so serotonin stays in the synaptic cleft longer.","id":"c2440ab3cb","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Psychological disorders","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Learning","q":"In Pavlov's experiments, the bell that came to cause salivation after being paired with food was the:","a":"Conditioned stimulus","w":["Unconditioned stimulus","Unconditioned response","Neutral response"],"e":"Before pairing, the bell was a neutral stimulus. Food is the unconditioned stimulus, and salivating to the bell is the conditioned response.","id":"206778bab0","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Learning","q":"Taking away a headache by taking aspirin makes you more likely to take aspirin next time. This is:","a":"Negative reinforcement","w":["Positive reinforcement","Negative punishment","Positive punishment"],"e":"Reinforcement increases a behavior, and 'negative' means something is removed (the headache). Punishment decreases a behavior.","id":"a681e05846","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Learning","q":"Which reinforcement schedule produces behavior that is most resistant to extinction?","a":"Variable ratio","w":["Fixed ratio","Fixed interval","Continuous reinforcement"],"e":"Rewards after an unpredictable number of responses, as with slot machines, keep people responding long after rewards stop.","id":"e9ae1434c1","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Learning","q":"A child learns to share after watching a classmate get praised for sharing. This is:","a":"Observational learning","w":["Classical conditioning","Habituation","Insight learning"],"e":"Bandura showed that people learn by watching models and the consequences they receive, as in the Bobo doll studies.","id":"8ccaa646ea","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7A","t":"Learning","q":"After a conditioned response has been extinguished, it may briefly reappear after a rest period. This is:","a":"Spontaneous recovery","w":["Generalization","Discrimination","Acquisition"],"e":"Extinction suppresses the response rather than erasing the learning, so it can return after a pause.","id":"e34c587ad0","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","q":"People perform simple, well-practiced tasks better when others are watching. This is:","a":"Social facilitation","w":["Social loafing","Deindividuation","Groupthink"],"e":"An audience raises arousal, which improves dominant (well-learned) responses but can hurt performance on new or hard tasks.","id":"f9b26934bc","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","q":"Group members put in less effort when their individual work can't be measured. This is called:","a":"Social loafing","w":["Social facilitation","Group polarization","The bystander effect"],"e":"When responsibility is spread across a group and individual effort isn't visible, people tend to slack off.","id":"a5e8967bcc","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","q":"After discussion, a group of moderately cautious people becomes even more cautious. This is:","a":"Group polarization","w":["Groupthink","Social loafing","Deindividuation"],"e":"Discussion tends to push a group's average opinion further in the direction it already leaned.","id":"a4f476893b","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","q":"A cohesive team silences doubts to keep harmony and makes a poor decision. This is:","a":"Groupthink","w":["Group polarization","Social facilitation","The foot-in-the-door technique"],"e":"Groupthink values consensus over critical evaluation. Warning signs include an illusion of invulnerability and pressure on dissenters.","id":"d6a790ea6b","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","q":"The more people who witness an emergency, the less likely any one of them is to help. This is the:","a":"Bystander effect","w":["Just-world hypothesis","Social loafing effect","Mere exposure effect"],"e":"Diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance (everyone looking to others to judge the situation) both contribute.","id":"4d2404895d","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Conformity and obedience","q":"In Asch's line-judgment experiments, many participants gave obviously wrong answers that matched the group's. This shows:","a":"Conformity","w":["Obedience","Compliance with a request","Group polarization"],"e":"Conformity is matching others' behavior or beliefs. Obedience, as in Milgram's studies, is following orders from an authority.","id":"4da5b6ec1d","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Conformity and obedience","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Conformity and obedience","q":"Milgram's studies found that most participants:","a":"Obeyed an authority figure and gave what they believed were dangerous shocks","w":["Refused to deliver any shocks once the learner first protested","Delivered the strongest shocks only when the learner was in the same room","Conformed to a group of peers who were also delivering shocks"],"e":"About 65% continued to the maximum level. Obedience dropped when the authority was farther away or the victim was closer.","id":"9a295d2f1c","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Conformity and obedience","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Socialization","q":"Agents of socialization include all of the following EXCEPT:","a":"Genetic inheritance","w":["Family","Peers","Mass media"],"e":"Socialization is how people learn their culture's norms and values through social contact. Genes are biological, not social, influences.","id":"cd1fa4548f","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Socialization","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Norms and deviance","q":"Merton's strain theory explains deviance as resulting from:","a":"A gap between culturally valued goals and the legitimate means available to reach them","w":["Being labeled deviant by powerful groups, which then shapes a person's identity","Learning deviant behavior and attitudes through close contact with deviant peers","A complete breakdown of social norms during periods of rapid social change"],"e":"Strain leads to adaptations such as innovation (new, possibly illegal means) or rebellion. Labeling theory and differential association are different explanations.","id":"5ce7374f5c","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Norms and deviance","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7B","t":"Norms and deviance","q":"Sutherland's differential association theory proposes that deviance is:","a":"Learned through interaction with people who support deviant behavior","w":["Caused by strain between cultural goals and available means","A label applied by more powerful groups to less powerful ones","Inherited biologically and expressed when social controls weaken"],"e":"People learn techniques, motives and attitudes toward breaking rules from those around them.","id":"1aa9d92f34","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Norms and deviance","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7C","t":"Attitudes","q":"Feeling uncomfortable when your actions conflict with your beliefs, and then changing a belief to match, illustrates:","a":"Cognitive dissonance","w":["Self-serving bias","The halo effect","Belief perseverance"],"e":"Festinger found people paid $1 to call a dull task fun later rated it as more enjoyable than people paid $20. They had too little justification for their action.","id":"3732846187","L":"7C · Attitude and behavior change","T":"Attitudes","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7C","t":"Attitudes","q":"After agreeing to put a small sign in their yard, homeowners are more likely to agree to a large billboard. This is the:","a":"Foot-in-the-door technique","w":["Door-in-the-face technique","Lowball technique","Reciprocity norm"],"e":"Agreeing to a small request makes people more likely to agree to a larger one. Door-in-the-face starts with a big request that's refused.","id":"ac06598930","L":"7C · Attitude and behavior change","T":"Attitudes","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7C","t":"Attitudes","q":"In the elaboration likelihood model, a person who is motivated and able to think carefully is persuaded mainly through the:","a":"Central route, by the strength of the arguments","w":["Peripheral route, by how attractive the speaker is","Peripheral route, by how many arguments there are","Central route, by the speaker's celebrity"],"e":"Central-route persuasion depends on argument quality and tends to last. Peripheral-route persuasion relies on surface cues.","id":"6b012e8124","L":"7C · Attitude and behavior change","T":"Attitudes","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"c":"7C","t":"Attitudes","q":"Social learning theory and self-efficacy were both central to the work of:","a":"Albert Bandura","w":["B. F. Skinner","Ivan Pavlov","Sigmund Freud"],"e":"Bandura emphasized observing models and believing you can succeed (self-efficacy), linking thoughts, behavior and environment.","id":"1a6d27940d","L":"7C · Attitude and behavior change","T":"Attitudes","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"What is the independent variable in this study?","a":"Whether participants slept the night after learning","w":["The percentage of word pairs each group recalled","The number of word pairs each participant learned","The time of morning when the first test was given"],"e":"The researchers manipulated sleep versus wakefulness by random assignment. Recall is the dependent variable they measured.","c":"6B","t":"Memory","s":null,"p":"p39207941","pi":0,"id":"a74e91bdf7","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"q":"Why did the researchers include Test 2?","a":"To rule out that the wake group did worse only because it was tired at Test 1","w":["To measure how quickly participants could learn a second set of word pairs","To give the sleep group a second chance to learn pairs they had missed","To confirm that random assignment had produced groups of equal ability"],"e":"After recovery sleep, the wake group was no longer sleep-deprived but still recalled less, which suggests the original sleep helped store the memories.","c":"6B","t":"Memory","s":null,"p":"p39207941","pi":1,"id":"5c24e12755","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"q":"Which conclusion do the results best support?","a":"Sleep soon after learning helps consolidate new declarative memories","w":["Sleep deprivation permanently damages the hippocampus","Recall improves the more often a word list is tested","Sleep has no effect on memory once people have recovered"],"e":"The sleep group's advantage held at Test 2, which fits the idea that sleep, especially slow-wave sleep, helps move new memories into long-term storage.","c":"6B","t":"Memory","s":null,"p":"p39207941","pi":2,"id":"13d3827596","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"q":"Because participants were randomly assigned to groups, the researchers can more reasonably claim:","a":"That sleep caused the difference in recall","w":["That the results apply to all ages","That the effect is the same in every culture","That participants could not guess the hypothesis"],"e":"Random assignment spreads other differences evenly across groups, which supports causal conclusions (internal validity). Generalizing beyond college students is a question of external validity.","c":"6B","t":"Memory","s":null,"p":"p39207941","pi":3,"id":"550b51fd08","L":"6B · Making sense of the environment","T":"Memory","g":"Foundation 6 · Perception and response"},{"q":"Which concept best explains the pattern in Table 1?","a":"Diffusion of responsibility","w":["Social facilitation","Group polarization","Cognitive dissonance"],"e":"Each person feels less personally responsible when others could also act, so helping drops and slows as the group grows. This is the bystander effect.","c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","s":null,"p":"pa96657b0","pi":0,"id":"ecdd4c96b5","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"What was the dependent variable?","a":"Whether and how quickly participants sought help","w":["The number of other people the participant believed were present","The content and length of the recorded seizure","Whether or not participants were deceived about the setup"],"e":"The researchers manipulated group size (the independent variable) and measured helping.","c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","s":null,"p":"pa96657b0","pi":1,"id":"bd7ae194ae","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"Telling participants about the deception afterward is an example of:","a":"Debriefing","w":["Informed consent","Random assignment","A double-blind procedure"],"e":"When deception is necessary, ethical guidelines require debriefing: explaining the true purpose after participation.","c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","s":null,"p":"pa96657b0","pi":2,"id":"b2ab92acee","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"If the other listeners were the participant's close friends instead of strangers, helping would most likely:","a":"Increase, because friends feel more responsible for one another and share clearer norms","w":["Decrease further, because responsibility would be spread across more trusted people","Stay the same, because the number of listeners would not change at all","Drop to zero, because each friend would wait for another friend to act first"],"e":"The bystander effect is weaker among people who know each other. Group cohesion and shared norms make it easier to act.","c":"7B","t":"Group behavior","s":null,"p":"pa96657b0","pi":3,"id":"7b24600fb1","L":"7B · Social processes and behavior","T":"Group behavior","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"The step-by-step decline in diabetes across all five quintiles best illustrates:","a":"The social gradient in health","w":["Absolute poverty","Upward social mobility","The demographic transition"],"e":"Health improves at each step up the income ladder, not only between the poorest and everyone else.","c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","s":null,"p":"p2a9b9953","pi":0,"id":"fcb23eab43","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"q":"Which conclusion is best supported by this study?","a":"Grocery distance and income are associated with diabetes, but causation isn't shown","w":["Living far from a grocery store causes type 2 diabetes in adults of every income level","Opening a grocery store in each Q1 neighborhood will cut diabetes there by about half","Income is related to grocery access, but it has no relationship with diabetes rates"],"e":"This is a cross-sectional, observational study. Other factors that vary with income, such as parks, fast food, health care and stress, could explain the link.","c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","s":null,"p":"p2a9b9953","pi":1,"id":"ed251ac6fb","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"q":"A conflict theorist would most likely explain these findings in terms of:","a":"Unequal distribution of resources between more and less powerful groups","w":["How the health care system helps keep society stable and orderly","The shared meanings residents attach to food and eating habits","Individual lifestyle choices made independently of social forces"],"e":"Conflict theory focuses on how inequality in power and resources shapes outcomes such as access to healthy food.","c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","s":null,"p":"p2a9b9953","pi":2,"id":"221e48540e","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"q":"Lower-income neighborhoods in the study had fewer parks AND more fast-food outlets. For the council member's proposal, this suggests that:","a":"A grocery store alone may not fully cut diabetes, since several factors differ","w":["The grocery store will definitely work, because access is the main cause of diabetes","Parks should be removed from the analysis, because they don't affect health at all","Fast-food outlets likely lower diabetes rates, because they offer convenient meals"],"e":"Several social determinants of health vary together, so changing just one may have a limited effect.","c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","s":null,"p":"p2a9b9953","pi":3,"id":"1b09ca9393","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"q":"During training, the light and tone served as:","a":"Conditioned stimuli","w":["Unconditioned stimuli","Unconditioned responses","Negative reinforcers"],"e":"They were neutral at first but became meaningful by being paired with cocaine, the unconditioned stimulus.","c":"7A","t":"Learning","s":null,"p":"pa74ecc7e","pi":0,"id":"e9bf6adbda","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"Lever pressing during training increased because cocaine acted as:","a":"A positive reinforcer","w":["A negative reinforcer","A positive punisher","A conditioned stimulus"],"e":"Something desirable was added after the behavior, making the behavior more likely. That's positive reinforcement in operant conditioning.","c":"7A","t":"Learning","s":null,"p":"pa74ecc7e","pi":1,"id":"2db8611aa9","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"The rise in pressing after the three-week break with no training is best described as:","a":"Spontaneous recovery","w":["Stimulus generalization","Acquisition","Habituation"],"e":"After extinction, a response can return after time passes. This shows extinction suppresses rather than erases learning.","c":"7A","t":"Learning","s":null,"p":"pa74ecc7e","pi":2,"id":"b7380fccb9","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"Cocaine's effect on dopamine most directly:","a":"Raises synaptic dopamine, strengthening the behavior's rewarding effect","w":["Lowers dopamine in the synapse, which the brain experiences as a craving","Blocks dopamine receptors, which makes natural rewards feel less pleasant","Speeds dopamine breakdown, which shortens each burst of reward signaling"],"e":"Blocking reuptake leaves dopamine in the synapse longer, which drives reward signaling in the mesolimbic pathway.","c":"7A","t":"Learning","s":null,"p":"pa74ecc7e","pi":3,"id":"c454d3578d","L":"7A · Individual influences on behavior","T":"Learning","g":"Foundation 7 · Behavior"},{"q":"Which phenomenon do these results best demonstrate?","a":"Stereotype threat","w":["The self-serving bias","The overjustification effect","The halo effect"],"e":"Reminding women of a negative stereotype about their group lowered their scores. With neutral instructions, they performed as well as men.","c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","s":null,"p":"p8b2f5777","pi":0,"id":"a7abd0f994","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"q":"Why is the 'women, neutral' condition important to the study's design?","a":"It shows how women score when the stereotype isn't raised, isolating the instructions' effect","w":["It controls for differences in overall math ability between women and men","It shows that men's scores do not depend on the instructions they are given","It makes the test easier, so it serves as a baseline for the hardest version"],"e":"Comparing women under the two instructions, with random assignment, isolates the effect of the instructions.","c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","s":null,"p":"p8b2f5777","pi":1,"id":"40befa41b8","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"q":"The anxiety ratings suggest that the effect on scores may work through:","a":"Heightened anxiety that interferes with performance","w":["Lower motivation to finish all of the test","A real difference in underlying math ability","Less time available to complete the questions"],"e":"Anxiety was highest in the group that scored lowest, which points to anxiety as a possible mediator. The study doesn't prove this, because anxiety was measured, not manipulated.","c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","s":null,"p":"p8b2f5777","pi":2,"id":"ab2ecf9599","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"q":"Which change would most improve the study's external validity?","a":"Repeating it with women from many backgrounds and fields, not just science","w":["Using a shorter test so participants don't get tired before the end","Removing the men's group so the study focuses only on women","Telling participants the hypothesis so they understand the purpose"],"e":"External validity is about generalizing beyond this sample and setting. A broader sample would show whether the effect holds more widely.","c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","s":null,"p":"p8b2f5777","pi":3,"id":"6ba72e19a2","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Self-concept","q":"Your belief in your ability to succeed at a specific task is called:","a":"Self-efficacy","w":["Self-esteem","Self-concept","Locus of control"],"e":"Self-efficacy is task-specific confidence. Self-esteem is your overall evaluation of your own worth.","id":"2dc5721a1f","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Self-concept","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Self-concept","q":"According to Cooley's 'looking-glass self,' a person's self-concept develops from:","a":"Imagining how others see and judge them","w":["Comparing themselves to an ideal self","Their biological temperament","Rewards and punishments for behavior"],"e":"We picture how we look to others, imagine their judgment, and form feelings about ourselves from that.","id":"287a6e083a","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Self-concept","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Self-concept","q":"In Mead's theory, the 'me' refers to:","a":"The social self, shaped by others' expectations","w":["The spontaneous, impulsive part of the self","The unconscious drives Freud called the id","The body's physical traits and appearance"],"e":"Mead split the self into the 'I' (spontaneous response) and the 'me' (the organized attitudes of others that we internalize).","id":"cc5792a9ae","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Self-concept","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Identity development","q":"According to Erikson, adolescents mainly face which psychosocial conflict?","a":"Identity vs. role confusion","w":["Intimacy vs. isolation","Industry vs. inferiority","Generativity vs. stagnation"],"e":"Adolescents explore and commit to an identity. Intimacy vs. isolation follows in young adulthood.","id":"ff6697e1e5","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Identity development","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Identity development","q":"Kohlberg's postconventional level of moral reasoning is based on:","a":"Abstract principles like justice and individual rights","w":["Avoiding punishment and gaining personal rewards","Gaining others' approval and following the law","Doing whatever authority figures say is right"],"e":"Kohlberg's levels are preconventional (consequences to self), conventional (social approval and law) and postconventional (universal principles).","id":"307dd8782b","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Identity development","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8A","t":"Identity development","q":"Freud's psychosexual stages run in which order?","a":"Oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital","w":["Anal, oral, phallic, genital, latency","Oral, phallic, anal, latency, genital","Latency, oral, anal, phallic, genital"],"e":"Freud believed unresolved conflicts at a stage could cause fixation, such as an 'oral' personality.","id":"15e4af7c99","L":"8A · Self-identity","T":"Identity development","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Attribution","q":"Explaining a stranger's rudeness by their personality instead of their bad day is an example of the:","a":"Fundamental attribution error","w":["Self-serving bias","Actor–observer bias","False consensus effect"],"e":"People tend to overestimate disposition and underestimate situation when explaining others' behavior.","id":"14ef36ef0f","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Attribution","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Attribution","q":"Crediting your own successes to skill but blaming your failures on bad luck is the:","a":"Self-serving bias","w":["Fundamental attribution error","Just-world hypothesis","Halo effect"],"e":"The self-serving bias protects self-esteem by attributing wins internally and losses externally.","id":"be505d1c13","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Attribution","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Attribution","q":"Believing that victims of misfortune must have done something to deserve it reflects the:","a":"Just-world hypothesis","w":["Self-serving bias","Optimism bias","Halo effect"],"e":"The belief that the world is fair can lead people to blame victims, which preserves a sense of control.","id":"174e06bf16","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Attribution","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Attribution","q":"Assuming an attractive person is also kind and intelligent is the:","a":"Halo effect","w":["Horn effect","Primacy effect","False consensus effect"],"e":"One positive trait colors judgments of unrelated traits.","id":"c7c0a06a38","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Attribution","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","q":"Which best describes the difference between prejudice and discrimination?","a":"Prejudice is an attitude, and discrimination is a behavior","w":["Prejudice is a behavior, and discrimination is an attitude","Prejudice is always positive, and discrimination is negative","Both are behaviors aimed at individuals"],"e":"Prejudice is an unjustified attitude toward a group, often based on stereotypes (beliefs). Discrimination is unequal treatment.","id":"73464c0ce2","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","q":"Worrying about confirming a negative stereotype about your group, and performing worse as a result, is called:","a":"Stereotype threat","w":["A self-fulfilling prophecy by a teacher","In-group bias","Ethnocentrism"],"e":"Steele and Aronson showed that reminding test-takers of a negative stereotype can lower their performance.","id":"1f15af55fe","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","q":"A teacher expects certain students to excel, treats them differently, and they do excel. This is a:","a":"Self-fulfilling prophecy","w":["Stereotype threat","Just-world belief","Halo effect"],"e":"Rosenthal and Jacobson's 'Pygmalion' study showed expectations can create the outcomes they predict.","id":"1a8e02da3d","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8B","t":"Prejudice","q":"Judging another culture by the standards of your own culture is:","a":"Ethnocentrism","w":["Cultural relativism","Cultural diffusion","Assimilation"],"e":"Cultural relativism means understanding a culture on its own terms.","id":"797d3d9e2b","L":"8B · Social thinking","T":"Prejudice","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"Goffman's dramaturgical approach compares social life to:","a":"A theater performance, with front-stage and back-stage behavior","w":["A market in which people trade social rewards and costs","A ladder of social classes that people climb or fall down","A machine whose interdependent parts keep society stable"],"e":"People manage the impressions they give (impression management) on the front stage and relax backstage.","id":"7bd32094b8","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"Which group is a primary group?","a":"A close family","w":["A university class","A company's employees","A political party"],"e":"Primary groups are small, intimate and long-lasting. Secondary groups are larger, impersonal and goal-focused.","id":"27e492b574","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"Repeatedly seeing someone tends to increase how much you like them. This is the:","a":"Mere exposure effect","w":["Proximity effect","Similarity effect","Reciprocity effect"],"e":"Familiarity breeds liking. Proximity matters partly because it increases exposure.","id":"4c379031d1","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, a securely attached infant typically:","a":"Gets upset when the caregiver leaves and is comforted when they return","w":["Shows no reaction when the caregiver leaves or comes back","Stays upset and pushes the caregiver away on their return","Prefers the stranger to the caregiver throughout the session"],"e":"The other patterns are avoidant (ignores caregiver), ambivalent/resistant (clingy and hard to soothe) and disorganized.","id":"414e84a0b0","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"Aggression aimed at hurting someone, as opposed to reaching another goal, is called:","a":"Hostile aggression","w":["Instrumental aggression","Relational aggression","Displaced aggression"],"e":"Instrumental aggression uses harm as a means to an end, such as a robbery.","id":"804589f9bd","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"8C","t":"Social interaction","q":"Communicating through facial expressions, gestures and posture is called:","a":"Nonverbal communication","w":["Impression management","Role strain","Symbolic interactionism"],"e":"Ekman found certain facial expressions of basic emotions are recognized across many cultures.","id":"b171a45ae1","L":"8C · Social interactions","T":"Social interaction","g":"Foundation 8 · Self and others"},{"c":"9A","t":"Sociological theories","q":"Which theory views society as a system of interrelated parts that work together to keep stability?","a":"Functionalism","w":["Conflict theory","Symbolic interactionism","Social constructionism"],"e":"Functionalists like Durkheim ask what purpose each institution serves. They distinguish manifest (intended) and latent (unintended) functions.","id":"398b433732","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Sociological theories","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Sociological theories","q":"Which theory emphasizes competition between groups for scarce resources and power?","a":"Conflict theory","w":["Functionalism","Symbolic interactionism","Rational choice theory"],"e":"Conflict theory comes from Marx and focuses on inequality and how dominant groups keep their advantages.","id":"d4a279a722","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Sociological theories","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Sociological theories","q":"Studying how people create shared meanings through symbols and everyday interaction is the focus of:","a":"Symbolic interactionism","w":["Conflict theory","Functionalism","Feminist theory"],"e":"This micro-level approach (Mead, Blumer) asks how people interpret and act on meanings.","id":"3ba1bb1ae2","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Sociological theories","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Sociological theories","q":"A latent function of college is:","a":"Helping students build social networks and find partners","w":["Teaching students the skills they need for future jobs","Granting degrees that certify what students have learned","Teaching academic subjects through lectures and courses"],"e":"Latent functions are unintended consequences. Teaching and credentials are college's manifest (intended) functions.","id":"9f3be8dcd8","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Sociological theories","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Institutions","q":"In Weber's ideal bureaucracy, which feature is expected?","a":"A clear hierarchy with written rules and specialized roles","w":["Decisions made mainly on the basis of personal loyalty","Leadership passed down within families across generations","Flexible roles with no formal rules or division of labor"],"e":"Weber described bureaucracy as rational and impersonal. The 'iron cage' was his warning about over-rationalization.","id":"6ce0a02edf","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Institutions","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Institutions","q":"The 'McDonaldization' of society refers to the spread of:","a":"Efficiency, predictability, calculability and control","w":["American fast-food chains into other countries","Small, family-owned businesses in city centers","Traditional, personal forms of authority at work"],"e":"Ritzer extended Weber's ideas on rationalization to describe how fast-food principles spread across institutions.","id":"cf8bd62de6","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Institutions","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Culture","q":"Values, beliefs and language are examples of:","a":"Nonmaterial culture","w":["Material culture","Social structure","Social stratification"],"e":"Material culture includes physical objects, such as tools and buildings.","id":"67b504339f","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Culture","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Culture","q":"A group within a society that rejects and opposes the dominant culture's norms is a:","a":"Counterculture","w":["Subculture","Dominant culture","Primary group"],"e":"A subculture differs from the mainstream but doesn't necessarily oppose it.","id":"a66682d594","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Culture","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Culture","q":"Cultural lag happens when:","a":"Nonmaterial culture is slow to adjust to changes in material culture","w":["A society adopts new technology before it has any written laws","Two cultures merge completely and lose their distinct traditions","Members of a culture reject every change to material and social life"],"e":"Ogburn's example was laws and norms trailing behind new technology.","id":"5c1c5c338a","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Culture","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Health and medicine","q":"In Parsons's 'sick role,' an ill person is expected to:","a":"Try to get well and seek help, in exchange for being excused from duties","w":["Keep working as usual so others are not burdened by the illness","Avoid medical care and rely on family to manage the illness at home","Accept full personal blame for the illness and the duties they miss"],"e":"The sick role carries rights (exemption from duties, not being blamed) and obligations (wanting to recover, seeking care).","id":"0b78f5552d","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Health and medicine","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9A","t":"Health and medicine","q":"Medicalization refers to:","a":"Defining a behavior or condition as a medical problem to be treated","w":["Paying for most health care through government programs","Training more physicians to meet a rising demand for care","Expanding the use of advanced technology inside hospitals"],"e":"Examples include how conditions like ADHD or menopause came to be framed and treated medically.","id":"aea14b53bc","L":"9A · Social structure","T":"Health and medicine","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Demographics","q":"In the demographic transition model, a country in Stage 2 typically has:","a":"High birth rates and falling death rates, so the population grows quickly","w":["Low birth rates and low death rates, so the population is stable","High birth rates and high death rates, so the population grows slowly","Falling birth rates and rising death rates, so the population shrinks"],"e":"Better sanitation and medicine lower death rates before birth rates fall, which causes rapid growth.","id":"7f8a61e832","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Demographics","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Demographics","q":"A population pyramid with a wide base that narrows quickly at the top suggests:","a":"High fertility and a young population","w":["Low fertility and an aging population","Zero population growth","Large immigration of older adults"],"e":"Many young people and relatively few older people points to high birth rates, typical of developing countries.","id":"22b233c7d2","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Demographics","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Demographics","q":"Push factors in migration are:","a":"Conditions that drive people away from where they live","w":["Features that attract people to a new place","Laws that limit how many people can immigrate","Patterns of moving between areas within one city"],"e":"Push factors include war, poverty or persecution. Pull factors include jobs, safety or family in the new place.","id":"87f40ce590","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Demographics","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Demographics","q":"The total fertility rate needed for a population to replace itself without migration is about:","a":"2.1 children per woman","w":["1.0 children per woman","3.5 children per woman","1.5 children per woman"],"e":"Slightly more than 2 accounts for children who don't survive to reproduce.","id":"f2b2114fbc","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Demographics","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Demographics","q":"Which is an example of an ascribed status?","a":"Race or ethnicity","w":["Occupation","Educational degree","Marital status"],"e":"Ascribed statuses are assigned at birth or without choice. Achieved statuses are earned or chosen.","id":"08231cbd06","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Demographics","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"9B","t":"Social change","q":"The growth of cities as people move from rural areas is called:","a":"Urbanization","w":["Gentrification","Suburbanization","Globalization"],"e":"Gentrification is wealthier people moving into poorer urban neighborhoods, often displacing residents.","id":"07a42036ae","L":"9B · Demographics","T":"Social change","g":"Foundation 9 · Society and culture"},{"c":"10A","t":"Stratification","q":"A doctor's child becoming a doctor would be an example of:","a":"No intergenerational mobility","w":["Upward intergenerational mobility","Downward intragenerational mobility","Horizontal mobility within one career"],"e":"Intergenerational mobility compares a person's position with their parents'. Intragenerational mobility is change within one lifetime.","id":"7fdf1761c0","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Stratification","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Stratification","q":"In a meritocracy, social position is mainly determined by:","a":"Individual talent and effort","w":["Family background","Inherited wealth","Caste membership"],"e":"Meritocracy is an ideal. Critics argue unequal access to resources makes real societies far from meritocratic.","id":"a6e7ad3f43","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Stratification","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Stratification","q":"Bourdieu's term for the education, tastes and knowledge that signal social class is:","a":"Cultural capital","w":["Social capital","Economic capital","Human capital"],"e":"Social capital refers to useful networks of relationships. Economic capital is money and property.","id":"7ae91f3e4e","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Stratification","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Stratification","q":"A family whose income is below what is needed for basic survival is in:","a":"Absolute poverty","w":["Relative poverty","Social exclusion","Downward mobility"],"e":"Relative poverty means having much less than others in your society, even if basic needs are met.","id":"20a897a16f","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Stratification","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","q":"The social gradient in health refers to the finding that:","a":"Health generally improves step by step with each rise in socioeconomic status","w":["Only people in absolute poverty have worse health than everyone else","Health is unrelated to income once a country reaches a certain wealth","Wealthier people have more chronic illness because of work-related stress"],"e":"The pattern holds across the whole range of income and education, not just at the bottom.","id":"2351f9d194","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","q":"Neighborhoods with little access to affordable, healthy food are often called:","a":"Food deserts","w":["Ethnic enclaves","Gentrified districts","Health professional shortage areas"],"e":"Food deserts are linked to diet-related diseases such as obesity and type 2 diabetes.","id":"a02f4c113e","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","q":"Policies and practices that disadvantage a group even without any individual's intent to discriminate are called:","a":"Institutional discrimination","w":["Individual discrimination","Prejudice","Stereotype threat"],"e":"Examples include school funding tied to local property taxes or lending practices that affect where families can live.","id":"5488a763d8","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"},{"c":"10A","t":"Inequality and health","q":"Residential segregation is most directly linked to unequal health outcomes through differences in:","a":"Access to quality schools, jobs, health care and safe environments","w":["Genetic differences that cluster between different neighborhoods","Personal preferences for unhealthy foods that vary by area","How often residents travel to other neighborhoods for errands"],"e":"Where people live shapes many social determinants of health at once.","id":"375b6f60d8","L":"10A · Social inequality","T":"Inequality and health","g":"Foundation 10 · Inequality"}]};
window.PASSAGES = {"p864f489b":{"title":"Three enzyme inhibitors","passage":"Researchers studied a liver enzyme that converts substrate S into product P. They measured initial reaction rates across a range of substrate concentrations, first with no inhibitor and then with each of three inhibitors (X, Y and Z) at a fixed concentration. They fit each data set to the Michaelis–Menten equation, v = Vmax[S] / (Km + [S]), to find the apparent Km and Vmax (Table 1).\n\nIn a separate test, inhibitor X's effect disappeared when substrate concentration was raised very high. Inhibitor Z bound the enzyme only after the substrate was already bound.","tbl":[["Condition","Apparent Km (mM)","Apparent Vmax (µmol/min)"],["No inhibitor","2.0","100"],["Inhibitor X","6.0","100"],["Inhibitor Y","2.0","50"],["Inhibitor Z","1.0","50"]],"cap":"Table 1. Kinetic constants with and without inhibitors.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p9cfe476d":{"title":"A recessive disorder in several families","passage":"Genetic counselors studied a rare disorder that causes thick mucus in the lungs and pancreas. Across 40 families, affected children were usually born to two unaffected parents. Males and females were affected at similar rates. The disorder showed up more often in families where the parents were cousins.\n\nSequencing showed that most affected children carried two copies of a mutant allele with a 3-nucleotide deletion in the coding region of a gene for a chloride channel. The deletion removes a single amino acid, and the misfolded channel protein is mostly destroyed before reaching the cell membrane.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p87b74dad":{"title":"Thyroid lab results","passage":"The hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which stimulates the anterior pituitary to secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to make thyroxine (T₄) and triiodothyronine (T₃), which raise basal metabolic rate. T₃ and T₄ feed back to inhibit both TRH and TSH secretion.\n\nA clinic measured blood TSH and free T₄ in three patients (Table 1).","tbl":[["Patient","TSH","Free T₄"],["1","High","Low"],["2","Low","High"],["3","Low","Low"]],"cap":"Table 1. Lab results compared with normal ranges.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p644e5969":{"title":"Blue-white screening","passage":"A student inserts a DNA fragment into a plasmid vector. The plasmid carries an ampicillin-resistance gene (ampR) and a copy of lacZ, which encodes β-galactosidase. The cloning site sits inside lacZ, so a successful insert disrupts the gene. β-galactosidase cleaves the colorless compound X-gal into a blue product.\n\nThe student mixes the ligation product with E. coli cells that have been made competent and then heat-shocks them. The cells are spread on agar containing ampicillin and X-gal. A control plate gets cells that were heat-shocked without any plasmid.\n\nAfter overnight growth, the experimental plate has 150 blue colonies and 50 white colonies. The control plate has no colonies.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p6fa82069":{"title":"Blocking the renin–angiotensin system","passage":"When blood pressure or blood flow to the kidney falls, juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen (made by the liver) into angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), found mostly on lung capillaries, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles, stimulates thirst and ADH release, and triggers the adrenal cortex to secrete aldosterone. Angiotensin II also inhibits renin release.\n\nACE also breaks down bradykinin, a peptide that dilates blood vessels and can irritate the airways.\n\nIn a clinical trial, patients with high blood pressure took an ACE inhibitor daily for 8 weeks.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p43d02800":{"title":"Measuring mitochondrial respiration","passage":"Researchers isolated mitochondria from rat liver and measured oxygen consumption with an oxygen electrode. Additions were made one after another to the same chamber. Succinate was supplied as the fuel. Oligomycin blocks the proton channel of ATP synthase. 2,4-Dinitrophenol (DNP) carries protons across the inner membrane.","tbl":[["Additions to the chamber","O₂ consumption (nmol O₂/min/mg protein)"],["Succinate","20"],["Succinate + ADP","80"],["Succinate + ADP + oligomycin","22"],["Succinate + ADP + oligomycin + DNP","120"]],"cap":"Table 1. Oxygen consumption after each sequential addition.","subject":"biobio","n":4},"p79ac3213":{"title":"Cleaning old paintings","passage":"For much of the twentieth century, conservators treated the yellowed varnish on an old painting as an intruder. Varnish darkens with age. It turns blues toward green and buries the crisp edges a painter worked hard to make. The remedy seemed obvious: remove it and let the original colors speak. Museums that took on ambitious cleanings often unveiled canvases so bright that visitors gasped, and the gasps were taken as proof that something had been recovered.\n\nYet the idea of an 'original' painting is less stable than it looks. Many painters knew their work would be varnished and mixed their colors with that final, warming layer in mind. Some applied glazes, thin translucent layers of tinted oil or resin, that are chemically hard to tell apart from later varnish. A solvent strong enough to lift centuries of grime may take a glaze with it, and a removed glaze cannot be put back. What the delighted visitor sees, then, may not be the painting as its maker last saw it, but a version no one in the painter's lifetime ever saw.\n\nThe defenders of thorough cleaning have a reply, and it is not a foolish one. Leaving discolored varnish in place is also a choice, they argue, and it too puts a modern taste, a fondness for the patina of age, in place of the painter's intention. The brown 'old master' look that collectors once prized was, on this view, an accident of chemistry later mistaken for a style.\n\nBoth camps share an assumption I find doubtful: that the conservator's task is to reach a single correct state of the object. A painting is not a message sealed in a bottle, waiting to be read exactly as written. It is a physical thing that has aged, been retouched, trimmed, remounted and looked at by generations whose eyes were trained differently from ours. To pretend that all of this can be peeled away is to treat history as dirt.\n\nWhat follows is not paralysis but humility. The most defensible conservation is reversible: it uses materials that a future conservator, with better instruments and different questions, could remove without harm. Cleaning should proceed in small tests that are documented and made public, so that viewers understand they are looking at an interpretation. The gasp in the gallery is a pleasure, but it is not evidence.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p45692a88":{"title":"What libraries are for","passage":"Ask a city council how its public library is doing and you will likely hear a number: books loaned, perhaps, or visits per year. Circulation figures have the virtue of being easy to count, and in an era of tight budgets, what can be counted tends to be what gets funded. When loans decline, as they have in many systems since the spread of e-books and streaming, the conclusion seems to write itself: the library is a declining institution, a warehouse for an outdated medium.\n\nThis reasoning mistakes the library's most visible function for its most important one. The sociologist Ray Oldenburg coined the term 'third place' for settings outside home and work, such as cafés, barbershops and pubs, where people gather informally and regulars come to know one another. Oldenburg's examples were mostly commercial, and there lies a problem: in a café, you are welcome roughly as long as you are buying. The public library is one of the very few indoor places in a modern city where a person may sit for hours, warm and dry, without spending money or explaining why they are there.\n\nThat fact explains who uses libraries most heavily: students without a quiet room at home, job seekers without internet access, older people who live alone, parents with small children on a cold afternoon. These visitors may never borrow a book. In the ledger of circulation statistics they are invisible; in the life of a neighborhood they are much of the point.\n\nNone of this means the library's traditional mission is obsolete. Reading remains at its center, and a library that abandoned books would lose the quiet seriousness that sets it apart from a shopping mall. But planners should stop treating everything other than lending as a distraction to be tolerated. Seating, restrooms, power outlets and long opening hours are not extras on top of the library's work; for many patrons, they are the work.\n\nThe harder question is whether a society that measures institutions by output can learn to value one whose chief product is unhurried presence. The answer will be decided less by librarians than by the people who write their budgets.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p4d35aca9":{"title":"Faithful or beautiful","passage":"There is an old joke among translators that a translation, like a lover, can be either faithful or beautiful, but not both. The joke is unkind in several directions at once, and it is also, I think, wrong about translation, though not for the reason usually given.\n\nThe usual rebuttal holds that a skilled translator can achieve both fidelity and beauty with enough effort. This underestimates the difficulty. A poem's sound is not decoration laid over its meaning; it is woven into it. When a line in one language rhymes, the rhyme binds two words together and asks the reader to hear them as related. Carry the meanings into another language and the bond usually snaps, because the matching words no longer chime. The translator may find a new rhyme, but it will join different words and suggest a different relationship. Something has been invented, not preserved.\n\nWhere the joke goes wrong is in its picture of fidelity. It imagines the original poem as a fixed set of meanings, so that a translation is faithful to the extent that it reproduces them one by one. But readers of the original do not receive a list of meanings; they go through an experience in which sense, sound, rhythm and expectation interact. A word-for-word version that keeps every dictionary meaning while turning music into prose is faithful to an inventory, not to the poem.\n\nSeen this way, the translator's choice is not between loyalty and betrayal but among different kinds of loyalty. One may keep the poem's argument and let its music go; one may rebuild the music and let some of the argument shift; one may produce a version so plain that it sends the reader back to the original. Each is honest if it says what it is doing. The dishonest translation is the one that pretends to have lost nothing.\n\nThis is why readers who cannot read the original are often best served by more than one translation. Each shows a different face of the poem, and the poem itself appears, if anywhere, in the space between them.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p7efa8aa5":{"title":"Railway time","passage":"Before the railways, every town kept its own time. Noon was when the sun stood highest over the local church, and a traveler moving east or west could find that the clocks of neighboring towns disagreed by several minutes. Few people minded, because few people traveled fast enough for the difference to matter.\n\nRailways changed that. A timetable that listed departures in a dozen local times invited confusion and, worse, collisions on single-track lines. Railway companies began to run on a single standard time, and towns along the lines gradually set their public clocks to match. What began as an operating convenience became, within a few decades, the way ordinary life was scheduled. Standard time zones, adopted widely in the late nineteenth century, made official what the railways had already made routine.\n\nIt is tempting to tell this as a story of simple progress: confusion replaced by coordination. But historians of labor have pointed to another side. The factory, like the railway, depended on synchronized human activity, and the factory clock became the instrument by which workers' time was measured, bought and disciplined. Fines for lateness, bells that marked the start and end of shifts, and later the punch clock all taught a new habit: experiencing the day as a series of units that belonged to someone else. Older rhythms of work, organized around tasks (the harvest brought in, the cloth woven), gave way to work organized around hours.\n\nThe point is not that clock time was imposed by villains. Coordinated time made possible things almost everyone came to value, from reliable trains to scheduled medical appointments. The point is that a technical standard is never only technical. It reorganizes who waits for whom, whose time counts as money, and what it means to be 'on time.'\n\nToday, as phones quietly update themselves across time zones and work increasingly spills beyond fixed hours, the settlement reached in the industrial era is shifting again. Whether the result frees time or claims more of it is not something the technology will decide by itself.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p555c93fe":{"title":"Keeping ruins as ruins","passage":"A ruin is, strictly speaking, a failure. A roofless abbey no longer shelters monks; a broken aqueduct carries no water. Yet societies spend considerable sums to keep such failures exactly as they are, stabilizing a wall so that it will neither fall nor be rebuilt. The arrangement would puzzle anyone who thought buildings existed only to be used.\n\nIn the nineteenth century, this puzzle divided architects. The French restorer Eugène Viollet-le-Duc held that restoration could return a building to a state of completeness, even one that, as he openly admitted, might never have existed at any single moment. His English critic John Ruskin saw such work as a kind of lie: a restored building, he thought, destroyed the very marks of age and labor that made it worth keeping, replacing a true old thing with a false new one.\n\nModern heritage practice has largely sided with Ruskin, though for reasons he might not recognize. Where Ruskin spoke of reverence, conservators now speak of evidence. A stone worn by centuries of feet is a record of use; a repair in mismatched brick is a record of a later community's priorities. Rebuild the wall and you erase the archive.\n\nStill, the ruin's appeal is not only documentary. Standing in a broken nave, open to the sky, visitors often describe a feeling that is hard to name, something between melancholy and relief. The ruin shows that even great works pass, and in doing so it relieves the present of having to be permanent. A fully restored cathedral says 'this endures'; a ruin says 'this, too, will end,' and some people find that the more comforting message.\n\nIf so, the decision to preserve a ruin as a ruin is not a compromise between using a building and abandoning it. It is a choice to keep a particular kind of meaning available, one that a complete building, however beautiful, cannot offer.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p28efd29e":{"title":"The obligations of a gift","passage":"In an influential essay published in the 1920s, the French anthropologist Marcel Mauss argued that gifts are never truly free. Across the societies he surveyed, a gift created three obligations: to give, to receive and to give back. Refusing a gift could be an insult; failing to return one could cost a person standing. Mauss's point was not that people are secretly calculating, but that exchanging gifts weaves social bonds that buying and selling do not.\n\nThe contrast with market exchange is useful. When I buy bread, the transaction is complete when I pay; the baker and I owe each other nothing more, and we can walk away as strangers. That is exactly its advantage: markets let people cooperate without knowing or trusting one another. A gift, by contrast, is designed to be incomplete. It leaves a debt that cannot be settled with exact change, and the relationship lives in that gap.\n\nThis is why people are often uncomfortable when a gift is returned too quickly or too precisely. Returning a dinner invitation the next evening, or handing back cash equal to the price of a present, turns the gift into a trade and quietly ends the relationship it was meant to build. Etiquette's insistence on delay and on rough rather than exact equivalence is not mere fussiness; it protects the open account that keeps the bond alive.\n\nSome critics conclude that gift economies are therefore hidden systems of obligation and power, in which generosity is a way of putting others in your debt. There is truth in this; competitive giving can humiliate as well as bind. But the criticism proves less than it seems to. Every relationship involves obligations, and the alternative to being bound to others is not freedom but isolation. The question is not whether gifts create ties but which ties a given society wants to encourage.\n\nOnline platforms that let neighbors lend tools or share extra food have learned this the hard way. Those that attach exact prices to every exchange tend to attract efficient strangers; those that leave exchanges loosely reciprocal tend to build communities, and sometimes resentments.","subject":"cars","n":4},"p90212bcf":{"title":"Blood flow through a narrowed artery","passage":"Researchers modeled blood flow through a coronary artery partly blocked by plaque. In the model, blood (density 1060 kg/m³) is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow through a horizontal vessel. Upstream of the plaque, the artery's inner radius is 2.0 mm and the mean flow speed is 0.20 m/s.\n\nThe researchers varied how far the plaque narrowed the vessel and recorded the mean flow speed at the narrowest point (Table 1).\n\nIn a second set of runs, they held the pressure drop across a short vessel segment constant and varied the segment's radius. Volume flow rate fell steeply as radius decreased, consistent with Poiseuille's law, Q = πr⁴ΔP / (8ηL). The authors note that in a living patient, small arterioles downstream of a blockage can dilate, which partly offsets the added resistance at rest.","tbl":[["Radius at plaque (mm)","Mean speed at plaque (m/s)"],["2.0","0.20"],["1.4","0.41"],["1.0","0.80"],["0.5","3.20"]],"cap":"Table 1. Flow speed at the narrowest point for four degrees of narrowing.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"p4ce3a823":{"title":"The bicarbonate buffer in blood","passage":"Blood pH is held near 7.4 mostly by the carbonic acid–bicarbonate buffer:\n\nCO₂(aq) + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻\n\nBecause H₂CO₃ is present only in tiny amounts and is in fast equilibrium with dissolved CO₂, physiologists use an apparent pKa of 6.1 that treats dissolved CO₂ as the acid. Dissolved CO₂ (in mM) is about 0.03 × P_CO₂ (in mmHg). In normal arterial blood, P_CO₂ is 40 mmHg and [HCO₃⁻] is 24 mM, so:\n\npH = 6.1 + log(24 / 1.2) = 6.1 + log 20 ≈ 7.4\n\nThe enzyme carbonic anhydrase, found in red blood cells, speeds up the conversion between CO₂ and H₂CO₃. The lungs adjust P_CO₂ within minutes by changing the rate of breathing, while the kidneys adjust [HCO₃⁻] over hours to days.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"p8b8d34fc":{"title":"Finding the focal length of a lens","passage":"A student uses an optical bench to study a thin glass lens. The object is a lit arrow 2.0 cm tall. For each object distance, the student moves a screen until a sharp image appears and records the image distance. When no image can be formed on the screen, she looks through the lens and uses parallax to find where a virtual image sits. In Table 1, a negative image distance means the image is on the same side of the lens as the object.","tbl":[["Object distance (cm)","Image distance (cm)"],["40","13.3"],["30","15.0"],["20","20.0"],["15","30.0"],["5","−10.0"]],"cap":"Table 1. Object and image distances for the lens.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"paf9f5059":{"title":"Separating a three-part mixture","passage":"A student is given a mixture of three compounds dissolved in diethyl ether (density 0.71 g/mL): benzoic acid (pKa 4.2), aniline (pKa of its conjugate acid, the anilinium ion, is 4.6) and naphthalene (neutral, nonpolar).\n\nStep 1: The ether solution is shaken with 1 M HCl in a separatory funnel. The aqueous layer is drained and saved as Layer A.\nStep 2: The remaining ether solution is shaken with 1 M NaOH. The aqueous layer is drained and saved as Layer B.\nStep 3: The ether that remains (Layer C) is dried with anhydrous MgSO₄, filtered and evaporated.\n\nEach compound is then recovered from its own layer. Carbonic acid, formed when bicarbonate is protonated, has a pKa of about 6.4.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"p7e77c390":{"title":"Radiotracers in medical imaging","passage":"Two isotopes dominate nuclear imaging. Technetium-99m, used in many routine scans, has a half-life of 6.0 hours. It drops from a long-lived excited nuclear state to a lower-energy state by emitting a gamma ray of about 140 keV, which a gamma camera outside the body detects.\n\nFluorine-18 (Z = 9), used in positron emission tomography (PET), has a half-life of about 110 minutes. It decays by positron emission. Each positron travels at most a few millimeters in tissue before meeting an electron. The two particles annihilate, producing two photons of 511 keV each that travel in nearly opposite directions. A ring of detectors records photon pairs that arrive at the same time, and a computer reconstructs where the decays happened.\n\nBecause fluorine-18 decays relatively quickly, it must be produced in a cyclotron near the imaging center.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"p53c1c514":{"title":"Measuring a rate law","passage":"A student studies the reaction A + B → C at 25 °C using the method of initial rates. In each trial, she mixes fresh solutions of A and B and measures how quickly C forms during the first few seconds, before the concentrations change noticeably.\n\nIn a follow-up experiment, she repeats every trial at 35 °C and finds that every initial rate roughly doubles.","tbl":[["Trial","[A] (M)","[B] (M)","Initial rate (M/s)"],["1","0.10","0.10","2.0 × 10⁻³"],["2","0.20","0.10","8.0 × 10⁻³"],["3","0.10","0.20","4.0 × 10⁻³"],["4","0.20","0.20","?"]],"cap":"Table 1. Initial rates at 25 °C.","subject":"chemphys","n":4},"p39207941":{"title":"Sleep and memory","passage":"Researchers recruited 60 college students and taught all of them a list of 40 word pairs at 9 p.m. Participants were then randomly assigned to one of two groups. The sleep group went home and slept normally. The wake group stayed in the lab overnight under supervision and was not allowed to sleep. Both groups were tested on the word pairs at 9 a.m. the next morning (Test 1).\n\nTo check whether tiredness alone explained the results, both groups were allowed a full night of recovery sleep and tested again 48 hours after learning (Test 2). Neither group studied the words between tests.","tbl":[["Group","Test 1 recall (%)","Test 2 recall (%)"],["Sleep","82","78"],["Wake","61","60"]],"cap":"Table 1. Mean percentage of word pairs correctly recalled.","subject":"psychsoc","n":4},"pa96657b0":{"title":"Who helps in an emergency?","passage":"Participants sat alone in booths and talked with other 'participants' over an intercom. In reality, the other voices were recordings. Partway through, one voice appeared to have a seizure and called for help. The researchers varied how many other people the participant believed were listening: none, one or four. They recorded whether the participant left the booth to get help before the recording ended, and how long it took.\n\nAfterward, every participant was told about the deception and why it was needed, and was given the chance to withdraw their data.","tbl":[["Others believed present","Helped before recording ended (%)","Mean time to help (s)"],["0","85","52"],["1","62","93"],["4","31","166"]],"cap":"Table 1. Helping by group size.","subject":"psychsoc","n":4},"p2a9b9953":{"title":"Neighborhood income and diabetes","passage":"A public health team surveyed 12,000 adults across a large city. Neighborhoods were sorted into five groups (quintiles) by median household income, from Q1 (lowest) to Q5 (highest). The team recorded the percentage of adults with diagnosed type 2 diabetes and the distance from each home to the nearest full-service grocery store.\n\nThe team noted that lower-income neighborhoods also had fewer parks and more fast-food outlets per resident. A city council member proposed opening a subsidized grocery store in each Q1 neighborhood.","tbl":[["Income quintile","Type 2 diabetes (%)","Mean distance to grocery store (km)"],["Q1 (lowest)","15.2","3.1"],["Q2","12.8","2.4"],["Q3","10.1","1.6"],["Q4","8.7","1.2"],["Q5 (highest)","6.4","0.8"]],"cap":"Table 1. Diabetes prevalence and grocery access by income quintile.","subject":"psychsoc","n":4},"pa74ecc7e":{"title":"Cues and relapse","passage":"Rats were trained to press a lever that delivered a dose of cocaine. Each dose was paired with a light and a tone. After rats pressed reliably, researchers began an extinction phase: lever presses no longer delivered cocaine, the light or the tone. Pressing gradually dropped to near zero over two weeks.\n\nIn the test phase, some rats were exposed to the light and tone alone, without cocaine, and their lever pressing jumped back up for a short time. Another group received the test after a three-week break with no training and also pressed more than they had at the end of extinction.\n\nCocaine blocks dopamine reuptake in brain reward pathways, including the nucleus accumbens.","subject":"psychsoc","n":4},"p8b2f5777":{"title":"Test instructions and performance","passage":"Women majoring in science were randomly assigned to take the same difficult 20-question math test under one of two instructions. In the 'diagnostic' condition, they were told the test had shown gender differences in the past. In the 'neutral' condition, they were told the test had never shown gender differences. A comparison group of men received the diagnostic instructions.\n\nAfter the test, participants rated how anxious they felt during it.","tbl":[["Group","Mean score (out of 20)","Mean anxiety (1–7)"],["Women, diagnostic","9.1","5.2"],["Women, neutral","13.6","3.4"],["Men, diagnostic","13.3","3.3"]],"cap":"Table 1. Test scores and self-rated anxiety.","subject":"psychsoc","n":4}};
